// Arts and Culture articles, part 3: ukiyo-e, poetry, the orchestra, Norse mythology and puppetry.
ADDART({id:"ukiyoe",t:"Ukiyo-e: Japanese Woodblock Prints",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"🗻",w:"Ukiyo-e",r:["japan","impress","vangogh","haikuart"],
p:[["Pictures of the floating world","Ukiyo-e means pictures of the floating world, a style of Japanese art popular from the 1600s to the 1800s."],["Printed from wood","Artists designed images that were carved into wooden blocks and printed in many colors."],["The Great Wave","Hokusai’s Under the Wave off Kanagawa is one of the most famous artworks in the world."]],
l:"Ukiyo-e is a genre of Japanese art that flourished during the Edo period, from the 17th to the 19th centuries. Its name means pictures of the floating world, referring to the pleasures of city life in Edo, now Tokyo: theater, entertainment and fashion. Most ukiyo-e were woodblock prints, made by teams of designers, carvers and printers and sold cheaply to ordinary townspeople. Artists such as Hokusai and Hiroshige created famous landscapes, and ukiyo-e later inspired European painters such as Monet and Van Gogh.",
s:[["The floating world","During the Edo period, Japan was peaceful and cities grew. Merchants and townspeople enjoyed kabuki theater, sumo, teahouses and festivals. Floating world described this lively, fleeting world of pleasure. Early ukiyo-e showed actors, beautiful women, wrestlers and scenes from popular stories."],["How prints were made","A publisher organized the work. An artist drew the design in ink on thin paper. A carver pasted it on a block of cherry wood and carved away everything except the lines. Separate blocks were carved for each color. Printers brushed ink onto the blocks and pressed paper onto them by hand, one color at a time, lining them up with precise marks."],["Hokusai","Katsushika Hokusai, who worked for about 70 years and called himself an old man mad about painting, created the series Thirty-six Views of Mount Fuji around 1830 to 1832. It includes Under the Wave off Kanagawa, often called The Great Wave, showing a huge wave curling over boats with Mount Fuji in the distance. He also made thousands of sketches, published as Hokusai Manga."],["Hiroshige","Utagawa Hiroshige was famous for landscapes showing travelers, weather and seasons. His series The Fifty-three Stations of the Tōkaidō, from the 1830s, shows stops along the great road between Edo and Kyoto, with rain, snow, mist and moonlight. His work was hugely popular in Japan and later abroad."]],
k:[["Meaning","Pictures of the floating world"],["Period","17th–19th centuries (Edo period)"],["Main technique","Color woodblock printing"],["Famous artists","Hokusai, Hiroshige, Utamaro, Sharaku"],["Most famous work","Under the Wave off Kanagawa (c. 1831)"]],
d:[["1603","The Edo period begins"],["1670s","Hishikawa Moronobu helps establish ukiyo-e prints"],["1765","Full-color brocade prints are developed"],["c. 1831","Hokusai’s Great Wave is published"],["1833–1834","Hiroshige’s Tōkaidō series appears"],["1860s–1880s","Ukiyo-e inspire Japonisme in Europe"]],
s2:[["The Great Wave around the world","The Great Wave has become one of the most recognized images in the world, reproduced on posters, clothes, stamps and even emoji. Thousands of copies were printed in Hokusai’s time; surviving original prints are held by museums such as the British Museum, the Metropolitan Museum of Art and the Tokyo National Museum."],["Inspiring Europe","After Japan opened to foreign trade in the 1850s, ukiyo-e prints reached Europe, sometimes used as wrapping paper for ceramics. Artists such as Claude Monet, Edgar Degas, Mary Cassatt and Vincent van Gogh collected them and borrowed their bold outlines, flat colors and unusual viewpoints. The craze for Japanese art was called Japonisme."],["Van Gogh’s copies","Vincent van Gogh owned hundreds of Japanese prints and painted copies of two Hiroshige prints, including Plum Park in Kameido. He wrote that all his work was in some way based on Japanese art, and his bright colors and strong outlines show its influence."],["Kabuki and beauties","Many ukiyo-e showed kabuki actors in dramatic poses, like posters of celebrities today. Kitagawa Utamaro was famous for portraits of beautiful women, and the mysterious Tōshūsai Sharaku produced striking actor portraits for less than a year in 1794 to 1795 before vanishing from the record."],["Decline and revival","In the late 1800s, photography and modern printing reduced demand for woodblock prints. In the 1900s, new movements revived the art: shin-hanga, new prints, continued traditional methods for collectors, while sōsaku-hanga, creative prints, had artists carve and print their own designs."],["Ukiyo-e and manga","Some see ukiyo-e as an ancestor of modern manga and anime, with their bold lines, expressive faces and popular subjects. The word manga itself was used for Hokusai’s sketchbooks, though it meant something like random sketches."]],
sp:["The Great Wave","Under the Wave off Kanagawa shows three boats of rowers bending beneath a towering wave whose foam curls like claws, while tiny Mount Fuji sits calmly in the distance. Hokusai made it when he was about 70. He used Prussian blue, a new imported pigment, which gave the print its striking color. It shows the power of nature and the fragility of human life."],
g:[["Ukiyo-e","Japanese art of the floating world, often woodblock prints"],["Woodblock print","A print made from a carved block of wood"],["Edo period","Japan’s era from 1603 to 1868, ruled by Tokugawa shoguns"],["Japonisme","European fascination with Japanese art in the 1800s"]],
f:["Ukiyo-e means pictures of the floating world.","Hokusai made The Great Wave when he was about 70.","Van Gogh painted copies of prints by Hiroshige."],
fun:"Some ukiyo-e prints first reached Europe as wrapping paper for porcelain."});

ADDART({id:"poetry",t:"Poetry",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"✒️",w:"Poetry",r:["haikuart","homer","shakes","balagtas","phlit"],
p:[["The art of words","Poetry uses the sound, rhythm and meaning of words to express feelings, ideas and stories."],["Older than writing","Poems were spoken and sung long before writing existed, passed down by memory."],["Many forms","Poems can rhyme or not, be long epics or short haiku, and follow strict patterns or none at all."]],
l:"Poetry is a form of literature that uses the beauty and rhythm of language, its sounds, images and patterns, to create meaning and emotion. Every culture has poetry, from ancient epics and religious hymns to songs, nursery rhymes and modern free verse. Poems may tell stories, express love or grief, celebrate nature, protest injustice or play with language. Poetry is often meant to be heard aloud, and many of the oldest poems were passed down orally for generations before being written.",
s:[["Sound and rhythm","Poets choose words for their sound as well as their meaning. Rhythm, or meter, is the pattern of stressed and unstressed syllables. Rhyme repeats sounds at the ends of lines. Alliteration repeats beginning sounds, as in Peter Piper picked a peck. These patterns make poems musical and memorable."],["Images and figures of speech","Poems paint pictures in the mind with imagery. Similes compare things using like or as, as in my love is like a red, red rose. Metaphors say one thing is another. Personification gives human qualities to objects or ideas. These techniques help readers see the world in new ways."],["Forms","Some poems follow strict forms. A sonnet has 14 lines with a set rhyme scheme. A haiku, from Japan, traditionally has three phrases of 5, 7 and 5 syllables. A ghazal, from Arabic and Persian poetry, has linked couplets. Free verse, common since the late 1800s, has no fixed rhyme or meter."],["Epic poetry","Epic poems are long stories of heroes and great deeds. The Epic of Gilgamesh from Mesopotamia is one of the oldest known works of literature. Homer’s Iliad and Odyssey, India’s Mahabharata and Ramayana, the Persian Shahnameh and West Africa’s Epic of Sundiata are among the world’s great epics."]],
k:[["Oldest major poem","Epic of Gilgamesh, Mesopotamia"],["Common forms","Sonnet, haiku, ghazal, ballad, free verse"],["Key techniques","Rhythm, rhyme, imagery, metaphor"],["World Poetry Day","21 March"],["Longest epic","Mahabharata, about 100,000 couplets"]],
d:[["c. 2100 BCE","Earliest versions of the Gilgamesh poems"],["c. 8th century BCE","Homer’s epics take shape"],["c. 1000 CE","Ferdowsi completes the Shahnameh"],["1300s","Dante completes the Divine Comedy"],["1838","Francisco Balagtas publishes Florante at Laura"],["1999","UNESCO declares 21 March World Poetry Day"]],
s2:[["Poets around the world","Great poets have written in every language: Li Bai and Du Fu in China; Rumi and Hafez in Persia; Kālidāsa and Kabir in India; Dante in Italy; Shakespeare in England; Pablo Neruda in Chile; Wisława Szymborska in Poland; and Rabindranath Tagore in Bengal. Many poems are read in translation worldwide."],["Poetry and song","Poetry and music have always been close. Ancient poems were often sung to music, and song lyrics are a kind of poetry. In 2016 the songwriter Bob Dylan won the Nobel Prize in Literature, sparking debate about whether songs count as literature."],["Philippine poetry","The Philippines has a rich poetic tradition, from oral forms such as the bugtong, riddles, and the epic Hinilawod to written works. Francisco Balagtas’s Florante at Laura, published in 1838, is a classic of Tagalog literature, and the poetic debate called balagtasan is named after him."],["Poetry in protest","Poems have often been used to protest injustice and inspire change. During the American civil rights movement, Langston Hughes and Maya Angelou wrote powerful poems about freedom and dignity. Poets in many countries have been jailed for their words, and their poems shared secretly."],["Spoken word and slam","Spoken word poetry is written to be performed aloud, often with strong rhythm and emotion. Poetry slams are competitions in which poets perform and audiences score them. Slams began in Chicago in the 1980s and spread worldwide, attracting many young poets."],["Why read poetry?","Poetry helps people express feelings that are hard to put into words, at moments such as weddings, funerals and celebrations. Reading and memorizing poems builds vocabulary and an ear for language, and writing poetry encourages creativity and careful observation."]],
sp:["The oldest poem","The Epic of Gilgamesh tells of Gilgamesh, a king of Uruk in Mesopotamia, who searches for immortality after the death of his friend Enkidu. Early versions were written on clay tablets in cuneiform around 4,000 years ago. The best-known version was found in the library of the Assyrian king Ashurbanipal at Nineveh. It includes a flood story similar to the later story of Noah."],
g:[["Meter","The rhythmic pattern of a line of poetry"],["Rhyme","Repetition of similar sounds at the ends of words"],["Metaphor","Describing something as if it were something else"],["Epic","A long poem about heroes and great deeds"]],
f:["The Epic of Gilgamesh is one of the oldest known poems.","A sonnet has 14 lines.","World Poetry Day is celebrated on 21 March."],
fun:"The Mahabharata, an Indian epic, is about ten times longer than the Iliad and the Odyssey combined."});

ADDART({id:"orchestra",t:"The Orchestra",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"🎻",w:"Orchestra",r:["mozart","musicw","opera","guitar"],
p:[["Many players together","An orchestra is a large group of musicians playing instruments together, led by a conductor."],["Four families","Orchestral instruments are grouped into strings, woodwinds, brass and percussion."],["Symphonies and more","Orchestras perform symphonies, concertos, opera, ballet and film music."]],
l:"An orchestra is a large ensemble of musicians playing string, woodwind, brass and percussion instruments, usually directed by a conductor. A full symphony orchestra may have 70 to 100 or more players. The modern orchestra developed in Europe in the 1600s and 1700s and grew larger in the 1800s, performing works by composers such as Haydn, Mozart, Beethoven, Tchaikovsky and Mahler. Today orchestras play classical music, opera, ballet and film and video game scores, and youth and community orchestras flourish worldwide.",
s:[["Strings","The string section, the largest, includes violins, violas, cellos and double basses. Players draw a bow across the strings or pluck them. Violins play the highest notes and often carry the melody; double basses provide the deep foundation. The harp is also part of the orchestra."],["Woodwinds","Woodwind instruments make sound when players blow across a hole or through a reed. They include flutes, piccolos, oboes, clarinets and bassoons. Though flutes are now usually made of metal, they still count as woodwinds."],["Brass and percussion","Brass instruments, such as trumpets, horns, trombones and tubas, are played by buzzing the lips into a cup-shaped mouthpiece. Percussion includes timpani, or kettledrums, cymbals, bass drums, triangles, xylophones and many others, adding rhythm, color and excitement."],["The conductor","The conductor stands in front of the orchestra, keeping the beat with a baton or hands, cueing entries and shaping the music’s speed, loudness and feeling. Before a concert, the conductor rehearses with the orchestra to decide how a piece will be played."]],
k:[["Size","Usually 70–100 or more players"],["Sections","Strings, woodwinds, brass, percussion"],["Largest section","Strings"],["Leader of the violins","Concertmaster"],["Tuning note","A, usually played by the oboe"]],
d:[["1607","Monteverdi’s opera L’Orfeo uses an early orchestra"],["1700s","Haydn and Mozart develop the classical symphony"],["1824","Beethoven’s Ninth Symphony premieres"],["1842","Vienna Philharmonic and New York Philharmonic are founded"],["1975","El Sistema youth orchestra program begins in Venezuela"],["1999","West–Eastern Divan Orchestra is founded"]],
s2:[["Seating","Orchestras are usually arranged in a semicircle facing the conductor, with strings at the front, woodwinds behind them, brass further back and percussion at the very back. This helps balance the sound, since brass and percussion are loud while strings are softer."],["Tuning up","Before a concert, the oboe plays the note A, and the other instruments tune to it. The oboe is used because its clear, steady sound is easy to hear. The concertmaster, the leading violinist, then leads the tuning of the strings."],["Great symphonies","A symphony is a large work for orchestra, usually in four movements. Famous examples include Mozart’s Symphony No. 40, Beethoven’s Fifth, with its famous opening of four notes, Beethoven’s Ninth, with its choral Ode to Joy, Dvořák’s New World Symphony and Tchaikovsky’s Pathétique."],["Orchestras around the world","Famous orchestras include the Vienna and Berlin Philharmonics, the London Symphony Orchestra, the New York Philharmonic and the Royal Concertgebouw Orchestra. Orchestras such as the NHK Symphony in Japan, the Shanghai Symphony and the Philippine Philharmonic Orchestra show how orchestral music has spread worldwide."],["El Sistema","In 1975 the Venezuelan musician José Antonio Abreu founded El Sistema, a program that gives free music education and orchestra places to children, many from poor neighborhoods. It has reached hundreds of thousands of young people and inspired similar programs in more than 60 countries. Its most famous graduate is the conductor Gustavo Dudamel."],["Orchestras and film","Many film and video game soundtracks are recorded by orchestras. Composers such as John Williams, who wrote the music for Star Wars, Jaws and Harry Potter films, Hans Zimmer and Joe Hisaishi, who scored many Studio Ghibli films, have brought orchestral music to huge audiences."]],
sp:["Beethoven’s Ninth","Beethoven’s Ninth Symphony, first performed in Vienna in 1824, was the first major symphony to include singers, who perform the poem Ode to Joy in its final movement. By then Beethoven was almost completely deaf; at the premiere, he had to be turned around to see the audience applauding. The Ode to Joy melody is now the anthem of the European Union."],
g:[["Conductor","The person who directs an orchestra"],["Symphony","A large musical work for orchestra"],["Concertmaster","The leading violinist of an orchestra"],["Ensemble","A group of musicians performing together"]],
f:["The oboe plays the note that the orchestra tunes to.","Strings are the largest section of the orchestra.","Beethoven was almost deaf when his Ninth Symphony premiered."],
fun:"The melody of Beethoven’s Ode to Joy is the anthem of the European Union."});

ADDART({id:"norsemyth",t:"Norse Mythology",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"⚡",w:"Norse mythology",r:["viking","gmyth","fairy","iceland"],
p:[["Gods of the North","Norse mythology is the collection of myths of the ancient Scandinavian peoples, including the Vikings."],["Odin, Thor and Loki","Its gods include Odin, the wise chief god, Thor, god of thunder, and Loki, the trickster."],["The end of the world","The myths tell of Ragnarök, a great battle in which the world is destroyed and reborn."]],
l:"Norse mythology is the body of myths and legends of the Norse, the peoples of Scandinavia in the Viking Age and earlier. It tells of gods such as Odin, Thor, Freyja and Loki, giants, elves and dwarves, and nine worlds connected by a giant ash tree, Yggdrasil. Much of what we know comes from medieval Icelandic texts, especially the Poetic Edda and the Prose Edda, written down after Scandinavia had become Christian. Norse myths have deeply influenced literature, films, comics and the names of the days of the week in English.",
s:[["The gods","The main gods, the Æsir, lived in Asgard. Odin, the all-father, gave up an eye to drink from a well of wisdom and learned the runes by hanging for nine nights on Yggdrasil. Thor, his son, protected gods and humans with his hammer Mjölnir. Frigg was Odin’s wife, and Freyja, of another group of gods called the Vanir, was goddess of love, beauty and war."],["Nine worlds","The Norse imagined nine worlds linked by Yggdrasil, the world tree. They included Asgard, home of the gods; Midgard, the world of humans, linked to Asgard by a rainbow bridge called Bifröst; Jötunheim, land of the giants; and Hel, the realm of the dead. Valhalla, Odin’s hall in Asgard, welcomed warriors who died bravely."],["Loki the trickster","Loki, a shape-shifter of giant birth, lived among the gods. He sometimes helped them with clever tricks and sometimes caused great harm. He arranged the death of Baldr, Odin’s beloved son, and was punished by being bound beneath a dripping serpent. At Ragnarök, he fights against the gods."],["Ragnarök","The myths foretell Ragnarök, the doom of the gods. After three long winters, monsters break free: the wolf Fenrir swallows Odin, and Thor kills the great serpent Jörmungandr but dies from its poison. Fire engulfs the world, which sinks into the sea. Then a new, green world rises, and a few gods and two humans survive to begin again."]],
k:[["Main sources","Poetic Edda, Prose Edda (13th century, Iceland)"],["Chief god","Odin"],["Thunder god","Thor, with the hammer Mjölnir"],["World tree","Yggdrasil"],["End of the world","Ragnarök"]],
d:[["c. 800–1050","The Viking Age"],["c. 1000","Iceland adopts Christianity"],["c. 1220","Snorri Sturluson writes the Prose Edda"],["c. 1270","The Codex Regius, with the Poetic Edda, is written"],["1876","Wagner’s Ring cycle, based partly on Norse myths, premieres"],["1962","Marvel Comics introduces its version of Thor"]],
s2:[["The Eddas","The Poetic Edda is a collection of anonymous poems about gods and heroes, preserved mainly in a manuscript called the Codex Regius from about 1270. The Prose Edda was written around 1220 by the Icelandic scholar and politician Snorri Sturluson as a guide to the myths for poets. Without them, much of Norse mythology would have been lost."],["Days of the week","Several English days of the week are named after Norse or related Germanic gods. Tuesday is named after Tyr, god of war and law; Wednesday after Woden, the English form of Odin; Thursday after Thor; and Friday probably after Frigg, Odin’s wife."],["Runes","The Norse wrote with runes, an alphabet of angular letters suited for carving on wood, stone and bone. In myth, Odin won the knowledge of runes through his sacrifice on Yggdrasil. Rune stones raised in memory of the dead still stand across Scandinavia."],["Heroes and sagas","Besides the gods, Norse legends tell of heroes. The Völsunga saga tells of Sigurd, who slays the dragon Fáfnir and wins a cursed treasure of gold. These stories inspired Richard Wagner’s operas and J. R. R. Tolkien, a professor of Old Norse and Old English, whose dwarves in The Hobbit take their names from the Poetic Edda."],["Norse myths today","Norse gods appear in many books, films, comics and games. Marvel’s Thor and Loki have become famous worldwide through films, though they differ greatly from the original myths. Writers such as Neil Gaiman have retold the myths for modern readers."],["Myths and belief","For the Norse, these stories were part of a living religion with rituals, sacrifices and festivals. After Christianity spread through Scandinavia between about 1000 and 1100, the old beliefs faded, though they survived in stories, place names and folklore. Today some people in Iceland and elsewhere practice a revived form of the old religion."]],
sp:["Odin’s ravens and Thor’s goats","Odin had two ravens, Huginn and Muninn, meaning thought and memory, who flew over the world each day and returned to tell him the news. Thor rode in a chariot pulled by two goats, Tanngrisnir and Tanngnjóstr. According to one myth, Thor could eat the goats for dinner and bring them back to life the next morning with his hammer, as long as their bones were kept whole."],
g:[["Æsir","The main family of Norse gods"],["Yggdrasil","The giant tree connecting the nine worlds"],["Ragnarök","The prophesied end and rebirth of the world"],["Rune","A letter of the ancient Germanic alphabet"]],
f:["Thursday is named after Thor.","Most Norse myths survive in texts from medieval Iceland.","Tolkien took the names of his dwarves from the Poetic Edda."],
fun:"Thursday is named after Thor, the Norse god of thunder."});

ADDART({id:"puppetry",t:"Puppetry",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"🎎",w:"Puppetry",r:["theatre","anim","indonesiacountry","tv"],
p:[["Bringing objects to life","Puppetry is the art of bringing figures to life for performance, using hands, strings, rods or shadows."],["An ancient art","Puppet theater has been performed for thousands of years in Asia, Europe, Africa and the Americas."],["Many kinds","Puppets include hand puppets, marionettes, rod puppets, shadow puppets and giant parade puppets."]],
l:"Puppetry is a form of theater in which performers, called puppeteers, manipulate figures called puppets to tell stories. Puppets may be moved by hand, strings, rods or other mechanisms, or appear as shadows cast on a screen. Puppetry is one of the oldest forms of theater and is found in cultures around the world, from Indonesia’s wayang shadow plays and Japan’s bunraku to Europe’s Punch and Judy and the Muppets on television. It entertains children and adults and often carries religious, moral or political messages.",
s:[["Hand and glove puppets","Glove or hand puppets fit over the puppeteer’s hand, with fingers moving the head and arms. They are simple to make and are often used in street shows and classrooms. Punch and Judy, a comic glove-puppet show from Britain descended from the Italian character Pulcinella, has been performed since the 1600s."],["Marionettes","Marionettes are puppets moved from above by strings or wires attached to a control bar. Skilled puppeteers can make them walk, dance and gesture realistically. Marionette traditions are strong in Czechia, Italy, Myanmar and many other places. Salzburg’s Marionette Theater in Austria performs whole operas."],["Rod and shadow puppets","Rod puppets are moved by sticks attached to the head, hands or body. Shadow puppets are flat figures, often made of leather, held between a light and a screen so the audience sees their shadows. Shadow puppetry has ancient traditions in China, India, Indonesia, Malaysia, Türkiye and Greece."],["Puppets on screen","In the 20th century, puppets became stars of television and film. Jim Henson’s Muppets, including Kermit the Frog, appeared on Sesame Street from 1969 and The Muppet Show from 1976. Puppets are also used in films, stop-motion animation and theater productions such as The Lion King and War Horse."]],
k:[["Main types","Hand, marionette, rod, shadow, giant puppets"],["Famous traditions","Wayang kulit, bunraku, Punch and Judy, water puppetry"],["UNESCO heritage","Wayang (2003), bunraku (2008) and others"],["Famous puppeteer","Jim Henson"],["Sesame Street began","1969"]],
d:[["c. 400 BCE","Greek writers mention string puppets"],["c. 1000s","Water puppetry develops in Vietnam"],["1600s","Punch and Judy shows begin in England"],["1684","Bunraku theater develops in Osaka, Japan"],["1969","Sesame Street premieres"],["2003","UNESCO recognizes Indonesian wayang puppet theater"]],
s2:[["Wayang kulit","In Indonesia, especially Java and Bali, wayang kulit shadow puppet plays are performed with intricately carved leather puppets. A single master puppeteer, the dalang, voices all the characters and moves the puppets through all-night performances, accompanied by a gamelan orchestra. Stories often come from the Indian epics, the Ramayana and the Mahabharata."],["Bunraku","Bunraku is Japan’s traditional puppet theater, developed in Osaka in the 1600s. Large puppets, about half human size, are each operated by three puppeteers in full view of the audience: a master who moves the head and right hand, and two assistants dressed in black. A chanter tells the story, accompanied by a three-stringed shamisen."],["Vietnamese water puppetry","Water puppetry, mũa rối nước, began in the rice paddies of northern Vietnam about a thousand years ago. Puppeteers stand waist-deep in water behind a screen, moving wooden puppets on long poles hidden beneath the surface, so the puppets seem to glide and dance on the water."],["Giant puppets","Some puppets are enormous. Giant puppets are paraded in festivals in Spain, Belgium, France and Brazil. Since 2010, the British company Royal de Luxe and others have staged street shows with puppets several stories tall. Little Amal, a 3.5-meter puppet of a young refugee girl, walked thousands of kilometers across Europe in 2021 to draw attention to child refugees."],["Puppets for learning and healing","Teachers use puppets to help children learn languages and tell stories, and therapists use them to help children talk about feelings and difficult experiences. Puppets can say things that people might find hard to say themselves."],["Puppetry in the Philippines","Puppetry is part of Philippine theater too. Groups such as Teatrong Mulat ng Pilipinas, founded by Amelia Lapeña-Bonifacio in 1977, have used puppets to tell Filipino and Asian stories to children, drawing on traditions from across Asia."]],
sp:["Little Amal","Little Amal is a giant puppet, 3.5 meters tall, of a nine-year-old Syrian refugee girl, created by the Handspring Puppet Company of South Africa, makers of the horse puppets in War Horse. In 2021 she walked about 8,000 kilometers from the border of Syria and Türkiye across Europe to the United Kingdom, greeted by communities along the way, to raise awareness of refugee children. She has since traveled to other countries, including the United States."],
g:[["Puppeteer","A person who operates puppets"],["Marionette","A puppet moved by strings from above"],["Shadow puppet","A flat puppet seen as a shadow on a screen"],["Dalang","The master puppeteer of Indonesian wayang"]],
f:["Indonesian wayang is recognized by UNESCO.","Bunraku puppets are each operated by three puppeteers.","Vietnamese water puppets perform on a pool of water."],
fun:"In Vietnamese water puppetry, puppeteers stand waist-deep in water behind a screen."});

