// Planet Earth articles, part 3: geysers, lakes, storms, grasslands and Kilimanjaro.
ADDART({id:"geysers",t:"Geysers and Hot Springs",c:"Geography",i:"♨️",w:"Geyser",r:["volc","iceland","taal","phpower"],
p:[["Fountains of hot water","A geyser is a hot spring that shoots water and steam into the air from time to time."],["Heated underground","Geysers form where water seeps down and is heated by hot rock deep below the surface."],["Rare wonders","There are only about 1,000 active geysers on Earth, and about half are in Yellowstone National Park."]],
l:"A geyser is a spring that erupts from time to time, throwing hot water and steam into the air. Geysers form only where three things come together: plenty of underground water, a strong source of heat from volcanic rock, and a special system of narrow cracks and chambers that lets pressure build up. Fewer than a thousand geysers exist worldwide, mostly in Yellowstone in the United States, Kamchatka in Russia, El Tatio in Chile, New Zealand and Iceland, which gave the word geyser to the world.",
s:[["How a geyser works","Rainwater and snowmelt trickle down through cracks into hot rock. Deep down, the weight of water above raises the boiling point, so the water becomes superheated, hotter than 100°C without boiling. When some of it finally turns to steam, it pushes water out of the vent, lowering the pressure. The rest flashes into steam all at once, blasting a column of water skyward. Then the chambers slowly refill and the cycle begins again."],["Hot springs, mud pots and fumaroles","Geysers are one kind of hydrothermal feature. Hot springs are pools of heated water that do not erupt. Mud pots are bubbling pools of hot, acidic mud. Fumaroles are vents that release steam and gases. Colorful bacteria and other microbes that love heat live in many hot springs, painting them bright orange, yellow and green."],["Yellowstone","Yellowstone National Park in the United States, the world’s first national park, founded in 1872, sits on top of a huge volcanic hotspot and holds about half of the world’s geysers. Old Faithful erupts roughly every 60 to 110 minutes, reaching heights of 30 to 55 meters. Steamboat Geyser, the tallest active geyser, can shoot water more than 90 meters high, but at unpredictable intervals."],["Iceland’s Geysir","The word geyser comes from Geysir, a famous spring in southwestern Iceland, from an Icelandic word meaning to gush. Geysir itself now erupts only rarely, but its neighbor Strokkur erupts every few minutes, sending water 15 to 20 meters or more into the air, to the delight of visitors."]],
k:[["Active geysers worldwide","About 1,000"],["Share in Yellowstone","About half"],["Tallest active geyser","Steamboat, Yellowstone"],["Name from","Geysir, Iceland"],["Largest ever recorded","Waimangu, New Zealand (1900–1904)"]],
d:[["1294","Geysir in Iceland becomes active after earthquakes"],["1870","Old Faithful is named by an expedition"],["1872","Yellowstone becomes the first national park"],["1900–1904","Waimangu Geyser erupts up to about 460 meters"],["1941","Russia’s Valley of Geysers in Kamchatka is discovered"],["2005","Cassini spacecraft sees geysers on Saturn’s moon Enceladus"]],
s2:[["Geyser fields of the world","Besides Yellowstone, major geyser fields include the Valley of Geysers on Russia’s Kamchatka Peninsula; El Tatio in the Andes of Chile, one of the highest geyser fields at over 4,000 meters; and the Taupō Volcanic Zone of New Zealand, where Pōhutu Geyser in Rotorua erupts many times a day."],["The biggest geyser ever","Waimangu Geyser in New Zealand, active from 1900 to 1904, was the most powerful geyser ever recorded. Its eruptions of black, muddy water and rocks reached up to about 460 meters high. It stopped after a landslide changed the local water table."],["Fragile features","Geysers are easily damaged. In New Zealand and Iceland, many geysers stopped erupting after people drilled wells for geothermal power nearby. Visitors throwing coins or rubbish into vents have also clogged geysers, which is why paths in geyser parks keep people at a safe distance."],["Heat from the Earth","The same underground heat that powers geysers can be used to make electricity and warm homes. Iceland gets most of its heating from geothermal energy. The Philippines is one of the world’s top producers of geothermal electricity, with power plants at places such as Tiwi, Makban and Leyte."],["Geysers in space","Geysers are not unique to Earth. Saturn’s moon Enceladus sprays plumes of water ice from cracks near its south pole, hinting at an ocean beneath its icy crust. In 1989 the Voyager 2 spacecraft saw dark plumes rising from Neptune’s moon Triton."],["Life in boiling water","The microbe Thermus aquaticus, discovered in a Yellowstone hot spring in the 1960s, survives in near-boiling water. An enzyme from it became essential to PCR, a technique used to copy DNA, which is used in medicine, forensic science and testing for diseases."]],
sp:["Old Faithful","Old Faithful was named in 1870 by members of the Washburn Expedition, who were impressed by how regularly it erupted. It is not the biggest geyser in Yellowstone, but it is one of the most predictable, so park rangers can forecast each eruption, usually within about ten minutes. Each eruption lasts from about one and a half to five minutes and can throw out tens of thousands of liters of boiling water."],
g:[["Geyser","A hot spring that erupts water and steam"],["Superheated","Heated above its normal boiling point without boiling"],["Fumarole","A vent releasing volcanic steam and gases"],["Geothermal","Heat from inside the Earth"]],
f:["About half of the world’s geysers are in Yellowstone.","The word geyser comes from Geysir in Iceland.","Saturn’s moon Enceladus has geysers of ice."],
fun:"An enzyme from a microbe found in a Yellowstone hot spring helps scientists copy DNA."});

ADDART({id:"lakes",t:"Lakes",c:"Geography",i:"🏞️",w:"Lake",r:["taal","iceage","nile","water","lotus"],
p:[["Bodies of water on land","A lake is a large body of water surrounded by land, filling a hollow in the ground."],["Many origins","Lakes form in basins carved by glaciers, in volcanic craters, in cracks in the crust and behind dams."],["Fresh and salty","Most lakes hold fresh water, but some, such as the Dead Sea, are saltier than the ocean."]],
l:"A lake is a body of standing water that fills a basin on land, larger and deeper than a pond. Lakes hold a large share of the world’s liquid surface fresh water and provide drinking water, food, transport and homes for countless plants and animals. The Caspian Sea is the largest lake by area, Lake Baikal in Russia is the deepest and oldest, and Lake Superior in North America is the largest freshwater lake by area. Lakes are found on every continent, even beneath the ice of Antarctica.",
s:[["How lakes form","Many lakes in Canada, Finland and northern Europe filled hollows carved by Ice Age glaciers. Rift lakes, such as Baikal and Tanganyika, formed where the Earth’s crust is pulling apart. Crater lakes fill the mouths of old volcanoes. Oxbow lakes form when a river cuts off a loop of its course. People also create reservoirs by building dams."],["Fresh and salt lakes","Most lakes have rivers flowing in and out, keeping them fresh. Lakes with no outlet lose water only by evaporation, leaving salts behind, so they become salty. The Dead Sea, on the border of Jordan and Israel, is almost ten times saltier than the ocean, so people float easily in it. Its shore is the lowest place on land, about 430 meters below sea level."],["Life in lakes","Lakes support fish, insects, birds and plants. Lake Baikal has thousands of species, many found nowhere else, including the nerpa, the world’s only freshwater seal. Africa’s Lake Malawi and Lake Victoria have hundreds of species of colorful cichlid fish."],["Lakes and people","People have always settled beside lakes for water, fish and transport. Lake Titicaca, on the border of Peru and Bolivia at about 3,800 meters, is sacred in Andean culture. The Great Lakes of North America connect cities such as Chicago and Toronto by shipping routes to the Atlantic Ocean."]],
k:[["Largest by area","Caspian Sea, about 371,000 km²"],["Deepest","Lake Baikal, 1,642 m"],["Largest freshwater by area","Lake Superior"],["Lowest","Dead Sea, about 430 m below sea level"],["Largest in the Philippines","Laguna de Bay"]],
d:[["c. 25 million years ago","Lake Baikal begins to form"],["c. 12,000 years ago","Melting glaciers create many northern lakes"],["1960s","The Aral Sea begins to shrink"],["1986","A gas release from Lake Nyos, Cameroon, kills about 1,700 people"],["1996","Lake Vostok is confirmed beneath Antarctic ice"],["2005","Cassini begins to find lakes of methane on Titan"]],
s2:[["Lake Baikal","Lake Baikal in Siberia is the world’s deepest lake, at 1,642 meters, and its oldest, at about 25 to 30 million years. It holds around a fifth of the world’s unfrozen surface fresh water, more than all the North American Great Lakes combined. In winter, its surface freezes so thick that roads are built across the ice."],["The Caspian Sea","The Caspian Sea, bordered by Russia, Kazakhstan, Turkmenistan, Iran and Azerbaijan, is the world’s largest inland body of water. It is called a sea because of its size and slightly salty water, but it is surrounded by land. It is famous for sturgeon, whose eggs are sold as caviar, and for oil and gas."],["The shrinking Aral Sea","The Aral Sea in Central Asia was once the fourth-largest lake in the world. From the 1960s, the Soviet Union diverted its feeding rivers to irrigate cotton fields, and the lake shrank to a fraction of its size, leaving ships stranded in the desert. A dam built in 2005 has helped the northern part to partly recover."],["Lakes of the Philippines","Laguna de Bay, beside Metro Manila, is the largest lake in the Philippines, used for fishing and water supply. Lake Lanao in Mindanao is one of the oldest lakes in the world, according to some estimates, and home to unique fish. Taal Lake fills a volcanic caldera and contains Volcano Island, which itself has a crater lake."],["Deadly lakes","In 1986 Lake Nyos in Cameroon suddenly released a huge cloud of carbon dioxide that had built up in its deep water. The heavy gas flowed into valleys and suffocated about 1,700 people and thousands of animals. Pipes now release the gas slowly to prevent another disaster."],["Lakes beyond Earth","Saturn’s largest moon, Titan, is the only other place known to have stable liquid lakes on its surface, but they are filled with liquid methane and ethane, not water. Lakes once existed on Mars too: NASA’s Perseverance rover is exploring Jezero Crater, an ancient lake bed."]],
sp:["Lake Vostok","Lake Vostok lies beneath almost 4 kilometers of Antarctic ice, sealed off from the air for perhaps millions of years. It is one of the largest of hundreds of lakes hidden under the Antarctic ice sheet. Russian scientists drilled down to its surface in 2012. Researchers hope to learn whether microbes survive in this dark, cold world, which may resemble the hidden oceans on some moons."],
g:[["Basin","A hollow in the land that can hold water"],["Rift lake","A lake formed where the crust is pulling apart"],["Reservoir","An artificial lake made by a dam"],["Oxbow lake","A curved lake left by a river"]],
f:["Lake Baikal is the deepest lake in the world.","The Caspian Sea is the largest lake by area.","Titan has lakes of liquid methane."],
fun:"Canada has more lakes than any other country in the world."});

ADDART({id:"storms",t:"Hurricanes, Typhoons and Tornadoes",c:"Geography",i:"🌀",w:"Tropical cyclone",r:["phtyph","climate","climchange","tsunami"],
p:[["Powerful storms","Hurricanes, typhoons and cyclones are giant spinning storms that form over warm oceans."],["One storm, three names","They are called hurricanes in the Atlantic, typhoons in the western Pacific and cyclones in the Indian Ocean."],["Twisting winds","Tornadoes are smaller but violent spinning columns of air reaching down from thunderstorms."]],
l:"Storms are disturbances of the atmosphere bringing strong winds and often heavy rain, thunder and lightning. The most powerful are tropical cyclones: huge spinning storms hundreds of kilometers wide that form over warm tropical seas. Depending on where they occur, they are called hurricanes, typhoons or cyclones. Tornadoes are narrow, violently rotating columns of air that form under thunderstorms. Weather forecasters use satellites, radar and computer models to track storms and warn people in time to stay safe.",
s:[["How tropical cyclones form","Tropical cyclones form over oceans warmer than about 26.5°C. Warm, moist air rises, cools and forms clouds, releasing heat that fuels more rising air. Earth’s rotation makes the storm spin, counterclockwise in the Northern Hemisphere and clockwise in the Southern. At the center is a calm eye, surrounded by the eyewall, where winds are strongest."],["Dangers","Tropical cyclones bring destructive winds, flooding rain and storm surges, walls of seawater pushed onto the coast, which often cause the most deaths. Landslides and disease can follow. The 1970 Bhola cyclone in Bangladesh, one of the deadliest natural disasters, killed hundreds of thousands of people."],["Tornadoes","Tornadoes form when winds at different heights in a thunderstorm blow at different speeds and directions, creating rotation that is tilted upright by rising air. Most last only minutes but can have winds faster than 400 km/h. The United States has more tornadoes than any other country, about 1,200 a year, especially in the region called Tornado Alley."],["Thunderstorms and lightning","Thunderstorms grow in towering cumulonimbus clouds. Lightning is a giant spark of electricity, heating the air to around 30,000°C, five times hotter than the Sun’s surface; the air expands so fast it makes the boom of thunder. Lightning strikes Earth about 40 to 50 times every second."]],
k:[["Ocean temperature needed","About 26.5°C or warmer"],["Strongest category","Category 5: winds 252 km/h or more"],["Tornado scale","Enhanced Fujita, EF0–EF5"],["Most tornadoes","United States, about 1,200 a year"],["Deadliest cyclone","1970 Bhola cyclone, Bangladesh"]],
d:[["1900","Galveston hurricane kills about 8,000 people in Texas"],["1925","Tri-State Tornado in the US kills 695 people"],["1953","Atlantic hurricanes begin to receive official names"],["1970","Bhola cyclone strikes East Pakistan, now Bangladesh"],["2005","Hurricane Katrina floods New Orleans"],["2013","Typhoon Haiyan (Yolanda) devastates the central Philippines"]],
s2:[["Measuring storms","The Saffir–Simpson scale ranks hurricanes from Category 1 to 5 by wind speed; Category 5 storms have winds of at least 252 km/h. Tornadoes are rated on the Enhanced Fujita scale, from EF0 to EF5, based on the damage they cause. Typhoon warnings in the Philippines use a system of signal numbers issued by PAGASA."],["Naming storms","Giving storms names makes warnings clearer. The World Meteorological Organization keeps lists of names for each ocean region; names of very destructive storms are retired. The Philippines’ weather agency, PAGASA, also gives local names to storms entering its area of responsibility, which is why Typhoon Haiyan is known in the Philippines as Yolanda."],["Typhoon Haiyan","Typhoon Haiyan, called Yolanda in the Philippines, struck on 8 November 2013, one of the strongest tropical cyclones ever to make landfall. Its storm surge swept through Tacloban and other towns in the Visayas, killing more than 6,000 people and destroying over a million homes. It led to major improvements in Philippine storm warnings."],["Tornado records","The deadliest tornado recorded struck Bangladesh in 1989, killing about 1,300 people. In 1999, a tornado near Oklahoma City produced winds measured by radar at about 480 km/h, among the fastest ever recorded on Earth. Tornadoes also occur in Europe, Argentina, Australia and many other places."],["Storms and climate change","Scientists find that a warmer climate is making the strongest tropical cyclones more intense and wetter, and that rising seas make storm surges more dangerous. Whether the total number of storms will change is less certain. Research continues as better data is collected."],["Staying safe","Early warnings save many lives. In a cyclone, people should follow evacuation orders and move away from coasts and rivers. In a tornado, the safest place is a basement or a small room without windows on the lowest floor. During lightning, people should go indoors and avoid open fields and tall, lone trees."]],
sp:["Hurricane hunters","Some aircraft fly straight into hurricanes on purpose. Hurricane hunter crews from the US Air Force and NOAA fly through the violent eyewall into the calm eye, dropping instruments called dropsondes that measure wind, pressure and temperature as they fall. Their data helps forecasters predict a storm’s path and strength more accurately."],
g:[["Tropical cyclone","A large spinning storm formed over warm tropical seas"],["Eye","The calm center of a tropical cyclone"],["Storm surge","Seawater pushed onto land by a storm"],["Tornado","A violently rotating column of air from a thunderstorm"]],
f:["Hurricanes and typhoons are the same kind of storm.","The eye of a cyclone is calm.","Lightning is hotter than the Sun’s surface."],
fun:"Cyclones spin counterclockwise north of the equator and clockwise south of it."});

ADDART({id:"grasslands",t:"Grasslands and Savannas",c:"Geography",i:"🌾",w:"Grassland",r:["lion","elephant","desert","trees","rice","antelopes","grasshoppers"],
p:[["Seas of grass","Grasslands are wide open lands where grasses are the main plants and trees are few."],["Many names","They are called prairies, steppes, pampas, veld and savannas in different parts of the world."],["Great herds","Grasslands support huge herds of grazing animals, from wildebeest to bison."]],
l:"Grasslands are regions where grasses, rather than trees or shrubs, are the main plants. They cover a large share of Earth’s land, between deserts and forests, where there is too little rain for thick forests but too much for desert. Tropical grasslands with scattered trees are called savannas; temperate grasslands include the prairies of North America, the steppes of Eurasia, the pampas of South America and the veld of southern Africa. Grasslands support great herds of animals and grow much of the world’s food.",
s:[["Why grass, not trees?","Grasslands have dry seasons, frequent fires and grazing animals, all of which keep trees from taking over. Grasses survive because they grow from the base, not the tip, so they regrow quickly after being eaten or burned. Their deep, tangled roots hold soil together and store food underground."],["Savannas","Savannas are warm grasslands with scattered trees, found in Africa, South America, India and Australia. They have a wet season and a long dry season. Africa’s savannas, such as the Serengeti, are home to elephants, giraffes, zebras, lions, cheetahs and the great migrations of wildebeest."],["Temperate grasslands","Temperate grasslands have hot summers and cold winters. The Eurasian Steppe stretches thousands of kilometers from Hungary to Mongolia and China and was home to nomadic herders such as the Mongols. The North American prairies and South American pampas are now largely farmland."],["Breadbaskets of the world","Grassland soils are often deep and fertile because of centuries of decaying grass roots. Many have been plowed to grow wheat, maize and other crops, which are themselves grasses, and to graze cattle and sheep. Rice, wheat, maize, barley and sugarcane all belong to the grass family."]],
k:[["Main types","Savanna and temperate grassland"],["Regional names","Prairie, steppe, pampas, veld"],["Grass species","About 12,000"],["Famous migration","Serengeti wildebeest, about 1.5 million"],["Largest grassland","Eurasian Steppe"]],
d:[["c. 8 million years ago","Grasslands spread widely as climates dry"],["c. 4000 BCE","Horses are domesticated on the steppe"],["1206","Genghis Khan unites the steppe peoples"],["1889","Fewer than about 1,000 American bison remain"],["1930s","The Dust Bowl strikes the North American plains"],["1981","Serengeti National Park becomes a World Heritage Site"]],
s2:[["The great migration","Each year, about 1.5 million wildebeest, along with hundreds of thousands of zebras and gazelles, travel in a great loop through the Serengeti in Tanzania and the Maasai Mara in Kenya, following the rains and fresh grass. They face crocodiles at river crossings and lions, hyenas and cheetahs along the way. It is one of the largest animal migrations on Earth."],["The American bison","Tens of millions of bison once roamed the North American prairies and were central to the life of many Indigenous peoples. In the 1800s, they were slaughtered on a vast scale, leaving fewer than about 1,000 by 1889. Conservation efforts have brought them back, and hundreds of thousands now live in parks and on ranches."],["The Dust Bowl","In the 1930s, after farmers plowed up the prairie grasses of the southern Great Plains, a long drought turned the bare soil to dust. Huge dust storms swept across the land, burying farms and forcing many families to leave. The disaster led to new farming methods to protect the soil."],["Fire on the grassland","Fire is a natural part of many grasslands. Lightning starts fires that burn dry grass but leave roots unharmed, so new green shoots soon appear, attracting grazing animals. Many Indigenous peoples used careful burning to manage grasslands, a practice that is being revived today."],["Grasslands in the Philippines","Grasslands cover a significant part of the Philippines, though many formed after forests were cleared and are dominated by tough cogon grass. Natural grasslands are also found in places such as the mountain grasslands of Mount Pulag and the Bukidnon plateau in Mindanao, used for cattle ranching."],["Threats and conservation","Grasslands are among the least protected habitats in the world. Much of the original prairie and pampas has been turned into farms and cities. Overgrazing, invasive plants and climate change also threaten them. Protecting grasslands helps store carbon in the soil, conserves wildlife and prevents erosion."]],
sp:["Grass that fights back","Grasses have developed clever defenses against grazing animals. Many contain tiny bits of silica, the material in sand and glass, which wear down the teeth of animals that eat them. In turn, horses, cows and other grazers evolved tall, tough teeth that keep growing or wear slowly. This long contest between grasses and grazers has shaped both over millions of years."],
g:[["Savanna","A tropical grassland with scattered trees"],["Prairie","A temperate grassland of North America"],["Steppe","A dry, temperate grassland of Eurasia"],["Grazer","An animal that feeds mainly on grass"]],
f:["Rice, wheat and maize are all grasses.","Grasses grow from the base, so they regrow after grazing.","About 1.5 million wildebeest migrate through the Serengeti."],
fun:"Bamboo is a grass, and some kinds can grow almost a meter in a single day."});

ADDART({id:"kilimanjaro",t:"Mount Kilimanjaro",c:"Geography",i:"🏔️",w:"Mount Kilimanjaro",r:["everest","volc","kenyacountry","iceage","climchange"],
p:[["Roof of Africa","Mount Kilimanjaro in Tanzania is the highest mountain in Africa, at 5,895 meters."],["A lone giant","It is the tallest freestanding mountain in the world, rising alone from the plains."],["Snow near the equator","Its summit has glaciers, even though it lies only about 330 kilometers from the equator."]],
l:"Mount Kilimanjaro, in northeastern Tanzania near the border with Kenya, is the highest mountain in Africa and the tallest freestanding mountain on Earth. It is a dormant volcano with three cones: Kibo, Mawenzi and Shira. Its highest point, Uhuru Peak on Kibo, reaches 5,895 meters. Though close to the equator, Kilimanjaro is crowned with glaciers, which are rapidly shrinking. It does not require technical climbing skills, so tens of thousands of people try to climb it each year.",
s:[["A volcanic mountain","Kilimanjaro formed over the past million years or more from volcanic eruptions linked to the East African Rift. Shira is the oldest cone and has collapsed; Mawenzi is jagged and eroded; Kibo, the youngest and highest, is dormant, with a crater at the top. Fumaroles still release volcanic gases in Kibo’s crater."],["Climbing through climates","A climb up Kilimanjaro passes through several zones: farmland and rainforest on the lower slopes, then moorland with giant groundsels and lobelias, then alpine desert, and finally the arctic summit with ice and snow. Climbers say it is like walking from the equator to the poles in a few days."],["People of the mountain","The Chagga people have farmed the fertile southern slopes for centuries, growing bananas and coffee and building irrigation channels. Many Tanzanians work as guides and porters, carrying food and equipment for climbers. Their skill and hard work make most climbs possible."],["First ascent","The first recorded ascent of Kibo’s summit was in 1889 by the German geographer Hans Meyer and the Austrian climber Ludwig Purtscheller, helped by local guides including Yohani Kinyala Lauwo, who lived to over 100. Today, routes such as Marangu, Machame and Lemosho lead climbers to the top in five to nine days."]],
k:[["Height","5,895 m (Uhuru Peak)"],["Country","Tanzania"],["Type","Dormant stratovolcano"],["Cones","Kibo, Mawenzi, Shira"],["First recorded ascent","1889"]],
d:[["1848","Missionary Johannes Rebmann reports snow on Kilimanjaro"],["1889","Hans Meyer and Ludwig Purtscheller reach the summit"],["1936","Hemingway publishes The Snows of Kilimanjaro"],["1961","The peak is renamed Uhuru, meaning freedom, at Tanganyika’s independence"],["1973","Kilimanjaro National Park is created"],["1987","The park becomes a UNESCO World Heritage Site"]],
s2:[["Disappearing ice","Kilimanjaro’s glaciers have shrunk by more than 80 percent since the early 1900s. Scientists think this is caused by a mix of drier air, less snowfall and warming temperatures, as the ice turns directly to vapor in the strong sunlight. Some studies predict that the ice may disappear entirely within the next few decades."],["Altitude sickness","At the summit, the air contains only about half as much oxygen per breath as at sea level. Climbers who go up too fast can suffer from altitude sickness, causing headaches, nausea and, in serious cases, dangerous swelling of the lungs or brain. Guides advise climbing slowly, pole pole in Swahili, and drinking plenty of water."],["Unusual plants","The middle slopes have strange plants found nowhere else, such as giant groundsels that can grow several meters tall, and lobelias that look like giant green bottles. These plants have adapted to cold nights and strong daytime sun by storing water and closing their leaves at night."],["Uhuru Peak","When Tanganyika, now part of Tanzania, became independent in 1961, a torch was placed on the summit and the highest point was named Uhuru Peak, meaning freedom in Swahili. The Uhuru Torch is still a national symbol."],["The Seven Summits","Kilimanjaro is one of the Seven Summits, the highest mountains on each of the seven continents, along with Everest, Aconcagua, Denali, Elbrus, Vinson and Puncak Jaya or Kosciuszko. Many mountaineers try to climb all seven, and Kilimanjaro is often the first."],["Water for the region","Rain and snow on Kilimanjaro’s forests feed streams and springs that supply water to farms, towns and wildlife on the plains around it. Protecting the mountain’s forests is important for millions of people who depend on this water."]],
sp:["A mountain alone","Unlike Everest, which is part of the Himalaya range, Kilimanjaro stands by itself, rising almost 5,000 meters above the surrounding plains. This makes it the tallest freestanding mountain on Earth. On clear days, it can be seen from Amboseli National Park in Kenya, where photographers capture elephants walking across the plains with the snowy peak behind them."],
g:[["Dormant volcano","A volcano not erupting now but which could erupt again"],["Stratovolcano","A tall, cone-shaped volcano built of layers of lava and ash"],["Glacier","A slow-moving mass of ice"],["Altitude sickness","Illness caused by low oxygen at high altitude"]],
f:["Kilimanjaro is the highest mountain in Africa.","Its summit is called Uhuru Peak, meaning freedom.","Kilimanjaro’s glaciers have shrunk by more than 80 percent."],
fun:"Climbing Kilimanjaro is like traveling from the equator to the Arctic in a few days."});

