// Space articles, part 3: moons, space agencies, cosmic distances, meteors and space junk.
ADDART({id:"moons",t:"Moons of the Solar System",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🌗",w:"Natural satellite",r:["moon","jupiter","saturn","uranep","pluto"],
p:[["Natural satellites","A moon is a natural object that orbits a planet, dwarf planet or asteroid."],["Hundreds of moons","Hundreds of moons are known in the Solar System, and new ones are still being found."],["Worlds of their own","Some moons have volcanoes, hidden oceans or thick atmospheres."]],
l:"A moon, or natural satellite, is a body that orbits a planet or another smaller object. Earth has one Moon, but the Solar System contains hundreds. Mercury and Venus have none, Mars has two tiny ones, and the giant planets have dozens each: Saturn alone had 274 known moons by 2025. Moons range from small captured rocks a few kilometers across to Ganymede, which is bigger than the planet Mercury. Several moons may hide oceans of liquid water beneath icy crusts, making them places where scientists search for life.",
s:[["How moons form","Moons form in different ways. Earth’s Moon probably formed from debris thrown out when a Mars-sized body hit the young Earth. The large moons of Jupiter and Saturn formed from disks of gas and dust around their planets, like mini solar systems. Many small, distant moons are captured asteroids or comet-like objects."],["Jupiter’s Galilean moons","In 1610 Galileo Galilei discovered Jupiter’s four largest moons: Io, Europa, Ganymede and Callisto. Io is the most volcanically active body in the Solar System. Europa has a smooth icy crust over a salty ocean. Ganymede is the largest moon of all and has its own magnetic field. Callisto is one of the most heavily cratered objects known."],["Saturn’s moons","Titan, Saturn’s largest moon, is the only moon with a thick atmosphere, and it has rivers and lakes of liquid methane. In 2005 the Huygens probe landed on its surface. Enceladus, a small, bright moon, sprays jets of water ice from an ocean beneath its south pole. Some of Saturn’s tiny moons orbit within its rings, shaping them."],["Moons of the outer planets","Uranus’s moons are named after characters from Shakespeare and Alexander Pope, such as Miranda, Titania and Oberon. Neptune’s largest moon, Triton, orbits backward, which suggests it was captured from the Kuiper Belt. Pluto’s largest moon, Charon, is about half Pluto’s width, so the two orbit a point in space between them."]],
k:[["Largest moon","Ganymede, about 5,270 km wide"],["Most volcanic","Io"],["Thick atmosphere","Titan"],["Most moons (2025)","Saturn, 274 known"],["Planets with no moons","Mercury and Venus"]],
d:[["1610","Galileo discovers Jupiter’s four large moons"],["1655","Christiaan Huygens discovers Titan"],["1877","Asaph Hall discovers Mars’s moons, Phobos and Deimos"],["1979","Voyager 1 sees active volcanoes on Io"],["2005","The Huygens probe lands on Titan"],["2025","128 new moons of Saturn are announced"]],
s2:[["Ocean worlds","Europa, Enceladus, Ganymede, Titan and possibly Callisto and Triton are thought to have oceans of liquid water beneath their icy surfaces, kept warm by tides from their planets. Since life on Earth needs liquid water, these hidden oceans are among the most exciting places to look for life beyond Earth."],["Missions to the moons","The European Space Agency’s JUICE spacecraft, launched in 2023, will study Ganymede, Europa and Callisto, and NASA’s Europa Clipper, launched in 2024, will make dozens of close flybys of Europa in the 2030s. NASA’s Dragonfly, a nuclear-powered drone, is planned to fly through Titan’s thick air."],["Mars’s little moons","Mars’s moons, Phobos and Deimos, are small, lumpy and dark, perhaps captured asteroids or debris from an ancient impact. Phobos orbits so close to Mars that it is slowly spiraling inward; in tens of millions of years it will probably break apart, forming a ring, or crash into Mars."],["Tidal locking","Many moons, including Earth’s Moon, always show the same face to their planet. This is called tidal locking: over millions of years, the planet’s gravity slowed the moon’s spin until one rotation took exactly as long as one orbit."],["Moons of asteroids","Even asteroids can have moons. Images taken by the Galileo spacecraft in 1993 revealed Dactyl, a tiny moon orbiting the asteroid Ida. In 2022 NASA’s DART mission deliberately crashed into Dimorphos, a small moon of the asteroid Didymos, to test whether an impact could change an asteroid’s path. It worked, shortening Dimorphos’s orbit by about 32 minutes."],["Counting moons","Moon counts keep changing as telescopes find more small, faint moons, often just a few kilometers across. In 2023 Saturn passed Jupiter as the planet with the most known moons, and in 2025 astronomers announced 128 more, bringing Saturn’s total to 274. Many of these are thought to be fragments of larger moons broken apart by collisions."]],
sp:["Io’s volcanoes","Io, the innermost of Jupiter’s large moons, has more than 400 active volcanoes. It is squeezed and stretched by Jupiter’s gravity as it orbits, and this tidal heating melts rock inside it. Some volcanoes shoot plumes of sulfur hundreds of kilometers into space, and its surface is painted yellow, orange and red by sulfur compounds. Voyager 1 discovered these volcanoes in 1979, the first active volcanoes seen beyond Earth."],
g:[["Natural satellite","A natural body orbiting a planet or other object"],["Tidal locking","When a moon always shows the same face to its planet"],["Ocean world","A world with a large body of liquid water, often under ice"],["Captured moon","A moon that formed elsewhere and was pulled into orbit"]],
f:["Ganymede is larger than the planet Mercury.","Titan has lakes of liquid methane.","Mercury and Venus have no moons."],
fun:"Saturn has more than 270 known moons, more than any other planet."});

ADDART({id:"spaceagencies",t:"Space Agencies of the World",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🛰️",w:"List of government space agencies",r:["space","apollo","iss","sat","rocket"],
p:[["Exploring together","Space agencies are organizations that plan and carry out space missions, mostly run by governments."],["Many nations in space","More than 70 countries now have space agencies or space programs."],["Public and private","Today private companies also build rockets and spacecraft alongside government agencies."]],
l:"A space agency is an organization, usually run by a government, that develops rockets, satellites and space missions. The first great space programs belonged to the Soviet Union and the United States during the Space Race. Today agencies in Europe, China, India, Japan and many other countries launch satellites, explore the Moon and planets and send astronauts into orbit. Countries increasingly cooperate on projects such as the International Space Station, while private companies have become major players in spaceflight.",
s:[["NASA","The National Aeronautics and Space Administration of the United States was created in 1958, soon after the Soviet Union launched Sputnik. NASA landed the first astronauts on the Moon in 1969, built the Space Shuttle, sent rovers to Mars and probes to every planet, and launched the Hubble and James Webb space telescopes."],["Soviet and Russian space programs","The Soviet Union launched the first satellite, Sputnik 1, in 1957 and the first human in space, Yuri Gagarin, in 1961. It also flew the first woman in space and the first spacewalk and built the Mir space station. Since 1992, Russia’s space program has been run by an agency now called Roscosmos, whose Soyuz rockets have carried astronauts for decades."],["Europe, Japan and China","The European Space Agency, formed in 1975 by countries working together, builds the Ariane rockets and leads missions such as Rosetta and JUICE. Japan’s JAXA, formed in 2003, brought back samples from asteroids with its Hayabusa probes. China’s space program put its first astronaut in orbit in 2003, landed on the far side of the Moon in 2019 and built the Tiangong space station."],["India","The Indian Space Research Organisation, ISRO, was founded in 1969. Its Mars Orbiter Mission, Mangalyaan, reached Mars on its first attempt in 2014, at a very low cost. In 2023 its Chandrayaan-3 mission made India the first country to land near the Moon’s south pole."]],
k:[["First satellite","Sputnik 1, Soviet Union, 1957"],["NASA founded","1958"],["ESA founded","1975"],["ISRO founded","1969"],["Philippine Space Agency","PhilSA, founded 2019"]],
d:[["1957","The Soviet Union launches Sputnik 1"],["1958","NASA is founded"],["1969","ISRO is founded; Apollo 11 lands on the Moon"],["1975","The European Space Agency is formed"],["1998","Assembly of the International Space Station begins"],["2019","The Philippine Space Agency is created"]],
s2:[["The Philippine Space Agency","The Philippine Space Agency, PhilSA, was created by law in 2019 to lead the country’s space program. Before that, Filipino engineers built Diwata-1, the country’s first microsatellite, launched in 2016 from the International Space Station, and Maya-1, its first cubesat, in 2018. These satellites help monitor weather, farmland and disasters."],["New space nations","Many countries have joined the space age recently. The United Arab Emirates sent its Hope probe into orbit around Mars in 2021. South Korea launched its own rocket into orbit in 2022. Countries including Nigeria, Vietnam, Indonesia, Brazil and others use satellites for communications, weather and mapping."],["Working together","Space is expensive, so agencies often cooperate. The International Space Station is a partnership of the United States, Russia, Europe, Japan and Canada. Instruments from many countries fly on each other’s spacecraft, and astronauts from more than 20 countries have visited the ISS."],["Private spaceflight","Since the 2000s, private companies such as SpaceX, Blue Origin and Rocket Lab have built their own rockets. In 2020 SpaceX’s Crew Dragon became the first privately built spacecraft to carry astronauts to the ISS. Reusable rockets, which land and fly again, have greatly reduced the cost of reaching orbit."],["Rules for space","The Outer Space Treaty of 1967, signed by over 100 countries, says that space is free for all nations to explore, that no country can claim the Moon or planets, and that nuclear weapons may not be placed in orbit. The United Nations Office for Outer Space Affairs helps countries cooperate."],["Benefits on Earth","Space programs have given us satellite weather forecasts, GPS navigation, global communications and images that track forests, crops and disasters. Technology developed for space has also led to improvements in medicine, water filters, materials and cameras."]],
sp:["Mangalyaan","India’s Mars Orbiter Mission, nicknamed Mangalyaan, arrived at Mars on 24 September 2014. India became the first Asian country to reach Mars orbit and the first nation to do so on its first attempt. The mission cost about 74 million US dollars, less than the budget of some Hollywood space films. It was planned to last six months but kept working for about eight years."],
g:[["Space agency","An organization that runs a space program"],["Satellite","An object that orbits a planet"],["Cubesat","A small satellite built from standard cube units"],["Reusable rocket","A rocket that can land and be flown again"]],
f:["Sputnik 1 was the first artificial satellite.","India reached Mars on its first attempt.","The Philippine Space Agency was created in 2019."],
fun:"India’s Mars mission cost less than some Hollywood films about space."});

ADDART({id:"distances",t:"Measuring Distances in Space",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"📏",w:"Cosmic distance ladder",r:["light","stars","galaxies","hubble","sun","variablestars"],
p:[["Huge distances","Space is so vast that kilometers become too small to measure it easily."],["Special units","Astronomers use astronomical units, light-years and parsecs."],["A ladder of methods","Different methods are used for nearby and faraway objects, like the rungs of a ladder."]],
l:"Distances in space are enormous. The Moon is about 384,400 kilometers away, the Sun about 150 million kilometers and the nearest star more than 40 trillion kilometers. To make such numbers manageable, astronomers use special units: the astronomical unit, the light-year and the parsec. They measure distances using a chain of methods called the cosmic distance ladder, from radar and parallax for nearby objects to special stars and exploding stars for distant galaxies.",
s:[["The astronomical unit","The astronomical unit, AU, is the average distance from Earth to the Sun, defined as exactly 149,597,870.7 kilometers. It is useful within the Solar System: Jupiter is about 5.2 AU from the Sun and Neptune about 30 AU. Light from the Sun takes about 8 minutes and 20 seconds to reach Earth."],["The light-year","A light-year is the distance light travels in one year, about 9.46 trillion kilometers. Light moves at almost 300,000 kilometers per second. The nearest star beyond the Sun, Proxima Centauri, is about 4.2 light-years away, so we see it as it was over four years ago. The Milky Way is about 100,000 light-years across."],["Parallax","Hold up a finger and look at it with one eye, then the other; it seems to jump against the background. Astronomers measure the tiny shift in a nearby star’s position as Earth moves around the Sun. The size of the shift reveals the distance. In 1838 Friedrich Bessel used parallax to measure the distance to the star 61 Cygni, the first time a star’s distance was measured."],["Standard candles","For distant objects, astronomers use standard candles, objects whose true brightness is known. By comparing how bright they look with how bright they really are, astronomers can work out their distance. Cepheid variable stars and a type of exploding star called a Type Ia supernova are important standard candles."]],
k:[["Astronomical unit","149,597,870.7 km"],["Light-year","About 9.46 trillion km"],["Parsec","About 3.26 light-years"],["Nearest star","Proxima Centauri, about 4.2 light-years"],["Andromeda Galaxy","About 2.5 million light-years"]],
d:[["c. 240 BCE","Eratosthenes estimates the size of the Earth"],["1838","Bessel measures the first stellar parallax"],["1912","Henrietta Leavitt discovers how Cepheids reveal distances"],["1924","Edwin Hubble shows Andromeda is a separate galaxy"],["1969","Apollo 11 leaves a laser reflector on the Moon"],["1998","Supernovae reveal that the universe’s expansion is speeding up"]],
s2:[["Henrietta Leavitt","Henrietta Swan Leavitt worked at Harvard College Observatory studying photographic plates. In 1912 she discovered that the brighter a Cepheid variable star is, the more slowly it pulses. This meant astronomers could use a Cepheid’s pulsing to work out its true brightness and so its distance. Her discovery allowed Edwin Hubble to measure the distance to other galaxies."],["Bouncing lasers off the Moon","Apollo astronauts left special mirrors called retroreflectors on the Moon. Observatories on Earth fire laser beams at them and time how long the light takes to bounce back, measuring the Moon’s distance to within a few millimeters. These measurements show that the Moon is moving away from Earth by about 3.8 centimeters a year."],["The parsec","Astronomers often use the parsec, short for parallax of one arcsecond. One parsec is the distance at which a star would show a parallax shift of one arcsecond, a tiny angle, about 3.26 light-years. Larger distances are given in kiloparsecs, thousands of parsecs, and megaparsecs, millions."],["Mapping the Milky Way","The European Space Agency’s Gaia spacecraft, launched in 2013, measured the positions and distances of almost two billion stars with extraordinary accuracy, creating the most detailed three-dimensional map of our galaxy ever made. It finished collecting data in 2025."],["Redshift","Light from distant galaxies is stretched toward the red end of the spectrum as the universe expands. The farther away a galaxy is, the greater its redshift. Measuring redshift lets astronomers estimate the distances of the most remote galaxies, some seen as they were over 13 billion years ago."],["Looking back in time","Because light takes time to travel, looking far into space means looking back in time. We see the Sun as it was eight minutes ago, the Andromeda Galaxy as it was 2.5 million years ago and the most distant galaxies as they were shortly after the Big Bang. The observable universe is estimated to be about 93 billion light-years across."]],
sp:["Voyager 1","NASA’s Voyager 1 spacecraft, launched in 1977, is the most distant human-made object, more than 24 billion kilometers from Earth. Radio signals, traveling at the speed of light, take almost a full day to reach it. Around late 2026, it is expected to reach one light-day from Earth. Even so, it would take Voyager tens of thousands of years to travel as far as the nearest star."],
g:[["Astronomical unit","The average distance from Earth to the Sun"],["Light-year","The distance light travels in one year"],["Parallax","The apparent shift of an object seen from two places"],["Standard candle","An object of known brightness used to measure distance"]],
f:["Sunlight takes about eight minutes to reach Earth.","A light-year is a distance, not a time.","The Moon is moving away from Earth by about 3.8 cm a year."],
fun:"When you look at the Andromeda Galaxy, you see light that left it 2.5 million years ago."});

ADDART({id:"meteors",t:"Meteors and Meteorites",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"☄️",w:"Meteoroid",r:["comet","earthplanet","moon","kuiper"],
p:[["Shooting stars","A meteor, or shooting star, is a streak of light made when a space rock burns up in the atmosphere."],["Rocks that land","A meteorite is a piece of space rock that survives the fall and reaches the ground."],["Showers of light","Several times a year, Earth passes through trails of comet dust, causing meteor showers."]],
l:"Meteoroids are small rocky or metallic bodies traveling through space, from grains of dust to boulders. When a meteoroid enters Earth’s atmosphere at high speed, it heats up and glows, creating a bright streak called a meteor or shooting star. Most burn up completely, but pieces that reach the ground are called meteorites. Dozens of metric tons of space dust and rock fall on Earth every day. Rare large impacts have shaped Earth’s history, including the one linked to the extinction of the dinosaurs.",
s:[["Meteoroid, meteor, meteorite","These three words describe the same object at different stages. In space, it is a meteoroid. As it glows in the atmosphere, it is a meteor. If it lands, it becomes a meteorite. Very bright meteors are called fireballs, and those that explode in the sky are called bolides."],["Why meteors glow","Meteoroids enter the atmosphere at speeds of about 11 to 72 kilometers per second. They squeeze the air in front of them so strongly that it heats up, making the meteoroid and the surrounding air glow. Most visible meteors are made by particles no bigger than a grain of sand, burning up about 80 to 120 kilometers above the ground."],["Meteor showers","Comets leave trails of dust along their orbits. When Earth passes through one, many meteors appear to come from the same point in the sky. The Perseids in August come from Comet Swift–Tuttle; the Geminids in December come from an asteroid called Phaethon; the Leonids in November come from Comet Tempel–Tuttle."],["Kinds of meteorites","Stony meteorites are the most common. Iron meteorites are made mostly of iron and nickel, from the cores of broken asteroids. Stony-iron meteorites contain both. A few meteorites come from the Moon and Mars, blasted off by impacts. Some, called carbonaceous chondrites, contain water and organic chemicals."]],
k:[["Speed","About 11–72 km per second"],["Glowing height","About 80–120 km up"],["Largest meteorite","Hoba, Namibia, about 60 metric tons"],["Best-known showers","Perseids, Geminids, Leonids"],["Dinosaur impact","About 66 million years ago"]],
d:[["c. 66 million years ago","An asteroid strikes Chicxulub, Mexico"],["c. 50,000 years ago","Meteor Crater forms in Arizona"],["1833","A great Leonid meteor storm amazes North America"],["1908","The Tunguska explosion flattens forest in Siberia"],["2013","The Chelyabinsk meteor explodes over Russia"],["2022","NASA’s DART spacecraft changes an asteroid’s orbit"]],
s2:[["The dinosaur killer","About 66 million years ago, an asteroid about 10 kilometers wide struck near what is now the Yucatán Peninsula in Mexico, leaving the Chicxulub crater, about 180 kilometers across. The impact threw dust and gases into the sky, darkening and cooling the planet. About three-quarters of all species, including the non-bird dinosaurs, died out."],["Tunguska","On 30 June 1908, a space rock exploded in the air over the Tunguska region of Siberia. The blast flattened about 80 million trees over an area of more than 2,000 square kilometers. Because the area was remote, few people were hurt. No crater was found, suggesting the object exploded before reaching the ground."],["Chelyabinsk","On 15 February 2013, a meteor about 20 meters wide exploded over Chelyabinsk, Russia, shining brighter than the Sun for a moment. The shock wave shattered windows across the city, injuring about 1,500 people, mostly from flying glass. Many dashboard cameras filmed it, giving scientists valuable data."],["Meteorite hunting","Meteorites are easiest to find in deserts and on the ice of Antarctica, where dark stones stand out. Teams have collected tens of thousands of meteorites from Antarctica. The Hoba meteorite in Namibia, about 60 metric tons of iron, is the largest ever found and has never been moved."],["Messengers from the early Solar System","Most meteorites are about 4.5 billion years old, older than any rock on Earth, and preserve material from the birth of the Solar System. The Murchison meteorite, which fell in Australia in 1969, contains amino acids, building blocks of proteins, showing that some ingredients of life form in space."],["Defending Earth","Astronomers track near-Earth asteroids to check whether any might hit our planet. In 2022 NASA’s DART spacecraft deliberately crashed into the small asteroid moon Dimorphos and changed its orbit, the first test of a way to push a dangerous asteroid aside. Asteroid Day is marked each year on 30 June, the anniversary of Tunguska."]],
sp:["Watching a meteor shower","To see a meteor shower, find a dark place away from city lights, lie back and look at a wide part of the sky. Allow about 20 minutes for your eyes to adjust to the dark. The best showers, such as the Perseids and Geminids, can produce up to about 100 meteors an hour under perfect conditions, and the best time is usually after midnight, when your side of Earth faces forward into the dust stream."],
g:[["Meteoroid","A small body traveling through space"],["Meteor","The streak of light from a meteoroid burning in the atmosphere"],["Meteorite","A meteoroid that reaches the ground"],["Fireball","An exceptionally bright meteor"]],
f:["Most shooting stars are made by grains the size of sand.","The Hoba meteorite has never been moved.","Some meteorites come from Mars."],
fun:"Shooting stars are not stars at all, but tiny bits of space dust burning up."});

ADDART({id:"spacejunk",t:"Space Junk",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🗑️",w:"Space debris",r:["sat","iss","rocket","spaceagencies","satellites"],
p:[["Litter in orbit","Space junk is the old satellites, rocket parts and fragments left in orbit around Earth."],["Dangerous speed","Pieces travel at about 28,000 km/h, so even tiny bits can damage spacecraft."],["A growing problem","As more satellites are launched, keeping space clean has become a major challenge."]],
l:"Space junk, or space debris, is human-made material orbiting Earth that no longer serves a purpose: dead satellites, spent rocket stages, flecks of paint and fragments from explosions and collisions. Tens of thousands of pieces large enough to track circle the planet, along with millions of smaller ones. Because they travel at very high speeds, even a fragment smaller than a marble can damage a satellite or spacecraft. Space agencies and companies are working on rules and technologies to reduce debris and clean up orbit.",
s:[["How much junk is there?","Radar and telescopes track tens of thousands of objects larger than about 10 centimeters. Scientists estimate there are about a million pieces between 1 and 10 centimeters, and well over 100 million smaller than 1 centimeter. Most of the mass comes from a few thousand dead satellites and old rocket bodies."],["Why it is dangerous","In low Earth orbit, objects move at about 7.8 kilometers per second. At that speed, a 1-centimeter fragment hits with the energy of a speeding car. The International Space Station has shields to protect against small debris and has moved to dodge larger pieces dozens of times."],["Collisions and explosions","Much debris comes from breakups. In 2009 an American Iridium communications satellite collided with a dead Russian satellite, Kosmos 2251, the first accidental collision between two intact satellites, creating thousands of pieces. Old rocket stages can also explode when leftover fuel ignites."],["Anti-satellite tests","Some countries have tested missiles by destroying their own satellites. China’s 2007 test created more than 3,000 trackable pieces, and a Russian test in 2021 forced astronauts aboard the ISS to shelter in their spacecraft. Many nations now pledge not to carry out such destructive tests."]],
k:[["Speed in low orbit","About 28,000 km/h"],["Regularly tracked objects","About 47,000 (ESA, 2026)"],["Pieces 1–10 cm","About 1.5 million (ESA, 2026)"],["Oldest satellite in orbit","Vanguard 1, launched 1958"],["Kessler syndrome","Proposed in 1978"]],
d:[["1958","Vanguard 1 is launched; it is still in orbit"],["1978","Donald Kessler warns of a chain reaction of collisions"],["1979","Skylab falls to Earth over Western Australia"],["2007","China destroys a weather satellite in a missile test"],["2009","Iridium 33 and Kosmos 2251 collide"],["2021","A Russian missile test creates a debris cloud"]],
s2:[["Kessler syndrome","In 1978 the NASA scientist Donald Kessler warned that if there were enough debris in orbit, collisions would create more fragments, causing more collisions, in a chain reaction. Some orbits could become too dangerous to use for generations. Preventing this is a major goal of space debris rules."],["Megaconstellations","Companies are launching thousands of small satellites to provide internet from space; SpaceX’s Starlink alone has launched many thousands. These systems are designed to steer away from collisions and to fall out of orbit at the end of their lives, but the sheer number of satellites makes careful traffic management essential."],["Falling back to Earth","Objects in low orbits slowly lose height because of thin air and eventually fall. Most burn up, but large pieces can survive. When the Skylab space station fell over Western Australia in 1979, the town of Esperance jokingly fined NASA for littering. Controlled spacecraft are often steered to crash in a remote part of the Pacific Ocean near Point Nemo."],["Cleaning up","Engineers are testing ways to remove debris, including nets, harpoons, robotic arms and magnetic docking plates. In 2018 the RemoveDEBRIS mission tested a net and harpoon in orbit. In 2024 the Japanese company Astroscale’s ADRAS-J spacecraft flew close to an old rocket stage to inspect it, a step toward removing it."],["Rules for clean space","Guidelines call for satellites in low orbit to be removed within a certain number of years after their missions end, by lowering them to burn up. Satellites in high geostationary orbit are moved farther out to a graveyard orbit. Spacecraft are also designed to release less debris during launch and operation."],["Hit by space junk","Only one person is known to have been touched by space debris: in 1997, Lottie Williams of Oklahoma was struck on the shoulder by a light piece of material, probably from a rocket, and was unhurt. The risk to any individual is extremely small, since most of Earth is ocean or empty land."]],
sp:["A chip in the window","Even tiny particles cause damage at orbital speeds. Space Shuttle windows had to be replaced after many missions because of small chips from paint flakes and dust. In 2021 a piece of debris punched a small hole in the Canadarm2 robotic arm on the ISS; luckily, the arm kept working. Cameras on the station regularly reveal small dents and pits from impacts."],
g:[["Space debris","Human-made objects in orbit that no longer serve a purpose"],["Low Earth orbit","Orbits up to about 2,000 km above Earth"],["Kessler syndrome","A chain reaction of collisions creating more debris"],["Graveyard orbit","A distant orbit where old satellites are parked"]],
f:["Debris in low orbit travels about ten times faster than a bullet.","Vanguard 1 has been in orbit since 1958.","The ISS has moved many times to avoid debris."],
fun:"An Australian town fined NASA for littering when Skylab fell to Earth."});

