// Human Body articles, part 3: the liver, blood types, surgery, heart disease and transplants.
ADDART({id:"liver",t:"The Liver",c:"Health and the Body",i:"⚗️",w:"Liver",r:["digest","blood","kidney","nutri"],
p:[["A busy organ","The liver is the largest internal organ, carrying out hundreds of jobs to keep the body healthy."],["Chemical factory","It cleans the blood, makes bile to digest fat and stores energy."],["It can regrow","The liver can regrow lost tissue, an ability rare among human organs."]],
l:"The liver is a large, reddish-brown organ in the upper right part of the abdomen, under the ribs. Weighing about 1.5 kilograms in adults, it is the largest internal organ and the largest gland in the body. It performs hundreds of tasks: it processes nutrients from food, stores energy, makes proteins needed for blood clotting, produces bile to digest fats, and breaks down toxins, medicines and alcohol. The liver can regenerate after injury, but long-term damage from viruses, alcohol or fat can lead to serious disease.",
s:[["Where it is and how it works","The liver sits below the diaphragm, protected by the ribs. It receives blood from two sources: the hepatic artery brings oxygen-rich blood, and the portal vein brings blood full of nutrients from the intestines. About 1.4 liters of blood flow through it every minute. Tiny units called lobules, made of liver cells called hepatocytes, process this blood."],["Bile and digestion","The liver produces bile, a greenish-yellow fluid that helps break down fats into tiny droplets so they can be digested. Bile is stored in the gallbladder, a small pouch under the liver, and released into the small intestine after meals. Bile also carries away waste, such as bilirubin, from old red blood cells."],["Storing energy","After a meal, the liver turns extra glucose, a sugar, into glycogen and stores it. Between meals, it turns glycogen back into glucose to keep blood sugar steady. The liver also stores vitamins A, D and B12, and iron and copper, releasing them when the body needs them."],["Cleaning the blood","The liver breaks down harmful substances, including alcohol, many medicines and waste products such as ammonia, which it turns into urea to be removed by the kidneys. It also removes old red blood cells and bacteria from the blood."]],
k:[["Weight","About 1.5 kg in adults"],["Location","Upper right abdomen"],["Blood flow","About 1.4 liters per minute"],["Main cells","Hepatocytes"],["Special ability","Regeneration"]],
d:[["c. 2000 BCE","Babylonian priests study animal livers to predict the future"],["1654","Francis Glisson describes the liver’s structure"],["1857","Claude Bernard identifies glycogen stored in the liver"],["1967","Thomas Starzl performs the first successful liver transplant"],["1981","A vaccine against hepatitis B is approved"],["1989","Hepatitis C virus is identified"]],
s2:[["Regeneration","The liver can regrow when part of it is removed or damaged. Even if more than half of a healthy liver is removed, the remaining part can grow back to nearly full size within a few months. This allows living people to donate part of their liver to someone who needs a transplant."],["Hepatitis","Hepatitis means inflammation of the liver, often caused by viruses. Hepatitis A spreads through contaminated food and water. Hepatitis B and C spread through blood and other body fluids and can cause long-term infection, scarring and liver cancer. Hundreds of millions of people live with hepatitis B or C. Vaccines prevent hepatitis A and B, and modern medicines can cure most cases of hepatitis C."],["Fatty liver disease","When too much fat builds up in liver cells, it causes fatty liver disease. It is increasingly common worldwide, linked to obesity, diabetes and unhealthy diets, as well as heavy drinking. Healthy eating, exercise and weight loss can often reverse it in its early stages."],["Cirrhosis","Long-term damage can cause cirrhosis, in which healthy liver tissue is replaced by scar tissue and the liver can no longer work properly. Causes include heavy alcohol use, chronic hepatitis and fatty liver disease. Advanced cirrhosis may require a liver transplant."],["Jaundice","Jaundice is a yellowing of the skin and eyes caused by a buildup of bilirubin, a yellow pigment from old red blood cells. In adults, it can signal liver disease. Many newborn babies develop mild jaundice because their livers are still maturing; it usually clears on its own or is treated with special blue light."],["Keeping the liver healthy","People can protect their livers by getting vaccinated against hepatitis B, avoiding too much alcohol, eating a balanced diet, staying active and taking medicines only as directed, since too much of some common medicines, such as paracetamol, can harm the liver."]],
sp:["The myth of Prometheus","In Greek mythology, the Titan Prometheus stole fire from the gods and gave it to humans. As punishment, Zeus chained him to a rock, where an eagle ate his liver every day, and every night the liver grew back. Some people wonder whether the ancient Greeks knew about the liver’s ability to regenerate, though the story may simply be a myth of endless punishment."],
g:[["Hepatocyte","The main type of liver cell"],["Bile","Fluid made by the liver to help digest fats"],["Glycogen","A stored form of glucose"],["Cirrhosis","Scarring of the liver"]],
f:["The liver is the largest internal organ.","The liver can regrow after part of it is removed.","Bile is stored in the gallbladder."],
fun:"The liver performs hundreds of different jobs in the body."});

ADDART({id:"bloodtypes",t:"Blood Types",c:"Health and the Body",i:"🩸",w:"Blood type",r:["blood","immune","heart","transplant"],
p:[["Not all blood is the same","People have different blood types, based on markers on the surface of their red blood cells."],["A, B, AB and O","The main blood group system is ABO, combined with the Rh factor, positive or negative."],["Matching saves lives","Blood transfusions must be matched, or the body may attack the donated blood."]],
l:"A blood type, or blood group, is a classification of blood based on the presence or absence of certain molecules, called antigens, on the surface of red blood cells. The most important systems are ABO, which gives types A, B, AB and O, and the Rh system, which makes blood positive or negative. Blood types are inherited from parents. Knowing a person’s blood type is essential for safe blood transfusions, organ transplants and pregnancy care.",
s:[["The ABO system","In 1901 the Austrian doctor Karl Landsteiner discovered that mixing blood from different people sometimes made it clump. He identified groups A, B and O; group AB was found soon after. Type A blood has A antigens, type B has B antigens, AB has both and O has neither. The plasma contains antibodies against the antigens a person does not have."],["The Rh factor","In 1940 Landsteiner and Alexander Wiener discovered another antigen, called the Rh factor after rhesus monkeys used in their experiments. People who have it are Rh positive; those without it are Rh negative. Combined with ABO, this gives eight common types, such as O positive or AB negative."],["Donors and recipients","People with O negative blood are called universal donors, because their red cells can usually be given to anyone. People with AB positive blood are universal recipients, able to receive red cells of any ABO and Rh type. Hospitals still test carefully before every transfusion to make sure blood is compatible."],["How blood types are inherited","Each person inherits one ABO gene from each parent. A and B are dominant over O, so a child with genes A and O has type A blood. Two parents with type A blood can have a child with type O if both carry the O gene. The Rh factor is inherited in a similar way."]],
k:[["Main systems","ABO and Rh"],["Discovered","1901, by Karl Landsteiner"],["Universal red-cell donor","O negative"],["Universal recipient","AB positive"],["Most common worldwide","O positive"]],
d:[["1667","Early animal-to-human blood transfusions are attempted"],["1901","Landsteiner discovers the ABO blood groups"],["1930","Landsteiner wins the Nobel Prize"],["1940","The Rh factor is discovered"],["1952","The rare Bombay blood group is discovered in India"],["2004","World Blood Donor Day is first held, on 14 June"]],
s2:[["Blood types around the world","Blood types vary across populations. Type O is the most common in many parts of the world, including much of Africa, the Americas and Southeast Asia, including the Philippines. Type B is more common in parts of Central and South Asia. Rh negative blood is relatively common in Europe but rare in East and Southeast Asia, making it harder to find donors there."],["Pregnancy and the Rh factor","If an Rh negative mother carries an Rh positive baby, her body may make antibodies against the baby’s blood, which can harm later pregnancies. This condition, called hemolytic disease of the newborn, is now largely prevented with an injection called anti-D immunoglobulin, introduced in the late 1960s."],["Rare blood","Scientists recognize more than 40 blood group systems besides ABO and Rh. Some people have very rare blood, such as the Bombay type, first found in Mumbai in 1952, or Rh-null, sometimes called golden blood, found in fewer than about 50 people known worldwide. International networks help find matching donors."],["Donating blood","Donated blood is separated into red cells, plasma and platelets, so one donation can help several patients, such as accident victims, people having surgery, mothers in childbirth and patients with cancer or blood disorders. Red cells can be stored for up to about six weeks, but platelets only for a few days, so regular donations are always needed."],["Blood types and personality","In Japan and South Korea, a popular belief holds that blood type shapes personality, for example that type A people are careful and type B people are creative. This idea, called ketsueki-gata in Japanese, appears in magazines and dating advice, but scientific studies have found no reliable link between blood type and personality."],["Artificial blood","Because donated blood is sometimes scarce, scientists are developing alternatives, including red blood cells grown in laboratories from stem cells and substances that can carry oxygen. In 2022 a British trial gave volunteers small amounts of lab-grown red blood cells for the first time. These may one day help people with rare blood types."]],
sp:["Karl Landsteiner","Karl Landsteiner was an Austrian-born doctor who worked in Vienna and later New York. Before his discovery of blood groups in 1901, blood transfusions were dangerous and often fatal. His work made safe transfusions possible, saving countless lives. He won the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine in 1930, and World Blood Donor Day is held on his birthday, 14 June."],
g:[["Antigen","A molecule that can trigger an immune response"],["Antibody","A protein that recognizes and attacks antigens"],["Transfusion","Giving blood from one person to another"],["Rh factor","A blood antigen that makes blood positive or negative"]],
f:["Karl Landsteiner discovered blood groups in 1901.","O negative blood can be given to almost anyone.","World Blood Donor Day is on 14 June."],
fun:"Fewer than about 50 people in the world are known to have Rh-null blood."});

ADDART({id:"surgery",t:"Surgery",c:"Health and the Body",i:"🩺",w:"Surgery",r:["ibnsina","pasteur","antibiotics","transplant"],
p:[["Healing by operating","Surgery treats injuries and diseases by operating on the body with instruments."],["Ancient roots","People performed surgery thousands of years ago, from ancient Egypt and India to Peru."],["Modern miracles","Anesthesia, antiseptics and new technology have made surgery far safer."]],
l:"Surgery is the branch of medicine that treats diseases, injuries and deformities by operating on the body, cutting, repairing or removing tissues. It is one of the oldest medical practices: skulls with healed holes show that people performed surgery thousands of years ago. Modern surgery began in the 1800s with anesthesia to prevent pain and antiseptics to prevent infection. Today surgeons replace joints, repair hearts, remove tumors and transplant organs, often using tiny cameras, lasers and robotic tools.",
s:[["Ancient surgery","Trepanation, drilling holes in the skull, was practiced in many places, including Neolithic Europe and the ancient Andes, and many patients survived. The Edwin Smith Papyrus from ancient Egypt describes how to treat wounds and broken bones. The Sushruta Samhita from ancient India described many operations, including rebuilding noses, and dozens of instruments."],["Medieval and early modern surgery","Abu al-Qasim al-Zahrawi, known in Europe as Albucasis, worked in Córdoba around 1000 CE and wrote a medical encyclopedia with drawings of about 200 surgical instruments, used in Europe for centuries. In the 1500s the French surgeon Ambroise Paré treated soldiers’ wounds more gently and tied off blood vessels instead of burning them."],["Anesthesia","Before anesthesia, surgery was agonizing, and surgeons worked as fast as possible. In 1804 the Japanese doctor Hanaoka Seishū used an herbal general anesthetic to remove a breast tumor. On 16 October 1846, William Morton publicly demonstrated ether anesthesia in Boston, and the use of ether and chloroform soon spread worldwide."],["Fighting infection","Many patients once died of infections after surgery. Inspired by Louis Pasteur’s germ theory, the British surgeon Joseph Lister began using carbolic acid to clean wounds and instruments in 1867, and death rates fell sharply. Later, surgeons began sterilizing instruments, wearing gloves, masks and gowns, and using antibiotics."]],
k:[["Oldest form","Trepanation, thousands of years old"],["Ether anesthesia demonstrated","16 October 1846"],["Antiseptic surgery","Joseph Lister, 1867"],["Keyhole surgery spread","1980s"],["WHO Surgical Safety Checklist","2008"]],
d:[["c. 1600 BCE","The Edwin Smith Papyrus is written in Egypt"],["c. 1000 CE","Al-Zahrawi writes his surgical encyclopedia"],["1804","Hanaoka Seishū performs surgery under general anesthesia"],["1846","Ether anesthesia is demonstrated in Boston"],["1867","Lister introduces antiseptic surgery"],["2000","The da Vinci surgical robot is approved in the US"]],
s2:[["Surgical specialties","Surgeons often specialize. Orthopedic surgeons treat bones and joints; cardiac surgeons operate on the heart; neurosurgeons work on the brain and spine; pediatric surgeons treat children; and plastic surgeons repair skin and tissue after injuries, burns or birth defects. Each specialty requires many years of training after medical school."],["Keyhole surgery","Keyhole, or laparoscopic, surgery uses small cuts through which surgeons insert a tiny camera and thin instruments, watching on a screen. It became widespread in the 1980s and 1990s for operations such as removing the gallbladder. Patients have less pain, smaller scars and recover faster than after open surgery."],["Robots in the operating room","In robotic surgery, a surgeon sits at a console and controls robotic arms that hold instruments inside the patient, with magnified 3D views. The da Vinci system, approved in the United States in 2000, is used worldwide. In 2001 surgeons in New York removed the gallbladder of a patient in Strasbourg, France, by remote control."],["The surgical team","Surgery is teamwork. Besides the surgeon, a team includes an anesthesiologist, who keeps the patient asleep and monitors breathing and heartbeat, and nurses and technicians who prepare instruments and assist. The World Health Organization’s Surgical Safety Checklist, introduced in 2008, helps teams avoid mistakes and has reduced complications."],["Surgery for everyone","A major international study in 2015 found that about five billion people lack access to safe, affordable surgery and anesthesia when they need it, especially in low-income countries. Organizations train local surgeons, build operating rooms and run programs to repair conditions such as cleft lips, cataracts and injuries."],["Surgery in the Philippines","The Philippines has a long tradition of medical training, and Filipino surgeons and nurses work in hospitals around the world. Major hospitals such as the Philippine General Hospital and the Philippine Heart Center perform complex operations, while medical missions bring surgery to remote islands where hospitals are far away."]],
sp:["The fastest surgeon","Before anesthesia, speed was a surgeon’s most important skill. The Scottish surgeon Robert Liston, working in London in the 1840s, was famous for amputating a leg in under three minutes. In December 1846, Liston performed one of the first operations in Europe using ether. After the patient woke, Liston declared that the American method beat mesmerism hollow, a sign of how much anesthesia changed surgery."],
g:[["Anesthesia","Medicine that prevents pain during surgery"],["Antiseptic","A substance that kills germs on skin or surfaces"],["Laparoscopy","Keyhole surgery using a tiny camera"],["Trepanation","Drilling a hole in the skull"]],
f:["People performed surgery on skulls thousands of years ago.","Ether anesthesia was first demonstrated publicly in 1846.","Robots now help surgeons in many operations."],
fun:"In 2001 surgeons in New York operated on a patient in France by remote control."});

ADDART({id:"heartdisease",t:"Heart Disease",c:"Health and the Body",i:"❤️‍🩹",w:"Cardiovascular disease",r:["heart","blood","exercise","nutri"],
p:[["The leading cause of death","Diseases of the heart and blood vessels kill more people worldwide than any other cause."],["Often preventable","Many cases can be prevented by not smoking, eating well, staying active and controlling blood pressure."],["Life-saving treatments","Modern medicines and procedures have greatly improved survival after heart attacks."]],
l:"Heart disease, or cardiovascular disease, includes conditions affecting the heart and blood vessels, such as coronary artery disease, heart attacks, strokes, heart failure and high blood pressure. Together, they are the leading cause of death worldwide, responsible for about a third of all deaths. Most are linked to risk factors that can be reduced: smoking, unhealthy diets, physical inactivity, high blood pressure, diabetes and harmful use of alcohol. Advances in prevention and treatment have saved millions of lives.",
s:[["Coronary artery disease","The heart muscle gets oxygen from coronary arteries. Over years, fatty deposits called plaque can build up inside them, a process called atherosclerosis, narrowing the arteries. This can cause chest pain, called angina, especially during exercise. If a plaque breaks open, a blood clot can form and block the artery."],["Heart attack","A heart attack happens when blood flow to part of the heart muscle is blocked, so the muscle begins to die. Common signs include chest pain or pressure, pain spreading to the arm, jaw or back, shortness of breath, sweating and nausea. Women more often have less typical symptoms. Calling emergency services quickly saves lives."],["Stroke","A stroke happens when blood supply to part of the brain is blocked by a clot or a burst blood vessel. Signs can be remembered with FAST: Face drooping, Arm weakness, Speech difficulty, Time to call for help. Fast treatment can reduce brain damage."],["High blood pressure","High blood pressure, or hypertension, strains the heart and blood vessels and is a leading cause of heart attacks, strokes and kidney disease. More than a billion adults worldwide have it, and many do not know because it often causes no symptoms. Regular checks, less salt, exercise and medicines can control it."]],
k:[["Global deaths","About 20 million a year"],["Share of all deaths","About one-third"],["Main risk factors","Smoking, diet, inactivity, blood pressure"],["Stroke warning signs","FAST"],["World Heart Day","29 September"]],
d:[["1929","Werner Forssmann inserts a catheter into his own heart"],["1948","The Framingham Heart Study begins"],["1958","The first internal pacemaker is implanted"],["1967","Christiaan Barnard performs the first human heart transplant"],["1977","Andreas Grüntzig performs the first coronary angioplasty"],["1987","The first statin medicine is approved"]],
s2:[["The Framingham Heart Study","In 1948 researchers began studying thousands of people in the town of Framingham, Massachusetts, following them, and later their children and grandchildren, for decades. The study helped identify the main risk factors for heart disease, such as high blood pressure, high cholesterol and smoking. The term risk factor itself became popular through this research."],["Treatments","Medicines such as aspirin, statins, which lower cholesterol, and blood pressure pills help prevent heart attacks and strokes. Angioplasty uses a tiny balloon passed through a blood vessel to open a blocked artery, often leaving a mesh tube called a stent. Bypass surgery uses vessels from elsewhere in the body to route blood around blockages."],["CPR and defibrillators","When the heart suddenly stops, cardiopulmonary resuscitation, CPR, pushing hard and fast on the center of the chest, keeps blood flowing to the brain. An automated external defibrillator, or AED, can deliver an electric shock to restart a normal rhythm. AEDs in public places give spoken instructions so anyone can use them."],["Heart disease in the Philippines","Heart disease is the leading cause of death in the Philippines, as in many countries. The Philippine Heart Center in Quezon City, opened in 1975, is a major hospital for heart care. Public health campaigns encourage reducing salt, sugar and tobacco and checking blood pressure regularly."],["Rheumatic heart disease","Rheumatic heart disease begins with untreated strep throat infections, which can lead the immune system to damage heart valves. It is now rare in wealthy countries but still affects millions of people, especially young people in low-income countries. Treating strep throat with antibiotics prevents it."],["A healthy heart","The heart benefits from regular physical activity, a diet rich in vegetables, fruits, whole grains and beans, limited salt, sugar and processed foods, not smoking, good sleep, a healthy weight and managing stress. Many of these habits begin in childhood and protect the heart for a lifetime."]],
sp:["The doctor who tested on himself","In 1929 a young German doctor, Werner Forssmann, wanted to show that a thin tube could be safely passed through a vein into the heart. When his boss refused permission, he numbed his own arm, threaded a catheter about 65 centimeters into his own heart and walked to the X-ray room to photograph it. He was criticized at the time, but cardiac catheterization became a key tool in heart medicine, and in 1956 he shared the Nobel Prize."],
g:[["Cardiovascular","Relating to the heart and blood vessels"],["Atherosclerosis","Buildup of plaque inside arteries"],["Hypertension","High blood pressure"],["Stent","A small mesh tube that holds an artery open"]],
f:["Heart disease is the leading cause of death worldwide.","FAST helps people recognize a stroke.","A defibrillator can restart a normal heartbeat."],
fun:"A doctor once put a tube into his own heart to prove it could be done safely."});

ADDART({id:"transplant",t:"Organ Transplants",c:"Health and the Body",i:"🫁",w:"Organ transplantation",r:["kidney","heart","liver","immune","bloodtypes"],
p:[["A gift of life","An organ transplant replaces a failing organ with a healthy one from a donor."],["Many organs","Kidneys, livers, hearts, lungs, pancreases, corneas and even hands and faces can be transplanted."],["Fighting rejection","Patients take medicines to stop their immune systems from attacking the new organ."]],
l:"An organ transplant is a medical procedure in which an organ or tissue is removed from one person, the donor, and placed in another, the recipient, to replace a damaged or failing one. Organs come from living donors, who can give a kidney or part of the liver, or from people who have died and agreed to donate. Since the first successful kidney transplant in 1954, transplants have saved millions of lives. Around the world, more than 150,000 organ transplants are performed each year, but many more people are waiting.",
s:[["The first successes","On 23 December 1954, a team led by Joseph Murray in Boston transplanted a kidney from Ronald Herrick into his identical twin brother, Richard. Because they were identical twins, Richard’s body did not reject the kidney. Murray later won the Nobel Prize. The first liver transplant to succeed was performed by Thomas Starzl in 1967, and the first human heart transplant by Christiaan Barnard in South Africa the same year."],["Rejection","The immune system protects the body by attacking anything foreign, including a transplanted organ. Doctors reduce this risk by matching donors and recipients by blood type and tissue type and by giving immunosuppressant medicines. The drug cyclosporine, approved in the early 1980s, greatly improved transplant success."],["Living and deceased donors","A living person can donate one kidney, since we can live with one, or part of the liver, which regrows. Most other organs come from deceased donors, people who have died, often after brain death, while their organs are kept working by machines. A single donor can save up to eight lives through organ donation."],["Waiting lists","There are not enough donated organs for everyone who needs one. Patients are placed on waiting lists, and organs are allocated based on medical urgency, compatibility and waiting time. Many die while waiting, which is why health organizations encourage people to register as donors."]],
k:[["First successful kidney transplant","1954, Boston"],["First heart transplant","1967, Cape Town"],["Transplants per year","More than 150,000 worldwide"],["Lives one donor can save","Up to eight"],["Key anti-rejection drug","Cyclosporine (1980s)"]],
d:[["1905","Eduard Zirm performs a successful cornea transplant"],["1954","The first successful kidney transplant"],["1967","First successful liver and first heart transplants"],["1983","Cyclosporine is approved, improving success rates"],["2005","The first partial face transplant is performed in France"],["2022","A man receives a genetically modified pig heart"]],
s2:[["Christiaan Barnard","On 3 December 1967, the South African surgeon Christiaan Barnard performed the first human-to-human heart transplant at Groote Schuur Hospital in Cape Town. The patient, Louis Washkansky, lived for 18 days before dying of pneumonia. Barnard’s next patient lived for more than a year and a half, and heart transplants gradually became more successful."],["Tissues and corneas","Besides organs, people can donate tissues such as corneas, skin, bone, heart valves and tendons. Cornea transplants restore sight to many people with damaged eyes. Bone marrow and stem cell transplants treat blood cancers such as leukemia; E. Donnall Thomas shared the 1990 Nobel Prize with Joseph Murray for this work."],["Hands and faces","Surgeons can now transplant complex body parts, such as hands, arms and faces, joining bones, muscles, nerves, blood vessels and skin. The first partial face transplant was performed in France in 2005 on Isabelle Dinoire, who had been injured by a dog. These operations greatly improve quality of life but require lifelong medicines."],["Organs from animals","Because human organs are scarce, scientists are developing xenotransplantation, using organs from animals, mostly pigs genetically modified to be less likely to be rejected. In 2022 a man in the United States received a pig heart and lived for about two months. In 2024 the first genetically modified pig kidneys were transplanted into living patients."],["Ethics and laws","Selling organs is illegal in almost all countries, and international agreements such as the Declaration of Istanbul of 2008 work to stop organ trafficking. Countries have different donation systems: some ask people to opt in by registering, while others, such as Spain, presume consent unless people opt out. Spain has one of the world’s highest donation rates."],["Growing new organs","Scientists hope to grow replacement organs from a patient’s own cells, avoiding rejection. Researchers have grown simple tissues, such as bladders, skin and windpipes, and are working on 3D-printing organs and growing mini organs called organoids. Fully working lab-grown hearts or kidneys are still many years away."]],
sp:["The Herrick twins","In 1954 Richard Herrick, 23, was dying of kidney failure. His identical twin, Ronald, offered one of his kidneys. Doctors confirmed that they were identical, even taking fingerprints, and on 23 December surgeons in Boston performed the operation. Richard lived for eight more years, married one of his nurses and had two children. Ronald lived a long, healthy life with one kidney, until 2010."],
g:[["Donor","A person who gives an organ or tissue"],["Recipient","A person who receives an organ or tissue"],["Rejection","When the immune system attacks a transplanted organ"],["Immunosuppressant","A medicine that weakens the immune response"]],
f:["The first successful kidney transplant was between twins.","A person can live with one kidney.","One organ donor can save up to eight lives."],
fun:"Doctors took fingerprints to prove the first kidney transplant twins were identical."});

