// Science articles, part 3: mixtures, metals, carbon chemistry and crystals.
ADDART({id:"mixtures",t:"Mixtures and Solutions",c:"Science",i:"🧂",w:"Mixture",r:["states","reactions","acid","water","alchemy"],
p:[["Substances mixed together","A mixture is two or more substances combined without forming a new chemical substance."],["Dissolving","In a solution, such as salt water, one substance dissolves evenly into another."],["Separating mixtures","Mixtures can be separated by filtering, evaporating, distilling and other methods."]],
l:"A mixture is a combination of two or more substances that are physically mixed but not chemically joined. Each part keeps its own properties, and the parts can usually be separated by physical methods. Air, seawater, soil, milk and salad are all mixtures. Some mixtures, called solutions, are so evenly mixed that they look like a single substance; others, such as sand in water, have parts that can be seen. Understanding mixtures helps chemists purify water, refine oil, make medicines and much more.",
s:[["Mixtures and compounds","In a compound, such as water, elements are chemically bonded in fixed proportions and have new properties. In a mixture, the substances can be combined in any amounts and are not bonded. Iron filings and sulfur powder stirred together form a mixture, and a magnet can pull the iron out. If they are heated, they react to form iron sulfide, a compound."],["Homogeneous and heterogeneous","A homogeneous mixture has the same makeup throughout, like sugar dissolved in water or the air we breathe. A heterogeneous mixture has visibly different parts, such as sand and gravel, or oil floating on water. Some mixtures fall in between."],["Solutions","In a solution, a substance called the solute dissolves in another called the solvent. Water is called the universal solvent because it dissolves so many substances. Solutions can also be gases, like air, or solids, like metal alloys such as brass. Hot water usually dissolves more sugar or salt than cold water."],["Colloids and suspensions","In a colloid, tiny particles are spread through another substance but do not dissolve or settle, as in milk, fog, mayonnaise and gelatin. Shining a beam of light through a colloid makes the beam visible, called the Tyndall effect. In a suspension, such as muddy water, larger particles eventually settle to the bottom."]],
k:[["Definition","Substances mixed but not chemically bonded"],["Air","About 78% nitrogen, 21% oxygen"],["Seawater salt","About 3.5%"],["Universal solvent","Water"],["Separation methods","Filtering, evaporation, distillation, chromatography"]],
d:[["c. 3000 BCE","People evaporate seawater to make salt"],["c. 800s","Distillation is refined by scholars in the Islamic world"],["1772–1774","Scientists discover that air contains several gases"],["1869","John Tyndall describes light scattering in colloids"],["1903","Mikhail Tsvet introduces chromatography"],["1960s","Large desalination plants begin operating"]],
s2:[["Filtering and evaporation","Filtering separates solid particles from a liquid by passing it through a material with tiny holes, such as filter paper or sand. Evaporation removes a liquid, leaving dissolved solids behind. People have made salt by evaporating seawater for thousands of years, as salt makers still do along the coasts of Pangasinan in the Philippines and in many other countries."],["Distillation","Distillation separates liquids by boiling points. The mixture is heated, the substance with the lower boiling point evaporates first, and its vapor is cooled back into a liquid in another container. Distillation is used to make fresh water from seawater, to refine crude oil into gasoline and diesel and to produce perfumes."],["Chromatography","Chromatography separates the substances in a mixture by how fast they move through a material. In a simple classroom experiment, a dot of ink on paper spreads into colored bands as water soaks upward. The Russian-Italian botanist Mikhail Tsvet invented it around 1903 to separate plant pigments. Today it is used to test food, medicines and crime-scene evidence."],["Other methods","A magnet separates iron from other materials, as in recycling plants. A centrifuge spins mixtures very fast, separating blood into plasma and cells. Decanting means carefully pouring off a liquid, leaving solids behind. Panning for gold uses water to wash away lighter sand, leaving heavy gold flakes in the pan."],["Making seawater drinkable","Desalination removes salt from seawater to make fresh water. Most modern plants use reverse osmosis, pushing seawater at high pressure through membranes that let water pass but block salt. Desalination supplies much of the drinking water in countries such as Saudi Arabia, the United Arab Emirates and Israel, but it uses a lot of energy."],["Mixtures in daily life","Many everyday products are mixtures carefully designed by chemists: toothpaste, paint, shampoo, medicines, concrete and soft drinks. Cooking, too, is often about making mixtures, such as dressings, batters and sauces, and sometimes about chemical reactions, such as baking bread."]],
sp:["Why oil and water don’t mix","Water molecules have a slight electric charge at each end, so they stick strongly to each other. Oil molecules have no such charges, so water pulls itself together and pushes oil out of the way. Soap and detergents work because their molecules have one end that attracts water and another that attracts oil, so they can surround grease and carry it away when you rinse."],
g:[["Solute","A substance dissolved in a solution"],["Solvent","The substance that does the dissolving"],["Colloid","A mixture of tiny particles that do not settle"],["Distillation","Separating liquids by boiling and condensing"]],
f:["Air is a mixture of gases.","Water is called the universal solvent.","Chromatography can separate the colors in ink."],
fun:"Soap works because its molecules grab water at one end and oil at the other."});

ADDART({id:"metals",t:"Metals",c:"Science",i:"🔩",w:"Metal",r:["atom","magnet","chip","goldage"],
p:[["Shiny and strong","Metals are elements that are usually shiny, strong and good at carrying heat and electricity."],["Most elements are metals","More than three-quarters of the known chemical elements are metals."],["Shaping history","Copper, bronze and iron gave their names to whole ages of human history."]],
l:"Metals are a large group of chemical elements, and their mixtures called alloys, that are usually shiny, hard, strong and good conductors of heat and electricity. Most can be hammered into sheets and drawn into wires. Iron, aluminum, copper, gold and silver are familiar metals. Metals have shaped human history, from the first copper tools to the steel of skyscrapers and the lithium in phone batteries. Of the 118 known elements, more than 90 are metals.",
s:[["Properties of metals","Metal atoms share a sea of loosely held electrons that can move freely. This explains why metals conduct electricity and heat so well, why they are shiny, reflecting light, and why they can bend and stretch without breaking. Most metals are solid at room temperature; mercury is a liquid."],["Alloys","Metals are often mixed to make alloys with better properties. Bronze, copper with tin, was harder than copper alone. Steel, iron with a little carbon, is far stronger than iron. Brass is copper and zinc; stainless steel adds chromium to resist rust. Solder, pewter and the gold used in jewelry are also alloys."],["The ages of metal","People first used native copper, found naturally as metal, about 10,000 years ago. Later they learned to smelt metals from rocks called ores. The Bronze Age began around 3300 BCE in the Middle East, and the Iron Age around 1200 BCE. Iron was harder to work but more plentiful, making strong tools and weapons available to many people."],["Corrosion","Many metals react slowly with air and water. Iron rusts, forming flaky iron oxide that weakens it. Copper turns green, as on the Statue of Liberty. Aluminum quickly forms a thin protective layer of oxide. Painting, coating with zinc, called galvanizing, and using alloys help protect metals."]],
k:[["Metallic elements","More than 90 of 118"],["Most abundant in Earth’s crust","Aluminum"],["Most used metal","Iron, mainly as steel"],["Liquid at room temperature","Mercury"],["Highest melting point","Tungsten, about 3,422°C"]],
d:[["c. 8000 BCE","People hammer native copper into objects"],["c. 3300 BCE","The Bronze Age begins in the Middle East"],["c. 1200 BCE","The Iron Age spreads"],["1825","Hans Christian Ørsted produces aluminum"],["1856","Henry Bessemer patents a cheap way to make steel"],["1886","The Hall–Héroult process makes aluminum cheap"]],
s2:[["Iron and steel","Iron makes up much of Earth’s core and is the fourth most common element in the crust. It is extracted from ores in blast furnaces. Most iron becomes steel, the world’s most important building material, used in bridges, cars, ships, railways and buildings. Around two billion metric tons of steel are made each year."],["Aluminum","Aluminum is the most common metal in Earth’s crust but is never found as pure metal. Until the 1880s it was so hard to extract that it was more valuable than gold; Napoleon III reportedly served honored guests with aluminum cutlery. The Hall–Héroult process, discovered in 1886, made it cheap. Recycling aluminum uses only about 5 percent of the energy needed to make it new."],["Precious metals","Gold, silver and platinum are called precious metals because they are rare and do not rust. Gold has been prized for jewelry and money for thousands of years. All the gold ever mined would fit into a cube about 22 meters on each side. Gold is also used in electronics because it conducts well and does not corrode."],["Metals of the modern world","Modern technology depends on many metals. Copper carries electricity in wires. Lithium, cobalt and nickel are used in rechargeable batteries. Titanium is strong and light, used in aircraft and artificial joints. Rare earth elements, such as neodymium, make powerful magnets for wind turbines, electric motors and headphones."],["Metals in the Philippines","The Philippines has rich mineral resources. It is one of the world’s largest producers of nickel ore, mined mainly in Mindanao and Palawan, and has long mined gold and copper in places such as Benguet. Mining brings income and jobs but must be managed carefully to protect forests, rivers and communities."],["Metals in the body","The body needs small amounts of certain metals. Iron in hemoglobin carries oxygen in the blood; calcium builds bones and teeth; sodium and potassium help nerves send signals; and zinc helps the immune system. But some metals, such as lead and mercury, are toxic even in small amounts."]],
sp:["The ice maiden of metals","Gallium is a soft, silvery metal that melts at about 30°C, so it can melt in your hand. Chemists sometimes play a trick with spoons made of gallium, which melt when used to stir a hot drink. Gallium is useful too: compounds such as gallium nitride are used in blue LEDs, laser diodes and fast phone chargers."],
g:[["Alloy","A mixture of a metal with other elements"],["Ore","A rock from which metal can be extracted"],["Smelting","Heating ore to extract metal"],["Conductor","A material that lets heat or electricity flow easily"]],
f:["Most elements are metals.","Steel is iron mixed with a little carbon.","Mercury is liquid at room temperature."],
fun:"Aluminum was once more precious than gold."});

ADDART({id:"carbonchem",t:"Carbon and Organic Chemistry",c:"Science",i:"💎",w:"Organic chemistry",r:["atom","plastics","cell","climchange","rfranklin"],
p:[["The element of life","Carbon is the basis of all known life, found in every living thing."],["Endless combinations","Carbon atoms link into chains and rings, forming millions of different compounds."],["Diamonds and pencils","Pure carbon can be soft graphite or diamond, the hardest natural material."]],
l:"Carbon is the sixth element of the periodic table and one of the most important. Each carbon atom can form four strong bonds with other atoms, including other carbon atoms, so it can build long chains, branches and rings. This makes possible an enormous variety of molecules, from the sugars, fats, proteins and DNA in living things to fuels, plastics and medicines. The study of carbon compounds is called organic chemistry. Pure carbon also exists in several forms, including diamond, graphite and graphene.",
s:[["Why carbon is special","A carbon atom has four electrons available for bonding, so it can join up to four other atoms. Carbon atoms bond strongly with each other and with hydrogen, oxygen, nitrogen and other elements. This allows them to build huge, stable molecules of almost any shape. Millions of carbon compounds are known, far more than for any other element except hydrogen."],["Forms of pure carbon","Different arrangements of carbon atoms produce very different materials. In diamond, each atom is bonded to four others in a rigid 3D framework, making it extremely hard. In graphite, used in pencils, atoms form flat sheets that slide over one another. Graphene is a single sheet of graphite one atom thick, while fullerenes are hollow balls and tubes of carbon."],["The molecules of life","Living things are built from four main kinds of carbon-based molecules: carbohydrates, such as sugars and starch, which provide energy; lipids, or fats, which store energy and form cell membranes; proteins, which do most of the work in cells; and nucleic acids, DNA and RNA, which carry genetic information."],["Hydrocarbons and fuels","Hydrocarbons are compounds of carbon and hydrogen. Methane, the main part of natural gas, has one carbon atom; propane, used in cooking gas tanks, has three; and gasoline contains chains of about 4 to 12. Coal, oil and natural gas formed from ancient living things. Burning them releases energy but also carbon dioxide, which contributes to climate change."]],
k:[["Atomic number","6"],["Bonds per atom","Four"],["Hardest natural form","Diamond"],["Thinnest form","Graphene, one atom thick"],["Dating method","Carbon-14 dating"]],
d:[["1772","Antoine Lavoisier shows diamond is a form of carbon"],["1828","Friedrich Wöhler makes urea from non-living chemicals"],["1865","August Kekulé proposes the ring structure of benzene"],["1949","Willard Libby develops radiocarbon dating"],["1985","Buckminsterfullerene is discovered"],["2004","Graphene is isolated using sticky tape"]],
s2:[["The birth of organic chemistry","People once believed that the chemicals of living things contained a special life force and could only be made by living organisms. In 1828 the German chemist Friedrich Wöhler made urea, a substance found in urine, from non-living chemicals. This helped show that the chemistry of life follows the same rules as all other chemistry."],["Kekulé’s dream","Benzene, a compound of six carbon and six hydrogen atoms, puzzled chemists. In 1865 August Kekulé proposed that its carbon atoms form a ring. He later said the idea came to him in a daydream of a snake biting its own tail. Ring-shaped molecules are now known to be common in medicines, dyes and living things."],["Radiocarbon dating","Living things take in carbon, including a tiny amount of radioactive carbon-14. When they die, the carbon-14 slowly decays at a known rate, halving about every 5,730 years. By measuring how much is left, scientists can date bones, wood, cloth and other once-living materials up to about 50,000 years old. Willard Libby won the 1960 Nobel Prize in Chemistry for this method."],["Graphene","In 2004 Andre Geim and Konstantin Novoselov at the University of Manchester peeled thin layers off graphite using sticky tape until they isolated graphene, a single layer of carbon atoms. Graphene is extremely strong, light and good at conducting electricity. They won the Nobel Prize in Physics in 2010. Scientists are exploring its use in electronics, batteries and filters."],["Buckyballs and nanotubes","In 1985 scientists discovered buckminsterfullerene, a molecule of 60 carbon atoms shaped like a soccer ball, named after the architect Buckminster Fuller, who designed similar domes. In 1991 the Japanese scientist Sumio Iijima drew wide attention to carbon nanotubes, tiny tubes of rolled-up carbon sheets, stronger than steel for their weight."],["The carbon cycle","Carbon moves constantly between the air, oceans, rocks and living things. Plants take in carbon dioxide during photosynthesis; animals eat plants and breathe out carbon dioxide; and dead organisms decay or become buried. Burning fossil fuels and cutting forests add extra carbon dioxide to the air, upsetting this balance and warming the planet."]],
sp:["Made in stars","All the carbon in your body was made inside stars. In the cores of old stars, three helium nuclei can fuse to form one carbon nucleus, in what is called the triple-alpha process. When those stars died, they scattered carbon into space, where it became part of new stars, planets and, eventually, living things. Carbon is the fourth most abundant element in the universe."],
g:[["Organic chemistry","The study of carbon compounds"],["Hydrocarbon","A compound of only carbon and hydrogen"],["Allotrope","A different form of the same element"],["Graphene","A single layer of carbon atoms"]],
f:["Diamond and pencil graphite are both pure carbon.","Carbon atoms can form four bonds.","Carbon-14 dating works up to about 50,000 years."],
fun:"The carbon in your body was made inside ancient stars."});

ADDART({id:"crystals",t:"Crystals",c:"Science",i:"❄️",w:"Crystal",r:["states","atom","rfranklin","chip","iceage"],
p:[["Order in nature","A crystal is a solid whose atoms are arranged in a neat, repeating pattern."],["Everywhere around us","Salt, sugar, snowflakes, quartz, diamonds and even metals are made of crystals."],["Crystals in technology","Crystals keep time in watches and form the silicon chips inside computers."]],
l:"A crystal is a solid material whose atoms, ions or molecules are arranged in a highly ordered, repeating pattern that extends in all directions. This inner order often gives crystals flat faces and sharp edges. Many substances form crystals, including table salt, sugar, ice, minerals and gemstones, and most metals are made of countless tiny crystals. Studying crystals with X-rays has revealed the structure of DNA, proteins and many medicines, and crystals are essential in modern electronics.",
s:[["How crystals grow","Crystals grow when atoms or molecules join together in an orderly way, as a liquid cools, as water evaporates from a solution or as vapor freezes. Slow growth usually produces larger, more perfect crystals. Salt crystals form as seawater evaporates; snowflakes form as water vapor freezes in clouds; and minerals crystallize from cooling magma deep underground."],["Crystal shapes","The pattern of atoms decides a crystal’s shape. Table salt forms cubes because sodium and chloride ions are arranged in a cubic grid. Snowflakes are six-sided because of the way water molecules link up in ice. Quartz forms six-sided columns with pointed tips. Scientists group crystals into seven crystal systems based on their symmetry."],["Gems","Gemstones are minerals prized for their beauty, hardness and rarity. Diamond is crystallized carbon; rubies and sapphires are forms of the mineral corundum colored by traces of other elements; emeralds are a form of beryl colored by chromium or vanadium. The Mohs scale, devised in 1812, ranks minerals from 1, talc, to 10, diamond, by hardness."],["X-ray crystallography","When X-rays pass through a crystal, they scatter into a pattern of spots that reveals how its atoms are arranged. In 1912 Max von Laue showed this effect, and William Henry Bragg and his son William Lawrence Bragg used it to work out crystal structures, winning the 1915 Nobel Prize in Physics. Lawrence was only 25, still among the youngest Nobel winners in science."]],
k:[["Definition","A solid with a regular repeating pattern of atoms"],["Crystal systems","Seven"],["Salt crystals","Cubic"],["Hardness scale","Mohs scale, 1 to 10"],["Largest known crystals","Gypsum, Naica, Mexico, up to about 12 m"]],
d:[["1611","Johannes Kepler writes about the six-sided snowflake"],["1812","Friedrich Mohs devises his hardness scale"],["1885","Wilson Bentley photographs a single snowflake"],["1912","Max von Laue discovers X-ray diffraction in crystals"],["1952","Rosalind Franklin’s team captures Photo 51 of DNA"],["2000","Giant crystals are found in Mexico’s Naica Cave"]],
s2:[["Dorothy Hodgkin","The British chemist Dorothy Crowfoot Hodgkin used X-ray crystallography to find the structures of important molecules, including penicillin in 1945, vitamin B12 and, after decades of work, insulin in 1969. Knowing these structures helped scientists make and improve medicines. She won the Nobel Prize in Chemistry in 1964."],["Photo 51 and DNA","In 1952 the X-ray crystallographer Rosalind Franklin and her student Raymond Gosling captured an X-ray image of DNA, known as Photo 51. Its X-shaped pattern showed that DNA has a helical structure. The image helped James Watson and Francis Crick build their model of the DNA double helix in 1953."],["The Cave of Crystals","In 2000 miners in the Naica mine in Chihuahua, Mexico, broke into a cave filled with giant crystals of gypsum, some about 12 meters long and weighing many metric tons. They grew slowly for hundreds of thousands of years in hot, mineral-rich water. The cave is extremely hot and humid, so explorers could stay inside only for short periods wearing special cooling suits."],["Crystals that keep time","Quartz crystals vibrate at a very steady rate when electricity is applied, a property called piezoelectricity. Most quartz watches use a tiny crystal that vibrates 32,768 times per second; a circuit counts the vibrations to keep time. Quartz crystals also keep time inside computers and phones."],["Silicon crystals","Computer chips are made from slices of extremely pure silicon crystal. Huge single crystals are grown by slowly pulling a seed crystal out of molten silicon, a method invented by the Polish chemist Jan Czochralski in 1916. The crystals are sliced into thin wafers on which billions of tiny transistors are built."],["Liquid crystals and quasicrystals","Liquid crystals flow like liquids but have some order, like crystals; they are used in LCD screens of watches, calculators and televisions. In 1982 the Israeli scientist Dan Shechtman discovered quasicrystals, which are ordered but never repeat exactly. Many scientists doubted him at first, but he won the 2011 Nobel Prize in Chemistry."]],
sp:["Grow your own crystals","You can grow crystals at home. Stir as much salt, sugar or alum as will dissolve into a jar of hot water, with an adult’s help. Hang a string in the jar and leave it somewhere undisturbed. Over several days, as the water cools and evaporates, crystals will grow on the string. Sugar crystals grown this way are called rock candy. The slower they grow, the larger and more perfect the crystals."],
g:[["Crystal","A solid with a regularly repeating arrangement of atoms"],["Lattice","The repeating pattern of points in a crystal"],["Piezoelectricity","Electricity produced when some crystals are squeezed"],["Crystallography","The study of crystal structures"]],
f:["Snowflakes are six-sided crystals of ice.","Salt crystals are shaped like cubes.","Quartz watches use vibrating crystals to keep time."],
fun:"The quartz crystal in a watch vibrates 32,768 times every second."});

;
