// Human Body articles, part 4: tuberculosis, dengue, HIV, pain, emotions, pregnancy and birth.
ADDART({id:"tb",t:"Tuberculosis",c:"Health and the Body",i:"🫁",w:"Tuberculosis",r:["lungs","antibiotics","vacc","microbe","pandemic"],
p:[["An ancient disease","Tuberculosis, or TB, is a disease caused by bacteria that usually attacks the lungs."],["Spread through the air","TB spreads when a person with the disease coughs or sneezes and others breathe in the germs."],["Curable","TB can be cured with a course of several medicines taken for months."]],
l:"Tuberculosis, often called TB, is an infectious disease caused by the bacterium Mycobacterium tuberculosis. It usually affects the lungs but can harm other parts of the body. TB has troubled humans for thousands of years and is still one of the world’s deadliest infectious diseases: in 2024 about 10.7 million people fell ill with it and about 1.2 million died, according to the World Health Organization. Yet TB is preventable and curable, and countries including the Philippines run large programs to find and treat it.",
s:[["How TB spreads","When someone with active TB in the lungs coughs, sneezes, speaks or sings, tiny droplets carrying bacteria float in the air. People nearby may breathe them in. Not everyone infected becomes sick: the immune system often walls the bacteria off, a state called latent TB. About a quarter of the world’s people are thought to carry latent TB, which may become active later, especially if the immune system weakens."],["Symptoms","Active TB of the lungs causes a cough lasting more than two weeks, sometimes with blood, along with chest pain, fever, night sweats, tiredness and weight loss. Because it once seemed to waste people away, TB was called consumption. Doctors diagnose it with chest X-rays, tests of coughed-up mucus called sputum and quick molecular tests."],["Treatment","TB is treated with a combination of antibiotics, usually taken every day for about six months. Taking every dose is important; stopping early can let the bacteria survive and become resistant. Health workers often support patients through treatment so they can finish it."],["Prevention","The BCG vaccine, first used in 1921, protects babies and young children against severe forms of TB and is given at birth in many countries, including the Philippines. Good ventilation, covering coughs, finding and treating cases early and treating latent infection in people at high risk all help stop TB from spreading."]],
k:[["Cause","Mycobacterium tuberculosis bacteria"],["New cases (2024)","About 10.7 million"],["Deaths (2024)","About 1.2 million"],["Vaccine","BCG, first used 1921"],["World TB Day","24 March"]],
d:[["c. 3000 BCE","Signs of TB are found in Egyptian mummies"],["1882","Robert Koch identifies the TB bacterium"],["1921","The BCG vaccine is first given"],["1943","Streptomycin, the first effective TB antibiotic, is discovered"],["1993","WHO declares TB a global emergency"],["2023","The UN holds a high-level meeting on ending TB"]],
s2:[["Robert Koch","On 24 March 1882, the German doctor Robert Koch announced that he had found the bacterium that causes TB. At the time, TB caused about one in seven deaths in Europe. His discovery proved that TB is an infection, not inherited, and it earned him the Nobel Prize in 1905. World TB Day is held on the anniversary of his announcement."],["Sanatoriums","Before antibiotics, there was no cure for TB. Many patients were sent to sanatoriums, hospitals in the mountains or countryside, where they rested and breathed fresh air. Some recovered, but many did not. The Philippine president Manuel L. Quezon died of TB in 1944 while in exile in the United States during World War II."],["Antibiotics change everything","In 1943 scientists in the laboratory of Selman Waksman in the United States discovered streptomycin, the first antibiotic that worked against TB. Other medicines followed, and doctors learned that using several together prevented resistance. Deaths from TB fell sharply in many countries."],["Drug-resistant TB","When TB bacteria survive incomplete treatment, they can become resistant to the main medicines. Multidrug-resistant TB is harder and longer to treat. Newer medicines and shorter treatment plans developed in the 2010s and 2020s have improved cure rates, but drug-resistant TB remains a serious challenge."],["TB around the world","Most TB cases occur in Asia and Africa. The countries with the most cases include India, Indonesia, China, the Philippines and Pakistan. TB is closely linked to poverty, crowded housing, malnutrition and HIV, which weakens the immune system. Many people with TB are never diagnosed, so finding missing cases is a major goal."],["Ending TB","The United Nations has set a goal of ending the TB epidemic by 2030. This requires faster diagnosis, shorter treatments, support for patients and better vaccines. Several new TB vaccines are being tested in clinical trials, which could protect adults and teenagers better than BCG."]],
sp:["Ancient bones tell a story","Scientists have found signs of TB in the spines of Egyptian mummies thousands of years old and in skeletons from ancient Peru, showing that TB existed in the Americas before Europeans arrived. By studying the DNA of TB bacteria from ancient remains, researchers are learning how the disease spread around the world with human migrations."],
g:[["Bacterium","A tiny single-celled organism; some cause disease"],["Latent TB","Infection without symptoms that can become active later"],["BCG","A vaccine that protects children against severe TB"],["Drug resistance","When germs survive the medicines meant to kill them"]],
f:["TB is caused by bacteria, not a virus.","TB is treated with several medicines for about six months.","World TB Day is on 24 March."],
fun:"Signs of tuberculosis have been found in Egyptian mummies thousands of years old."});

ADDART({id:"dengue",t:"Dengue",c:"Health and the Body",i:"🦟",w:"Dengue fever",r:["malaria","microbe","vacc","pandemic","immune"],
p:[["Spread by mosquitoes","Dengue is a disease caused by a virus spread by the bites of Aedes mosquitoes."],["Common in the tropics","It is common in warm, rainy parts of Asia, Africa and the Americas, including the Philippines."],["Prevention starts at home","Removing standing water where mosquitoes breed helps prevent dengue."]],
l:"Dengue is a viral infection spread to people by the bite of infected Aedes mosquitoes, mainly Aedes aegypti. It is the most common mosquito-borne viral disease in the world, found in more than 100 countries in tropical and subtropical regions. Most infections are mild or cause no symptoms, but some people develop severe dengue, which can be life-threatening. Cases have risen sharply in recent decades, with a record number reported in 2024. There is no specific cure, but early care greatly reduces deaths.",
s:[["The virus and the mosquito","Dengue is caused by four closely related types of dengue virus. Aedes aegypti mosquitoes, with white markings on their legs, bite mostly during the day, especially in the early morning and late afternoon. They breed in small pools of clean, still water, in flower pots, old tires, buckets, gutters and water containers around homes."],["Symptoms","Symptoms usually begin four to ten days after a bite. They include high fever, severe headache, pain behind the eyes, muscle and joint pain, nausea and rash. The aches can be so intense that dengue was once called breakbone fever. Most people recover within about a week."],["Severe dengue","A small share of patients develop severe dengue, usually just as the fever goes down. Warning signs include severe stomach pain, repeated vomiting, bleeding from the gums or nose, blood in vomit and extreme tiredness or restlessness. Severe dengue needs urgent hospital care. A second infection with a different dengue type raises the risk of severe illness."],["Treatment","There is no medicine that kills the dengue virus. Patients rest, drink plenty of fluids and take paracetamol for fever and pain. Doctors usually advise avoiding aspirin and ibuprofen, which can increase bleeding. In hospital, careful fluid treatment saves the lives of most patients with severe dengue."]],
k:[["Cause","Dengue virus, four types"],["Spread by","Aedes mosquitoes, mainly Aedes aegypti"],["Countries affected","More than 100"],["Old name","Breakbone fever"],["ASEAN Dengue Day","15 June"]],
d:[["1779–1780","Epidemics matching dengue occur in Asia, Africa and the Americas"],["1906","Mosquitoes are shown to spread dengue"],["1950s","Severe dengue outbreaks are recorded in the Philippines and Thailand"],["2011","ASEAN Dengue Day is first observed"],["2019","The Philippines declares a national dengue epidemic"],["2024","A record number of dengue cases is reported worldwide"]],
s2:[["A growing problem","Dengue cases reported to the World Health Organization have increased many times over since 2000, and about half the world’s population now lives in areas at risk. Reasons include rapid growth of cities, travel, poor water storage and waste disposal, and warmer temperatures that help mosquitoes spread to new areas."],["Dengue in the Philippines","Dengue is a major health concern in the Philippines, with cases rising in the rainy season from about June to November. In 2019 the government declared a national dengue epidemic. Health campaigns encourage the 4S strategy: search and destroy breeding sites, use self-protection, seek early consultation and support community action."],["Vaccines","Dengue vaccines are tricky to develop because they must protect against all four virus types. The first vaccine, Dengvaxia, was found to raise the risk of severe disease in people never previously infected, and in 2017 the Philippines stopped its school vaccination program, sparking controversy and a fall in public trust in vaccines. A newer vaccine, TAK-003 (Qdenga), was recommended by WHO in 2023 for some age groups in high-risk areas."],["Bacteria against dengue","Scientists in the World Mosquito Program release Aedes mosquitoes carrying natural bacteria called Wolbachia, which make it harder for the mosquitoes to spread dengue. Releases in cities such as Yogyakarta in Indonesia have greatly reduced dengue cases in trials."],["Stopping mosquitoes at home","The best protection is preventing mosquitoes from breeding and biting. People can cover water containers, empty and scrub vases and buckets weekly, clear gutters, dispose of old tires and cans, use window screens and bed nets, wear long sleeves and use insect repellent. Community clean-up drives help protect whole neighborhoods."],["Related diseases","The same Aedes mosquitoes can spread other viruses, including chikungunya, Zika and yellow fever. Controlling these mosquitoes helps protect against all of them."]],
sp:["Mosquito eggs that wait","Aedes aegypti lays its eggs on the inside walls of containers just above the water line. The eggs can survive dry for many months. When rain or fresh water covers them, they hatch, and within about a week new adult mosquitoes can emerge. This is why scrubbing the inside of containers, not just emptying them, is important to stop dengue."],
g:[["Virus","A tiny germ that can only multiply inside living cells"],["Aedes aegypti","The main mosquito that spreads dengue"],["Severe dengue","A dangerous form of dengue with bleeding or shock"],["Breeding site","A place where mosquitoes lay eggs"]],
f:["Dengue mosquitoes bite mostly during the day.","There are four types of dengue virus.","Mosquito eggs can survive dry for months."],
fun:"Dengue was once called breakbone fever because of its painful aches."});

ADDART({id:"hiv",t:"HIV and AIDS",c:"Health and the Body",i:"🎗️",w:"HIV/AIDS",r:["immune","microbe","pandemic","blood","vacc"],
p:[["A virus that attacks defenses","HIV is a virus that damages the immune system, the body’s defense against infections."],["AIDS","Without treatment, HIV can lead to AIDS, when the immune system becomes very weak."],["Treatable","Modern medicines let people with HIV live long, healthy lives and prevent passing it on."]],
l:"HIV, the human immunodeficiency virus, attacks the immune system, especially white blood cells called CD4 cells that help fight infections. Without treatment, HIV gradually weakens the immune system and can lead to AIDS, acquired immunodeficiency syndrome, in which the body cannot fight off infections and certain cancers. Since the 1980s, more than 40 million people have died of AIDS-related illnesses. Today there is no cure, but daily medicines control the virus so well that people living with HIV can expect a near-normal lifespan.",
s:[["How HIV spreads","HIV is passed from one person to another through certain body fluids: blood, sexual fluids and breast milk. It can spread through sexual contact, sharing needles or from a mother to her baby during pregnancy, birth or breastfeeding. It is not spread by hugging, shaking hands, sharing cups, toilets or swimming pools, or by mosquitoes."],["What HIV does","HIV enters CD4 cells and uses them to make copies of itself, destroying them in the process. Over years, the number of CD4 cells falls. When it drops very low, or certain serious infections appear, a person is said to have AIDS. Many people with HIV feel well for years, so testing is the only way to know."],["Treatment","Antiretroviral therapy, a combination of medicines, stops HIV from multiplying. Taken every day, it keeps the virus at very low levels and lets the immune system recover. When the virus is so low that tests cannot detect it, it cannot be passed on through sex, a message summed up as undetectable equals untransmittable, or U=U."],["Prevention","HIV can be prevented by using condoms, never sharing needles and testing regularly. People at high risk can take PrEP, medicine that prevents infection. Treating pregnant women with HIV can almost always prevent the virus from passing to their babies. Donated blood is screened for HIV."]],
k:[["Virus","Human immunodeficiency virus"],["Living with HIV (2024)","About 40 million people"],["Deaths since the 1980s","More than 40 million"],["Treatment","Antiretroviral therapy"],["World AIDS Day","1 December"]],
d:[["1981","Doctors in the US report the first cases of what became known as AIDS"],["1983","Scientists at the Pasteur Institute identify the virus"],["1987","AZT becomes the first approved HIV medicine"],["1988","The first World AIDS Day is held"],["1996","Combination therapy transforms HIV treatment"],["2025","A twice-yearly injection to prevent HIV is approved in the US"]],
s2:[["Discovering the virus","In 1981 doctors noticed rare infections and cancers in previously healthy young men in the United States. In 1983 Françoise Barré-Sinoussi and Luc Montagnier at the Pasteur Institute in Paris identified the virus that causes AIDS; they won the Nobel Prize in 2008. HIV is thought to have crossed into humans from chimpanzees in Central Africa in the early 1900s."],["A global epidemic","HIV spread worldwide in the 1980s and 1990s. Africa south of the Sahara has been hit hardest and still has about two-thirds of the people living with HIV. By the late 1990s, AIDS was a leading cause of death in many African countries, leaving millions of children orphaned."],["From death sentence to manageable condition","In the 1980s an HIV diagnosis usually meant death within years. The first medicine, AZT, came in 1987, and in 1996 combination therapy proved that HIV could be controlled. Programs such as the Global Fund, created in 2002, and others helped bring treatment to millions in poorer countries, and AIDS-related deaths have fallen by more than half since their peak."],["HIV in the Philippines","While HIV infections fell in many countries, the Philippines has had one of the fastest-growing HIV epidemics in the Asia-Pacific region, especially among young people. The Philippine HIV and AIDS Policy Act of 2018 expanded free testing and treatment and allowed young people aged 15 and older to get tested."],["Fighting stigma","People with HIV have often faced fear, discrimination and rejection, which discourage testing and treatment. The red ribbon, created in 1991, became a symbol of awareness and support. Health workers stress that people with HIV can work, study, play sports and have families, and that treatment protects others."],["Toward a cure and vaccine","Scientists have not yet created an HIV vaccine, because the virus changes rapidly. A handful of people have been cured after bone marrow transplants for cancer from donors with a rare genetic resistance, but this risky method cannot be used widely. In 2025 a long-acting injection given twice a year was approved in the United States to prevent HIV."]],
sp:["U=U","In the 2010s, large studies following thousands of couples found that people with HIV whose treatment kept the virus undetectable did not pass HIV to their partners through sex. This led health organizations to endorse the message Undetectable = Untransmittable. It encourages people to get tested and stay on treatment, and helps reduce fear and stigma."],
g:[["Immune system","The body’s defense against germs"],["CD4 cell","A white blood cell that HIV attacks"],["Antiretroviral","A medicine that stops HIV from multiplying"],["Stigma","Unfair negative beliefs about a group of people"]],
f:["HIV is not spread by hugging or sharing cups.","Daily medicines can keep HIV under control.","World AIDS Day is on 1 December."],
fun:"The red ribbon, a worldwide symbol of HIV awareness, was created in 1991."});

ADDART({id:"pain",t:"Pain",c:"Health and the Body",i:"🤕",w:"Pain",r:["nervous","brain","surgery","skin"],
p:[["A warning signal","Pain is the body’s alarm system, warning us of injury or danger."],["Made in the brain","Nerves send signals, but the feeling of pain is created by the brain."],["Many treatments","Medicines, rest, exercise and other methods can relieve pain."]],
l:"Pain is an unpleasant feeling that warns of actual or possible damage to the body. When we touch something hot or cut a finger, special nerve endings send signals through the spinal cord to the brain, which produces the feeling of pain and makes us pull away or protect the injury. Pain can be short-lived, called acute pain, or last for months or years, called chronic pain. It is shaped not only by injury but also by emotions, attention and past experiences.",
s:[["How pain works","The skin, muscles, joints and organs contain nerve endings called nociceptors, which respond to harmful heat, cold, pressure or chemicals. They send electrical signals along nerves to the spinal cord and up to the brain. Reflexes can make you jerk your hand away from a flame before you even feel the pain."],["Acute and chronic pain","Acute pain comes on suddenly, from injuries, burns or surgery, and fades as the body heals. Chronic pain lasts longer than about three months and may continue after healing, as with back pain, arthritis or migraines. Chronic pain affects a large share of adults worldwide and can make work, sleep and daily life difficult."],["The brain and pain","The brain decides how much something hurts. Fear and worry can make pain feel worse, while distraction, laughter and feeling safe can make it feel milder. Athletes and soldiers sometimes do not notice serious injuries until later. In the 1960s, the gate control theory proposed that signals in the spinal cord can block pain signals, which is one reason rubbing a bumped elbow helps."],["Relieving pain","Medicines such as paracetamol, ibuprofen and aspirin relieve mild to moderate pain. Strong painkillers called opioids, such as morphine, treat severe pain but can be addictive and must be used carefully. Rest, ice or heat, exercise, physical therapy and mental techniques such as relaxation also help."]],
k:[["Pain sensors","Nociceptors"],["Chronic pain","Lasting more than about three months"],["First anesthetic surgery","1840s"],["Aspirin first sold","1899"],["Nobel Prize for heat and touch sensors","2021"]],
d:[["c. 400 BCE","Hippocrates recommends willow bark for pain"],["1804","Friedrich Sertürner isolates morphine from opium"],["1846","Ether anesthesia is demonstrated in Boston"],["1899","Aspirin goes on sale"],["1965","Ronald Melzack and Patrick Wall publish the gate control theory"],["2021","David Julius and Ardem Patapoutian win the Nobel Prize for discovering sensors for heat and touch"]],
s2:[["People who feel no pain","A few people are born unable to feel pain, a rare condition caused by changes in certain genes. It might sound lucky, but it is dangerous: they may break bones, burn themselves or bite their tongues without noticing. Their experiences show how important pain is for protecting the body."],["Phantom pain","Many people who have lost an arm or leg still feel it, and some feel pain in the missing limb, called phantom limb pain. In the 1990s the neuroscientist V. S. Ramachandran used a simple mirror box to trick the brain into seeing the missing limb move, which relieved pain for some patients."],["Why chili peppers feel hot","Capsaicin, the chemical in chili peppers, activates the same nerve sensors that detect dangerous heat, so the brain feels burning even though no harm is done. The discovery of this sensor, called TRPV1, by David Julius helped explain how we sense temperature and pain, and earned a share of the 2021 Nobel Prize."],["Referred pain","Sometimes pain is felt in a different place from its cause. During a heart attack, pain may be felt in the left arm, jaw or back, because nerves from these areas share pathways in the spinal cord. Doctors use knowledge of referred pain to diagnose problems."],["Painkillers and their risks","Opioid painkillers are powerful but can cause addiction and dangerous breathing problems. In some countries, overuse of opioids has caused a crisis of addiction and overdose deaths. At the same time, many people in poorer countries cannot get enough pain relief, even for cancer. Doctors aim to balance relief with safety."],["The placebo effect","When people are given a pill with no active medicine but believe it will help, their pain often decreases. This placebo effect is real: the brain releases its own natural painkillers, called endorphins. Scientists test new medicines against placebos to find out whether they truly work."]],
sp:["Willow bark to aspirin","For thousands of years, people in Egypt, Greece and elsewhere chewed willow bark or drank willow tea to ease pain and fever. In the 1800s chemists found that its active substance was related to salicylic acid. In 1897 Felix Hoffmann at the German company Bayer made a gentler form, acetylsalicylic acid, sold as aspirin from 1899. It became one of the most widely used medicines in history."],
g:[["Nociceptor","A nerve ending that detects harmful stimuli"],["Chronic pain","Pain lasting longer than about three months"],["Opioid","A strong painkiller related to morphine"],["Endorphin","A natural painkiller made by the body"]],
f:["Pain is produced by the brain.","Some people are born unable to feel pain.","Aspirin comes from a substance found in willow bark."],
fun:"Chili peppers feel hot because they trigger the same nerve sensors as real heat."});

ADDART({id:"emotions",t:"Emotions",c:"Health and the Body",i:"😊",w:"Emotion",r:["brain","mental","hormone","memory","sleep"],
p:[["Feelings that move us","Emotions such as joy, fear, anger and sadness are reactions of the mind and body to what happens around us."],["Brain and body","Emotions involve the brain, hormones, heartbeat, breathing and facial expressions."],["Managing feelings","Understanding and managing emotions helps people stay healthy and get along with others."]],
l:"Emotions are complex reactions that involve feelings, thoughts, body changes and behavior. Joy, sadness, fear, anger, surprise and disgust are among the most familiar, but people experience many more, such as pride, shame, love, jealousy and hope. Emotions help us respond to danger, connect with others and make decisions. They are produced by networks in the brain and influenced by hormones, culture and personal experience. Learning to recognize and manage emotions is an important part of mental health.",
s:[["Emotions in the brain","Many brain areas work together to create emotions. The amygdala, a small almond-shaped structure, helps detect threats and triggers fear. The prefrontal cortex, behind the forehead, helps us think about and control emotional reactions. Chemical messengers such as dopamine, serotonin and oxytocin also shape how we feel."],["Emotions in the body","Emotions change the body. Fear makes the heart race, breathing quicken and muscles tense, preparing for action, the fight-or-flight response described by the American scientist Walter Cannon in 1915. Embarrassment can make cheeks blush, and sadness can bring tears. Long-term stress can affect sleep, digestion and the immune system."],["Facial expressions","Charles Darwin wrote about emotional expressions in humans and animals in 1872. In the 1960s and 1970s, the psychologist Paul Ekman found that people in many cultures, including an isolated community in Papua New Guinea, recognized some facial expressions such as happiness, fear and anger. Other researchers argue that emotions and their expressions vary more across cultures than this suggests."],["Managing emotions","Everyone feels strong emotions. Helpful ways to manage them include naming the feeling, taking slow breaths, going for a walk, talking with someone trusted, writing in a journal and getting enough sleep. Feelings are not good or bad in themselves, but how we act on them matters."]],
k:[["Key brain area for fear","Amygdala"],["Control center","Prefrontal cortex"],["Darwin’s book","The Expression of the Emotions in Man and Animals (1872)"],["Stress response","Fight or flight"],["Emotional intelligence","Concept proposed in 1990"]],
d:[["1872","Darwin publishes his book on emotional expression"],["1884","William James proposes that emotions follow body changes"],["1915","Walter Cannon describes the fight-or-flight response"],["1970s","Paul Ekman studies facial expressions across cultures"],["1990","Peter Salovey and John Mayer describe emotional intelligence"],["2015","The film Inside Out shows emotions as characters"]],
s2:[["Emotional intelligence","In 1990 the psychologists Peter Salovey and John Mayer described emotional intelligence: the ability to recognize, understand and manage our own emotions and to understand those of others. The idea became popular through a 1995 book by Daniel Goleman. Schools in many countries now teach social and emotional skills."],["Words for feelings","Languages have words for emotions that others lack. Filipino has kilig, the fluttery thrill of romance, and gigil, the urge to squeeze something unbearably cute. German has Schadenfreude, pleasure at someone else’s misfortune, and Japanese has amae, the comfort of depending on someone’s kindness. Learning such words can help people notice and describe their own feelings."],["Why we cry","Humans are the only animals known to cry tears from emotion. Emotional tears may signal to others that we need help or comfort, and many people feel calmer after crying. Tears from sadness, joy and laughter all come from the same glands that keep our eyes moist."],["Emotions and decisions","People once thought emotions got in the way of good thinking. But studies of patients with damage to emotional areas of the brain, described by the neuroscientist Antonio Damasio, found that they struggled to make even simple decisions. Emotions help us weigh what matters."],["Empathy","Empathy is the ability to understand and share the feelings of others. It helps people cooperate, make friends and care for each other. Babies begin showing simple signs of empathy, such as crying when other babies cry, and empathy develops through childhood."],["When emotions become overwhelming","Everyone feels sad, worried or angry at times, but when such feelings are very intense, last a long time or make daily life hard, they may be signs of conditions such as depression or anxiety. These are common and treatable, and talking to a parent, teacher, doctor or counselor is an important first step."]],
sp:["Inside Out","The 2015 animated film Inside Out, made with advice from psychologists including Paul Ekman and Dacher Keltner, shows the emotions of a girl named Riley as characters: Joy, Sadness, Fear, Anger and Disgust. The film’s message, that sadness is important too because it helps us get comfort and support, has been used by teachers and therapists to help children talk about feelings."],
g:[["Emotion","A reaction involving feelings, thoughts and body changes"],["Amygdala","A brain area involved in fear and emotion"],["Empathy","Understanding and sharing others’ feelings"],["Fight or flight","The body’s response to danger"]],
f:["The amygdala helps the brain detect danger.","Darwin wrote a book about emotions in 1872.","Filipino has special words such as kilig and gigil."],
fun:"The Filipino word gigil describes the urge to squeeze something extremely cute."});

ADDART({id:"pregnancy",t:"Pregnancy and Birth",c:"Health and the Body",i:"👶",w:"Pregnancy",r:["cell","hormone","mendel","nutri","vacc"],
p:[["A new life grows","Pregnancy is the time, about nine months, when a baby develops inside its mother’s womb."],["From one cell","Every person begins as a single fertilized cell that divides into trillions."],["Care for mother and baby","Good care before, during and after birth keeps mothers and babies healthy."]],
l:"Pregnancy is the period during which a baby, at first called an embryo and later a fetus, grows inside the uterus, or womb. It lasts about 40 weeks, counted from the start of the mother’s last menstrual period, and is divided into three trimesters. During this time, a single fertilized cell develops into a baby with a beating heart, a working brain and all its organs. Birth usually happens through labor, though sometimes by surgery. Good prenatal care helps protect the health of both mother and child.",
s:[["The beginning","A pregnancy begins when an egg cell from the mother is fertilized by a sperm cell from the father, forming one cell called a zygote, which carries genes from both parents. It divides again and again as it travels to the uterus and attaches to its wall. The developing baby is called an embryo for the first eight weeks after fertilization, and a fetus after that."],["How the baby grows","By about six weeks of pregnancy, a tiny heart can be seen beating on an ultrasound scan. By the end of the first trimester, the fetus has arms, legs, fingers and toes. In the second trimester it begins to move, kick and hear sounds. In the third trimester it grows quickly, its lungs mature and it usually turns head-down, ready for birth."],["Lifeline: the placenta","The placenta is an organ that grows in the uterus during pregnancy. Connected to the baby by the umbilical cord, it passes oxygen and nutrients from the mother’s blood to the baby and carries away waste. The baby floats in a sac of amniotic fluid, which cushions it and keeps it warm."],["Birth","Labor begins when muscles of the uterus start to tighten regularly, gradually opening the cervix. Then the mother pushes the baby out through the birth canal, followed by the placenta. Sometimes doctors deliver babies by caesarean section, an operation through the abdomen, when this is safer. Babies born before 37 weeks are called premature and may need special care."]],
k:[["Length","About 40 weeks"],["Stages","Three trimesters"],["Embryo becomes fetus","About 8 weeks after fertilization"],["Premature birth","Before 37 weeks"],["Apgar score","Newborn check, created 1952"]],
d:[["c. 1350 BCE","An Egyptian papyrus describes a pregnancy test using barley and wheat"],["1847","James Young Simpson uses chloroform during childbirth"],["1952","Virginia Apgar introduces her newborn score"],["1958","Ian Donald publishes the use of ultrasound in pregnancy"],["1978","Louise Brown is the first baby born through IVF"],["2000","The UN sets goals to reduce maternal deaths"]],
s2:[["Prenatal care","During pregnancy, health workers check the mother’s blood pressure, weight and blood, track the baby’s growth and give vaccines and supplements such as iron and folic acid. A healthy diet, rest and avoiding smoking, alcohol and unsafe medicines help the baby develop well. Regular checkups help find problems early."],["Ultrasound","Ultrasound scans use high-frequency sound waves to create pictures of the baby inside the womb. In 1958 the Scottish doctor Ian Donald published research showing how ultrasound could be used in pregnancy. It is safe, painless and lets doctors check the baby’s growth, position and heartbeat."],["The Apgar score","In 1952 the American anesthesiologist Virginia Apgar created a quick test to check newborns one and five minutes after birth. It scores the baby’s color, heart rate, reflexes, muscle tone and breathing from 0 to 2 each. The Apgar score is now used in hospitals around the world."],["Midwives","Midwives are trained health workers who care for women during pregnancy, birth and afterward. In many countries, including the Philippines, traditional birth attendants called hilot once helped most births in villages. Today health programs encourage giving birth in health facilities with skilled attendants, which greatly reduces deaths."],["Safer motherhood","Every year, hundreds of thousands of women die from causes related to pregnancy and childbirth, mostly in low-income countries, and most of these deaths are preventable with skilled care. Maternal deaths worldwide have fallen substantially since 2000, but reducing them further remains a major global health goal."],["Twins and multiples","Sometimes more than one baby grows at once. Identical twins form when one fertilized egg splits in two, so they share the same genes. Fraternal twins develop from two separate eggs and are no more alike than other brothers and sisters. Twin births are more common in some populations, such as in parts of West Africa."]],
sp:["The first IVF baby","On 25 July 1978, Louise Brown was born in Oldham, England, the first person conceived through in vitro fertilization, IVF, in which an egg is fertilized by sperm in a laboratory dish and then placed in the mother’s uterus. The scientists Robert Edwards and Patrick Steptoe developed the method; Edwards won the Nobel Prize in 2010. More than ten million babies have since been born through IVF."],
g:[["Embryo","A baby in the earliest stage of development"],["Fetus","A developing baby from about eight weeks after fertilization until birth"],["Placenta","The organ that nourishes a baby in the womb"],["Trimester","One of three stages of pregnancy, each about three months long"]],
f:["Pregnancy lasts about 40 weeks.","Every person begins as a single cell.","Identical twins share the same genes."],
fun:"More than ten million babies have been born through IVF since 1978."});

;
