// Technology articles, part 3: bicycles, submarines, solar power, hydroelectric power and refrigeration.
ADDART({id:"bicycle",t:"The Bicycle",c:"Technology",i:"🚲",w:"Bicycle",r:["wheel","cars","rail","jeepney","flight"],
p:[["Two wheels and pedals","A bicycle is a human-powered vehicle with two wheels, moved by pushing pedals."],["Efficient travel","Cycling is one of the most energy-efficient ways to travel ever invented."],["Around the world","There are about a billion bicycles in the world, used for transport, work, sport and fun."]],
l:"A bicycle is a vehicle with two wheels, one behind the other, powered by a rider pushing pedals that turn the back wheel through a chain. Invented in Europe in the 1800s, the bicycle changed daily life by giving ordinary people a cheap, fast way to travel. Today there are roughly a billion bicycles worldwide. They carry people to work and school, deliver goods, race in great events such as the Tour de France and help cities cut traffic and pollution.",
s:[["The running machine","In 1817 the German inventor Karl Drais built a wooden two-wheeled machine with no pedals. Riders sat on it and pushed along the ground with their feet. Called the running machine or dandy horse, it was a fashionable craze for a short time and proved that a person could balance on two wheels in a line."],["Pedals and penny-farthings","In the 1860s French makers added pedals to the front wheel, creating the velocipede, nicknamed the boneshaker because of its rough ride. To go faster, builders made front wheels bigger and bigger, leading in the 1870s to the high-wheeled penny-farthing, which was fast but dangerous to fall from."],["The safety bicycle","In 1885 the English inventor John Kemp Starley built the Rover safety bicycle, with two wheels of similar size and a chain driving the rear wheel. In 1888 John Boyd Dunlop developed a practical air-filled tire. These inventions created the basic design of bicycles still used today and set off a worldwide cycling boom."],["How a bicycle works","When a rider pushes the pedals, a toothed wheel called the chainring turns a chain, which turns a smaller cog on the rear wheel. Gears let riders change how hard they must pedal for hills or flat roads. Brakes press pads against the wheel rims or discs. Staying upright depends on steering and forward motion."]],
k:[["Bicycles worldwide","About 1 billion"],["First two-wheeler","Karl Drais, 1817"],["Safety bicycle","John Kemp Starley, 1885"],["Air-filled tire","John Boyd Dunlop, 1888"],["World Bicycle Day","3 June"]],
d:[["1817","Karl Drais builds the running machine"],["1860s","Pedal velocipedes appear in France"],["1885","The Rover safety bicycle is introduced"],["1888","Dunlop develops the air-filled tire"],["1903","The first Tour de France is held"],["2018","The UN declares 3 June World Bicycle Day"]],
s2:[["Freedom on wheels","In the 1890s the bicycle gave many women new independence, letting them travel on their own and encouraging more practical clothing. The American campaigner Susan B. Anthony said in 1896 that the bicycle had done more to free women than anything else in the world."],["Bicycles at work","Around the world, bicycles carry goods and people. In the Philippines, pedicabs, called padyak or trisikad, are bicycles with sidecars that carry passengers on short trips. In many countries, delivery riders, postal workers and farmers depend on bicycles to earn a living."],["Cycling cities","Some cities are famous for cycling. In the Netherlands and Denmark, separate bike lanes make cycling safe, and in Amsterdam and Copenhagen a large share of trips are made by bicycle. Many cities, from Bogotá to Paris, have built bike lanes and bike-sharing systems to reduce traffic and pollution."],["Racing","Bicycle racing has been popular since the 1860s. The Tour de France, first held in 1903, is a three-week race of more than 3,000 kilometers through France and neighboring countries. Cycling has been part of the modern Olympic Games since 1896, including road, track, mountain bike and BMX events."],["From bicycles to airplanes","The Wright brothers, Wilbur and Orville, ran a bicycle shop in Dayton, Ohio. Their skills with light frames, chains and balance helped them build the first successful powered airplane in 1903. Early cars also borrowed bicycle parts, such as chains and air-filled tires."],["Electric bikes","Electric bicycles, or e-bikes, have a battery-powered motor that helps the rider pedal. They make longer trips and hills easier and have become very popular, especially in China and Europe. They also raise new questions about road safety and rules."]],
sp:["The most efficient machine","A person on a bicycle uses less energy to travel a kilometer than a person walking, and far less than any car, train or plane per person. Cycling turns the power of leg muscles into motion very efficiently because the wheels roll with little friction. That is why the bicycle is often called the most efficient means of transport ever invented."],
g:[["Chainring","The front toothed wheel turned by the pedals"],["Gear","A toothed wheel that changes pedaling effort"],["Velocipede","An early pedal-powered bicycle"],["Pedicab","A bicycle that carries passengers in a sidecar or cab"]],
f:["The first two-wheeler had no pedals.","The Tour de France began in 1903.","The Wright brothers were bicycle makers."],
fun:"The first bicycle, built in 1817, had no pedals at all."});

ADDART({id:"submarine",t:"Submarines",c:"Technology",i:"🚢",w:"Submarine",r:["ships","pacific","nuclear","ww2"],
p:[["Ships that dive","A submarine is a vessel that can travel underwater as well as on the surface."],["Sinking and rising","Submarines dive and surface by filling tanks with water or air."],["Exploring the deep","Small submarines called submersibles explore the deepest parts of the ocean."]],
l:"A submarine is a watercraft able to operate independently underwater. Inventors dreamed of underwater boats for centuries, but practical submarines appeared only in the late 1800s. Military submarines played major roles in both world wars and the Cold War, and today nuclear-powered submarines can stay submerged for months. Smaller research submersibles have carried people to the deepest point in the ocean, nearly 11 kilometers down, revealing strange life and landscapes in the deep sea.",
s:[["How submarines dive","Submarines have ballast tanks that can be filled with water or air. To dive, they let water in, becoming heavier than the water around them. To rise, they blow compressed air into the tanks, pushing the water out. Small fins called diving planes help steer the submarine up and down, like wings."],["Early submarines","In the 1620s the Dutch inventor Cornelis Drebbel built a rowed underwater boat that traveled beneath the River Thames in England. During the American Revolution, the one-person Turtle tried to attach a bomb to a British ship in 1776 but failed. In 1864 the H. L. Hunley became the first submarine to sink an enemy warship, though it sank too."],["Submarines at war","German U-boats sank many ships in both world wars, trying to cut off supplies to Britain. American submarines sank much of Japan’s merchant fleet in the Pacific during World War II. After the war, submarines carrying nuclear missiles became a key part of Cold War strategy."],["Life on board","Submarine crews live in tight spaces for weeks or months, sharing small bunks and working in shifts. Machines make fresh water from seawater and produce oxygen. Because there is no daylight, lighting changes to mark day and night. Sonar, which uses sound, helps submarines detect objects in the dark sea."]],
k:[["Early underwater boat","Cornelis Drebbel, 1620s"],["First nuclear submarine","USS Nautilus, 1954"],["Deepest dive","Challenger Deep, about 10,900 m"],["Diving method","Ballast tanks"],["Underwater navigation","Sonar"]],
d:[["1620s","Drebbel’s underwater boat travels beneath the Thames"],["1776","The Turtle attacks a British warship"],["1864","The H. L. Hunley sinks a warship"],["1954","USS Nautilus, the first nuclear submarine, is launched"],["1960","Trieste reaches the bottom of the Challenger Deep"],["2012","James Cameron makes a solo dive to the Challenger Deep"]],
s2:[["Nuclear submarines","In 1954 the United States launched USS Nautilus, the first nuclear-powered submarine. A nuclear reactor provides power for years without refueling and needs no air, so such submarines can stay underwater for months. In 1958 Nautilus became the first vessel to travel under the ice to the North Pole. Several countries now operate nuclear submarines."],["The deepest dive","On 23 January 1960, the Swiss engineer Jacques Piccard and US Navy officer Don Walsh descended in the bathyscaphe Trieste to the bottom of the Challenger Deep in the Mariana Trench, about 10,900 meters down. In 2012 the filmmaker James Cameron reached it alone, and in 2019 the explorer Victor Vescovo dived to the deepest points of all five oceans."],["Alvin","The research submersible Alvin, first launched in 1964, has made thousands of dives. In 1977 scientists aboard Alvin discovered hydrothermal vents on the seafloor near the Galápagos Islands, surrounded by giant tube worms and other life that depends on chemical energy rather than sunlight. In 1986 Alvin visited the wreck of the Titanic."],["Submarines and submersibles","A submarine can travel on its own for long periods. A submersible is a smaller craft, often supported by a ship at the surface, used for research, filming or rescue. Remotely operated vehicles, robots without people aboard, now do much deep-sea exploration more safely and cheaply."],["Dangers of the deep","The deep sea is extremely dangerous: at 4,000 meters, the water pressure is about 400 times that at the surface. Submarines must be built to strict standards. In 2023 the private submersible Titan imploded during a dive to the Titanic wreck, killing all five people aboard, a tragedy that led to calls for stronger safety rules."],["Submarines in stories","Jules Verne’s 1870 novel Twenty Thousand Leagues Under the Seas imagined the electric submarine Nautilus, commanded by the mysterious Captain Nemo, long before such vessels existed. The novel inspired many inventors, and the first nuclear submarine was named after Verne’s ship."]],
sp:["The Turtle","The Turtle, built by the American inventor David Bushnell in 1775, was a wooden, egg-shaped submarine just big enough for one person, who turned hand cranks to move it and pumped water to dive. In 1776, during the American Revolution, Sergeant Ezra Lee tried to attach an explosive to the British warship HMS Eagle in New York Harbor but could not fix it to the hull. It was the first recorded use of a submarine in war."],
g:[["Ballast tank","A tank filled with water or air to make a vessel sink or rise"],["Sonar","Using sound waves to detect objects underwater"],["Submersible","A small underwater craft, often supported by a ship"],["Periscope","A tube with mirrors for seeing above the surface"]],
f:["Submarines dive by filling tanks with water.","USS Nautilus was the first nuclear submarine.","People have reached the deepest point in the ocean."],
fun:"The first nuclear submarine was named after the submarine in a Jules Verne novel."});

ADDART({id:"solarpower",t:"Solar Power",c:"Technology",i:"☀️",w:"Solar power",r:["renew","sun","elec","battery","chip"],
p:[["Electricity from sunlight","Solar power turns sunlight into electricity using solar panels or concentrated sunlight."],["Clean and growing","Solar power produces no pollution while running and is the fastest-growing source of electricity."],["Cheaper than ever","The cost of solar panels has fallen dramatically since 2010."]],
l:"Solar power is energy from the Sun converted into electricity or heat. Most solar electricity comes from photovoltaic cells, usually made of silicon, which produce electricity when sunlight strikes them. Others use mirrors to concentrate sunlight and boil water to drive turbines. Solar power releases no greenhouse gases while operating and has become one of the cheapest ways to make electricity. Installations range from tiny panels on calculators and rooftops to vast solar farms covering many square kilometers.",
s:[["How solar cells work","A solar cell is made of layers of a semiconductor, usually silicon, treated so that one layer has extra electrons and the other has spaces for them. When light hits the cell, it knocks electrons loose, and they flow through a circuit as electric current. This is called the photovoltaic effect. Many cells are joined to make a panel."],["The history of solar cells","In 1839 the French physicist Edmond Becquerel discovered that light could create electricity in certain materials. In 1954 scientists at Bell Laboratories in the United States made the first practical silicon solar cell, converting about 6 percent of sunlight into electricity. In 1958 the satellite Vanguard 1 became the first to use solar power."],["Concentrated solar power","Concentrated solar power plants use mirrors to focus sunlight onto a receiver, heating a fluid that makes steam to drive turbines. Some store heat in molten salt, so they can keep making electricity after sunset. The Noor complex in Ouarzazate, Morocco, is one of the largest such plants."],["The solar boom","Since 2010, the cost of solar panels has fallen by about 90 percent, thanks to mass production, mostly in China, and better technology. Solar has become the world’s fastest-growing source of new electricity, and global solar capacity passed 2 terawatts in 2024. China, the European Union, the United States and India are among the largest users."]],
k:[["Main technology","Photovoltaic (PV) cells"],["Common material","Silicon"],["First practical cell","Bell Labs, 1954"],["First solar satellite","Vanguard 1, 1958"],["Global capacity","More than 2 terawatts (2024)"]],
d:[["1839","Edmond Becquerel discovers the photovoltaic effect"],["1954","Bell Labs makes the first practical silicon solar cell"],["1958","Vanguard 1 uses solar cells in space"],["2008","The Philippines passes its Renewable Energy Act"],["2016","Solar Impulse 2 completes a round-the-world solar flight"],["2024","Global solar capacity passes 2 terawatts"]],
s2:[["Rooftop solar","Millions of homes, schools and businesses have solar panels on their roofs. In many places, owners can sell extra electricity to the power grid, a system called net metering. Rooftop solar lets people make their own electricity and lowers their bills, especially in sunny countries."],["Solar in the Philippines","The Philippines, with strong tropical sunshine, has been building more solar power under its Renewable Energy Act of 2008. Large solar farms have been built in Luzon and the Visayas, and projects such as Terra Solar in Nueva Ecija and Bulacan are planned among the largest solar and battery plants in the world. Small solar systems also bring electricity to remote island villages."],["Light for everyone","Hundreds of millions of people, mostly in Africa and parts of Asia, still lack electricity. Small solar home kits and solar lanterns provide light, phone charging and power for radios and fans, often replacing dangerous kerosene lamps. Solar mini-grids can power whole villages."],["When the Sun goes down","Solar panels produce no electricity at night and less on cloudy days. To provide steady power, solar is combined with batteries, other power sources and better electricity grids. Falling battery prices have made solar-plus-storage projects increasingly common."],["Flying on sunshine","Between 2015 and 2016, the airplane Solar Impulse 2, piloted by Bertrand Piccard and André Borschberg, flew around the world using only solar power, storing energy in batteries to fly through the night. The journey showed what clean energy could achieve, though solar airliners remain far off."],["Panels and the environment","Solar panels produce clean electricity for 25 years or more, but making them uses energy and materials, and old panels must be recycled. Large solar farms need land, so some are built over reservoirs, on rooftops and parking lots or combined with farming, called agrivoltaics."]],
sp:["Solar power in space","Almost all satellites and spacecraft, including the International Space Station, are powered by solar panels. The ISS has huge solar arrays covering an area larger than a basketball court. Spacecraft traveling to Jupiter, such as Juno, use extra-large panels because sunlight there is about 25 times weaker than at Earth. Missions beyond Jupiter usually rely on nuclear power instead."],
g:[["Photovoltaic","Producing electricity directly from light"],["Solar panel","A group of solar cells joined together"],["Net metering","Selling extra solar electricity back to the grid"],["Terawatt","One trillion watts"]],
f:["Solar cells are usually made of silicon.","The first practical solar cell was made in 1954.","Solar panels power the International Space Station."],
fun:"A solar-powered airplane flew all the way around the world in 2015 and 2016."});

ADDART({id:"hydropower",t:"Hydroelectric Power and Dams",c:"Technology",i:"💧",w:"Hydroelectricity",r:["renew","elec","bridges","wcycle","nile"],
p:[["Power from flowing water","Hydroelectric power uses the energy of falling or flowing water to make electricity."],["Dams and reservoirs","Most hydropower plants use dams that store water in large lakes called reservoirs."],["A major renewable source","Hydropower is one of the world’s largest sources of renewable electricity."]],
l:"Hydroelectric power, or hydropower, is electricity generated from moving water. Most plants use a dam to hold back a river, creating a reservoir. Water released from the reservoir flows through turbines, spinning them to drive generators. Hydropower has been used for more than a century and supplies a large share of the world’s electricity; countries such as Norway, Brazil and Paraguay get most of their power from it. Dams also provide water and flood control, but they can harm rivers and displace communities.",
s:[["From water wheels to turbines","People have used water wheels to grind grain for more than 2,000 years, in ancient Greece, Rome, China and the Islamic world. In the 1800s engineers developed efficient water turbines. In 1878 the English industrialist William Armstrong lit his house, Cragside, with hydroelectric power, and in 1882 the first commercial hydroelectric plant opened in Appleton, Wisconsin."],["How a hydropower plant works","Water stored high behind a dam has potential energy. When gates open, water rushes down through a large pipe called a penstock and spins the blades of a turbine. The turbine turns a generator, which produces electricity. The water then flows back into the river below. The higher the drop and the more water, the more electricity."],["Kinds of hydropower","Large dams store huge amounts of water. Run-of-river plants use a river’s natural flow with little or no storage. Pumped-storage plants pump water uphill to an upper reservoir when electricity is plentiful and release it to make power when demand is high, working like giant batteries. Small hydro plants can power villages in mountain areas."],["Benefits","Hydropower produces no air pollution while running, can be switched on quickly to meet demand and can last for many decades. Dams can also store water for farms and cities, control floods and create lakes for fishing and recreation."]],
k:[["Largest power station","Three Gorges Dam, China"],["Three Gorges capacity","About 22,500 megawatts"],["First commercial plant","Appleton, Wisconsin, 1882"],["Mostly hydro-powered countries","Norway, Brazil, Paraguay and others"],["Key part","Turbine"]],
d:[["c. 3rd century BCE","Water wheels are used in ancient Greece"],["1882","The first commercial hydroelectric plant opens"],["1895","Niagara Falls power plant begins sending AC electricity"],["1936","Hoover Dam is completed in the United States"],["1984","Itaipu Dam begins producing power"],["2012","Three Gorges Dam reaches full capacity"]],
s2:[["Three Gorges Dam","The Three Gorges Dam on the Yangtze River in China is the world’s largest power station, with a capacity of about 22,500 megawatts. Completed in stages, it reached full operation in 2012. It also helps control floods. But building it flooded towns and farmland and required more than a million people to move, and it changed the river’s ecology."],["Itaipu","The Itaipu Dam on the Paraná River is shared by Brazil and Paraguay. For years it produced more electricity annually than any other plant. It supplies most of Paraguay’s electricity and a significant share of Brazil’s, and the two countries share its output under a treaty signed in 1973."],["Dams in the Philippines","Dams are important for water and power in the Philippines. Angat Dam in Bulacan supplies most of Metro Manila’s water and also generates electricity. San Roque Dam in Pangasinan and Magat Dam in Isabela provide power and irrigation, and the Agus River plants in Mindanao, including those near Maria Cristina Falls, supply much of the island’s electricity."],["Problems with dams","Dams block fish such as salmon from swimming upstream to breed, trap sediment that would enrich farmland downstream and change water temperatures. Reservoirs can flood forests, farms and the homes of Indigenous peoples. In dry years, low water can reduce power output. Some old dams have been removed to restore rivers."],["Dam safety","Dams must be carefully built and maintained, because a failure can release catastrophic floods. In 1975 the failure of the Banqiao Dam in China during a typhoon caused one of the worst dam disasters in history. Today engineers monitor dams with sensors and inspections."],["Hydropower and climate","Hydropower helps reduce emissions from fossil fuels, and pumped storage helps balance wind and solar power. But droughts and changing rainfall linked to climate change can reduce water levels in reservoirs, affecting electricity supplies in some regions."]],
sp:["Lighting Niagara","In 1895 the Adams Power Plant at Niagara Falls began producing electricity using alternating current, the system championed by Nikola Tesla and George Westinghouse. In 1896 it began sending power about 40 kilometers to the city of Buffalo, New York. Its success helped make alternating current the standard for electricity around the world."],
g:[["Turbine","A machine with blades turned by flowing water, steam or air"],["Reservoir","An artificial lake that stores water"],["Penstock","A pipe carrying water to a turbine"],["Pumped storage","Storing energy by pumping water uphill"]],
f:["The Three Gorges Dam is the world’s largest power station.","Water wheels have been used for over 2,000 years.","Angat Dam supplies most of Metro Manila’s water."],
fun:"Pumped-storage hydropower plants work like giant water batteries."});

ADDART({id:"refrigeration",t:"Refrigeration and Air Conditioning",c:"Technology",i:"🧊",w:"Refrigeration",r:["thermo","elec","climchange","vacc","steam"],
p:[["Keeping things cool","Refrigerators and air conditioners move heat from one place to another to keep things cold."],["Fresh food and medicine","Refrigeration keeps food fresh, medicines safe and buildings comfortable."],["A cool revolution","Before refrigerators, people stored ice cut from frozen lakes or kept food in cool cellars."]],
l:"Refrigeration is the process of removing heat from a space or substance to make it colder. Refrigerators, freezers and air conditioners all work by pumping heat from inside to outside, using special fluids called refrigerants. Modern refrigeration, developed in the 1800s and spread widely in the 1900s, transformed how people eat, live and work. It keeps food from spoiling, allows vaccines to reach remote places and makes hot cities comfortable, but its energy use and chemicals also affect the environment.",
s:[["Before refrigerators","People have long found ways to keep things cool. Ancient Persians built yakhchāls, domed structures that stored ice through hot summers. Many cultures kept food in cool cellars, springs or caves. In the 1800s a large ice trade shipped blocks cut from frozen lakes in New England to cities as far away as the Caribbean and India."],["How a refrigerator works","A refrigerant fluid flows through pipes inside and outside the fridge. Inside, it evaporates, absorbing heat from the food, just as sweat cools your skin when it evaporates. A compressor then squeezes the gas, making it hot, and it releases that heat through coils at the back, turning back into a liquid. The cycle repeats, pumping heat out."],["Inventors of cold","In 1755 the Scottish professor William Cullen showed that evaporating a liquid could produce a little ice. In 1834 Jacob Perkins patented a vapor-compression machine. In the 1870s the German engineer Carl von Linde built efficient refrigeration machines for breweries. Electric home refrigerators became common in wealthy countries from the 1920s and 1930s."],["Air conditioning","In 1902 the American engineer Willis Carrier designed a system to control temperature and humidity in a printing plant in New York. Air conditioning spread to cinemas, offices and homes. It made it easier to live and work in hot places and changed architecture, as buildings with sealed windows and glass walls became possible."]],
k:[["Main parts","Compressor, condenser, evaporator"],["Working fluid","Refrigerant"],["First artificial cooling demo","William Cullen, 1755"],["Modern air conditioning","Willis Carrier, 1902"],["Ozone treaty","Montreal Protocol, 1987"]],
d:[["c. 400 BCE","Persians use yakhchāls to store ice"],["1755","William Cullen demonstrates artificial cooling"],["1834","Jacob Perkins patents a vapor-compression refrigerator"],["1902","Willis Carrier designs modern air conditioning"],["1987","The Montreal Protocol protects the ozone layer"],["2016","The Kigali Amendment limits HFC refrigerants"]],
s2:[["Frozen food","The American inventor Clarence Birdseye noticed that fish caught by Inuit people in the Canadian Arctic froze almost instantly and tasted fresh when thawed. In the 1920s he developed quick-freezing methods for food. Frozen foods became widespread after home freezers spread, changing how people shop and cook."],["The cold chain","A cold chain is a chain of refrigerated storage and transport that keeps food and medicines cold from where they are made to where they are used. Many vaccines must be kept between 2 and 8°C, and some at far colder temperatures. Solar-powered fridges help deliver vaccines to remote clinics without reliable electricity."],["Refrigerants and the ozone layer","In the 1930s refrigerators began using CFCs, chemicals that seemed safe and stable. In the 1970s and 1980s scientists discovered that CFCs were destroying the ozone layer, which shields Earth from harmful ultraviolet light. The Montreal Protocol of 1987, signed by every country in the world, phased them out, and the ozone layer is slowly recovering."],["Cooling and climate","Replacement chemicals called HFCs do not harm the ozone layer but are powerful greenhouse gases. The Kigali Amendment of 2016 commits countries to reduce them. Air conditioners also use large amounts of electricity, and demand is rising fast as temperatures and incomes grow, so engineers are developing more efficient machines and natural refrigerants."],["Cooling in hot countries","In tropical countries such as the Philippines, refrigeration is vital for preserving fish, meat and produce, and ice plants supply fishing fleets. Air conditioning is common in malls, offices and many homes. Energy-efficient appliances and building designs with shade and ventilation help reduce electricity use."],["Einstein’s fridge","In 1930 Albert Einstein and his former student Leo Szilard patented a refrigerator with no moving parts, inspired by news of a family killed by toxic gas leaking from their fridge. It was never widely produced, but engineers have since revisited the design."]],
sp:["The ice king","In 1806 the Boston merchant Frederic Tudor shipped a cargo of ice cut from frozen ponds to the Caribbean island of Martinique. Many people laughed, but Tudor learned to insulate ice with sawdust and built icehouses in hot ports. His company eventually shipped ice as far as Calcutta in India. He became known as the Ice King, and the ice trade boomed until mechanical refrigeration replaced it."],
g:[["Refrigerant","A fluid that carries heat in a cooling system"],["Compressor","A pump that squeezes refrigerant gas"],["Cold chain","Refrigerated storage and transport from start to finish"],["CFC","A chemical once used in fridges that damages the ozone layer"]],
f:["Refrigerators pump heat out rather than making cold.","Air conditioning was invented for a printing plant.","The Montreal Protocol protects the ozone layer."],
fun:"Albert Einstein co-invented a refrigerator with no moving parts."});

;
