// Arts and Culture articles, part 4: the circus, musical theatre, sculpture, calligraphy, drums and percussion.
ADDART({id:"circus",t:"The Circus",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"🎪",w:"Circus",r:["theatre","puppetry","ballet","elephant"],
p:[["Daring and delight","A circus is a traveling show of acrobats, clowns, jugglers, tightrope walkers and other performers."],["Born in a ring","The modern circus began in London in 1768, performed in a round ring."],["New forms","Today many circuses use only human performers and combine acrobatics with theater and music."]],
l:"A circus is a company of performers who present feats of skill, strength, balance and comedy, usually in a ring or under a big tent. Acrobats, aerialists, jugglers, tightrope walkers, clowns and, in the past, trained animals have thrilled audiences for centuries. The modern circus began in London in 1768, but acrobatic traditions are far older, especially in China. Since the 1970s, new circus companies such as Cirque du Soleil have blended acrobatics with theater, dance and music, often without animals.",
s:[["Ancient roots","The word circus comes from Latin for circle; in ancient Rome, the Circus Maximus was a huge arena for chariot races. Acrobats, jugglers and tumblers performed at fairs and courts in many lands. In China, acrobatic performances have a history of more than 2,000 years, featuring balancing, plate spinning and human pyramids."],["The first modern circus","In 1768 Philip Astley, a former cavalry soldier, began showing trick horse riding in London in a ring about 13 meters across, a size that let riders stand on galloping horses. He added clowns, acrobats and jugglers between the riding acts. The ring size he chose is still common today. Soon circuses spread across Europe and to the Americas."],["Circus acts","Classic circus acts include the flying trapeze, invented by the Frenchman Jules Léotard in 1859, whose name lives on in the leotard; the tightrope; juggling; contortion; trick cycling; and the human cannonball. Clowns provide comedy and keep the show moving. Many performers come from circus families who have trained for generations."],["The big top","In the 1800s American circuses began touring in huge canvas tents, called big tops, traveling by wagon and later by railroad. Showmen such as P. T. Barnum and the Ringling brothers created giant circuses with parades, menageries of animals and three rings with acts happening at once."]],
k:[["Modern circus founded","1768, London"],["Founder","Philip Astley"],["Standard ring","About 13 m across"],["Flying trapeze","Jules Léotard, 1859"],["Cirque du Soleil founded","1984, Quebec"]],
d:[["c. 200 BCE","Acrobatic shows flourish in Han dynasty China"],["1768","Philip Astley opens his riding show in London"],["1782","The Royal Circus gives the art its modern name"],["1859","Jules Léotard performs the first flying trapeze act"],["1984","Cirque du Soleil is founded in Canada"],["2017","Ringling Bros. and Barnum & Bailey Circus closes after 146 years"]],
s2:[["Chinese acrobatics","Chinese acrobatic troupes are famous for astonishing balance and flexibility, stacking chairs, spinning plates, diving through hoops and forming human towers. The town of Wuqiao in Hebei Province has been called a cradle of Chinese acrobatics, and its schools have trained performers for centuries."],["Clowns","Clowns have entertained audiences since ancient times. The English performer Joseph Grimaldi, in the early 1800s, created the classic white-faced clown, and clowns are still sometimes called Joeys in his honor. Different traditions include the elegant whiteface, the clumsy auguste and the sad tramp clown. Hospital clowns visit sick children to bring laughter."],["Animals in the circus","For a long time, circuses featured lions, tigers, elephants and bears performing tricks. Concerns grew about cruelty, cramped travel and unnatural behavior, and many countries now ban or restrict wild animals in circuses. Bolivia became one of the first to ban all circus animals in 2009. Many circuses now focus entirely on human skill."],["Contemporary circus","In the 1970s and 1980s, new circus companies in France, Canada and Australia began combining acrobatics with storytelling, dance, live music and dramatic lighting. Cirque du Soleil, founded in Quebec in 1984 by former street performers, became the most famous, with shows seen by hundreds of millions of people."],["Circus around the world","The Moscow State Circus became world famous for its skilled performers during the Soviet era. In Mongolia, contortionists continue a long tradition. In Ethiopia and Kenya, youth circus groups give young people skills and confidence. Many countries have circus schools that train professional performers."],["Training and safety","Circus artists train for years to master their skills, building strength, flexibility and balance. Safety nets, harnesses and spotters protect them while they learn. Circus skills such as juggling and balancing are now taught in schools and community programs because they build coordination, teamwork and confidence."]],
sp:["Jules Léotard","Jules Léotard was a French gymnast whose father ran a swimming pool in Toulouse, where he practiced swinging between ropes and bars over the water. In 1859, at the Cirque Napoléon in Paris, he performed the first flying trapeze act, leaping from one swinging bar to another. He became a star, and the song The Daring Young Man on the Flying Trapeze was written about him. The tight one-piece garment he wore is still called a leotard."],
g:[["Aerialist","A performer who does acts high in the air"],["Big top","The large tent of a traveling circus"],["Contortionist","A performer who bends the body into unusual positions"],["Ringmaster","The host who introduces the circus acts"]],
f:["The modern circus began in London in 1768.","The leotard is named after a trapeze artist.","Chinese acrobatics are over 2,000 years old."],
fun:"The leotard is named after Jules Léotard, the inventor of the flying trapeze act."});

ADDART({id:"musicals",t:"Musical Theatre",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"🎤",w:"Musical theatre",r:["opera","theatre","ballet","cinema","opm"],
p:[["Stories told in song","Musical theatre combines songs, spoken words, dance and acting to tell a story on stage."],["Broadway and the West End","New York’s Broadway and London’s West End are the most famous centers of musical theatre."],["Loved worldwide","Musicals are performed in many languages and countries, and many have become films."]],
l:"Musical theatre is a form of theater in which songs, music and dance are woven into the storytelling. Characters sing to express feelings that words alone cannot, and big dance numbers bring scenes to life. Musicals grew from opera, operetta, vaudeville and music hall in the 1800s and early 1900s and flourished on New York’s Broadway and in London’s West End. Shows such as West Side Story, Les Misérables and The Lion King have been performed around the world, and Filipino performers have become stars of the genre.",
s:[["Origins","Musical theatre has many ancestors. European operettas, light operas with spoken dialogue, were popular in the 1800s; in England, Gilbert and Sullivan wrote comic operettas such as The Pirates of Penzance. In America, variety shows called vaudeville, along with minstrel shows, burlesque and revues, featured songs, comedy and dance."],["The book musical","In 1927 Show Boat, by Jerome Kern and Oscar Hammerstein II, told a serious story about race and love along the Mississippi River, with songs that grew from the plot. In 1943 Rodgers and Hammerstein’s Oklahoma! fully combined story, song and dance, setting the model for the book musical, in which every element serves the story."],["The golden age","From the 1940s to the 1960s, Broadway produced classics such as South Pacific, The King and I, My Fair Lady, The Sound of Music and West Side Story, which retold Romeo and Juliet among rival gangs in New York with music by Leonard Bernstein and dance by Jerome Robbins. Many became popular films."],["Megamusicals","From the 1970s and 1980s, spectacular shows from London, often with sung-through scores, became global hits: Andrew Lloyd Webber’s Cats and The Phantom of the Opera, and Les Misérables and Miss Saigon by Claude-Michel Schönberg and Alain Boublil. They were staged in dozens of countries and languages."]],
k:[["Main centers","Broadway (New York), West End (London)"],["First modern book musical","Show Boat (1927)"],["Longest-running Broadway show","The Phantom of the Opera (1988–2023)"],["Top award (US)","Tony Award, since 1947"],["First Asian woman to win a Tony","Lea Salonga, 1991"]],
d:[["1879","Gilbert and Sullivan’s The Pirates of Penzance premieres"],["1927","Show Boat opens on Broadway"],["1943","Oklahoma! opens"],["1957","West Side Story opens"],["1989","Miss Saigon opens in London, starring Lea Salonga"],["2015","Hamilton opens on Broadway"]],
s2:[["Lea Salonga","The Filipino singer and actress Lea Salonga was chosen at 17 to play Kim in Miss Saigon in London in 1989. When the show moved to Broadway, she won the 1991 Tony Award for Best Actress in a Musical, the first Asian woman to win a Tony. She later played Éponine and Fantine in Les Misérables and was the singing voice of Disney’s Jasmine and Mulan."],["Filipinos on the world stage","The search for Miss Saigon’s cast in Manila launched the international careers of many Filipino performers, and Filipino actors have since appeared in musicals in London, New York, Asia and Australia. At home, companies such as Repertory Philippines, founded in 1967, Tanghalang Pilipino and the Philippine Educational Theater Association (PETA) stage musicals in English and Filipino. PETA’s Rak of Aegis, built on songs by the band Aegis, became a hit original musical."],["Musicals on film","When sound films arrived in 1927, film musicals quickly followed. Hollywood made classics such as The Wizard of Oz, Singin’ in the Rain and The Sound of Music. Animated musicals such as The Little Mermaid and Frozen are hugely popular. India’s film industry, including Bollywood, has long made films in which song and dance are central."],["Hamilton","In 2015 Lin-Manuel Miranda’s Hamilton told the story of the American founding father Alexander Hamilton using hip hop, rap, R&B and pop, with a cast mainly of actors of color. It became a cultural phenomenon, won 11 Tony Awards and the Pulitzer Prize for Drama, and brought many young people to musical theatre."],["Making a musical","A musical is created by a team: a composer writes the music, a lyricist writes the song words and a book writer writes the story and dialogue. A director, choreographer, designers and musicians bring it to the stage. Actors in musicals must be triple threats, able to sing, dance and act."],["Musicals worldwide","Musical theatre thrives beyond New York and London. South Korea, Japan and Germany have large musical industries, Japan’s all-female Takarazuka Revue has performed since 1914, and Chinese and Australian productions tour widely. Schools and community theaters everywhere stage musicals, giving young people a chance to perform."]],
sp:["The Phantom of the Opera","Andrew Lloyd Webber’s The Phantom of the Opera, based on a French novel by Gaston Leroux, tells of a masked musical genius haunting the Paris Opera House. It opened in London in 1986 and on Broadway in 1988, where it ran for 35 years and nearly 14,000 performances, the longest run in Broadway history, before closing in 2023. It has been seen by well over a hundred million people worldwide."],
g:[["Book musical","A musical in which songs and dances grow from the story"],["Lyricist","The writer of a song’s words"],["Choreographer","The person who creates dances"],["Sung-through","A musical with little or no spoken dialogue"]],
f:["Show Boat (1927) was an early modern musical.","Lea Salonga won a Tony Award in 1991.","The Phantom of the Opera ran 35 years on Broadway."],
fun:"Japan’s Takarazuka Revue, with an all-female cast, has performed musicals since 1914."});

ADDART({id:"sculpture",t:"Sculpture",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"🗿",w:"Sculpture",r:["michel","caveart","architecture","ukiyoe"],
p:[["Art in three dimensions","Sculpture is art made in three dimensions, by carving, modeling, casting or assembling materials."],["Among the oldest arts","The oldest known sculptures are carved figures about 40,000 years old."],["Every culture","From Egypt’s Sphinx to China’s Terracotta Army, sculpture appears in every civilization."]],
l:"Sculpture is the art of making three-dimensional forms, either freestanding figures that can be seen from all sides or reliefs that project from a flat background. Sculptors carve stone and wood, model clay and wax, cast metal and assemble found objects. Sculpture is one of humanity’s oldest arts, used to honor gods and rulers, remember the dead, decorate buildings and express ideas. Famous examples include the Great Sphinx, the Terracotta Army, the moai of Rapa Nui, Michelangelo’s David and modern works in public parks around the world.",
s:[["Techniques","Carving removes material from a block of stone or wood with chisels and other tools. Modeling builds up soft material such as clay or wax. Casting pours molten metal, often bronze, into a mold; the ancient lost-wax method used a wax model that melted away. Assemblage joins different materials and objects together."],["Ancient sculpture","Some of the oldest known sculptures, such as the Lion-man of Hohlenstein-Stadel in Germany, carved from mammoth ivory, are about 40,000 years old. Ancient Egyptians carved colossal statues of pharaohs and the Great Sphinx. The Nok culture of Nigeria made striking terracotta heads more than 2,000 years ago, and ancient Greeks created lifelike marble and bronze figures."],["Great sculptures of Asia","In 1974 farmers digging a well in Xi’an, China, discovered the Terracotta Army, about 8,000 life-size clay soldiers buried with the first emperor of China around 210 BCE. Buddhist sculpture spread across Asia, from the giant Buddhas of Leshan in China to the relief carvings of Borobudur in Indonesia and Angkor in Cambodia."],["Renaissance and beyond","Italian Renaissance sculptors revived classical styles. Donatello cast a bronze David in the 1400s, and Michelangelo carved his marble David, over 5 meters tall, between 1501 and 1504. Later, Gian Lorenzo Bernini made marble seem to move, and in the 1800s Auguste Rodin created powerful works such as The Thinker."]],
k:[["Main techniques","Carving, modeling, casting, assembling"],["Oldest known","Lion-man figure, about 40,000 years old"],["Terracotta Army","About 8,000 figures, found 1974"],["Michelangelo’s David","Carved 1501–1504"],["Common materials","Stone, wood, clay, bronze"]],
d:[["c. 38,000 BCE","The Lion-man figure is carved in Germany"],["c. 2500 BCE","The Great Sphinx of Giza is carved"],["c. 210 BCE","The Terracotta Army is buried in China"],["1504","Michelangelo completes David"],["1886","The Statue of Liberty is dedicated in New York"],["1935","The Oblation is unveiled at the University of the Philippines"]],
s2:[["The moai of Rapa Nui","On Rapa Nui, or Easter Island, in the Pacific, the Polynesian inhabitants carved nearly a thousand giant stone statues called moai, mostly between about 1250 and 1500. Many represent honored ancestors. Some are over 9 meters tall. Islanders say the statues walked to their places; experiments show they could be moved upright by rocking them with ropes."],["The Benin Bronzes","The Kingdom of Benin, in present-day Nigeria, produced thousands of brass and bronze plaques and sculptures from the 1200s onward, recording kings, warriors and court life. In 1897 British forces looted them. They were scattered among museums worldwide, and since the 2020s several countries have begun returning them to Nigeria."],["Sculpture in the Philippines","Philippine sculpture includes the carved bulul figures of the Ifugao, which guard rice harvests, and the religious santos of the Spanish colonial period. Guillermo Tolentino created the Oblation at the University of the Philippines and the Bonifacio Monument in Caloocan, and the modernist Napoleon Abueva, a National Artist, worked in wood, stone and metal."],["Modern sculpture","In the 1900s sculptors explored new forms. Constantin Brâncuși simplified shapes to their essence, Henry Moore created flowing reclining figures with holes, and Alexander Calder invented mobiles, hanging sculptures that move in the air. Louise Bourgeois built giant spider sculptures. Some artists began using light, sound and even living plants."],["Public sculpture","Statues and monuments in squares and parks honor heroes, mark events and give cities their character, such as the Statue of Liberty in New York, Christ the Redeemer in Rio de Janeiro and the Merlion in Singapore. Communities sometimes debate which figures should be honored, and some statues have been moved or removed."],["How sculpture is cared for","Outdoor sculptures face weathering, pollution and damage. Conservators clean bronze, repair cracked stone and protect wood from insects. Some famous originals, such as Michelangelo’s David, have been moved indoors to protect them, with copies placed outside."]],
sp:["The Terracotta Army","When farmers digging a well near Xi’an in 1974 found fragments of clay figures, they uncovered one of the greatest archaeological discoveries of the 20th century. The pits hold about 8,000 soldiers, along with horses and chariots, made to guard Qin Shi Huang, the first emperor of a unified China, in the afterlife. Each soldier’s face is different, and they were originally painted in bright colors, which faded quickly after exposure to air."],
g:[["Relief","Sculpture that projects from a flat background"],["Lost-wax casting","Casting metal using a wax model that melts away"],["Chisel","A sharp tool for carving stone or wood"],["Mobile","A hanging sculpture that moves"]],
f:["The oldest sculptures are about 40,000 years old.","The Terracotta Army has about 8,000 figures.","Michelangelo’s David is over 5 meters tall."],
fun:"Each soldier in China’s Terracotta Army has a different face."});

ADDART({id:"calligraphy",t:"Calligraphy",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"🖌️",w:"Calligraphy",r:["print","haikuart","goldage","ukiyoe","phlit"],
p:[["Beautiful writing","Calligraphy is the art of beautiful handwriting, done with brushes or pens."],["Many traditions","Chinese, Arabic, Japanese, Indian and European calligraphy are among the great traditions."],["Art and meaning","In calligraphy, the shape of the letters is as important as the words themselves."]],
l:"Calligraphy, from Greek words meaning beautiful writing, is the art of forming letters and characters with skill and expression. Calligraphers use brushes, reed pens, quills or metal nibs, and ink on paper, silk or parchment. In East Asia and the Islamic world, calligraphy has long been considered one of the highest forms of art. Medieval European scribes copied books by hand in elegant scripts. Today calligraphy survives in art, wedding invitations, logos and the revival of traditional scripts such as Philippine baybayin.",
s:[["Chinese calligraphy","In China, calligraphy, shufa, has been honored for more than 2,000 years. Writers use a soft brush and ink to form characters with strokes of varying thickness, speed and energy. The Four Treasures of the Study are the brush, ink, paper and inkstone. Wang Xizhi, who lived in the 300s, is revered as the sage of calligraphy."],["Japanese and Korean calligraphy","Calligraphy spread from China to Korea and Japan. In Japan, shodō, the way of writing, combines Chinese characters with Japanese kana scripts and is closely linked to Zen Buddhism. Many Japanese schoolchildren practice it, especially at New Year. Korean calligraphy, seoye, uses both Chinese characters and the Korean hangul alphabet."],["Arabic calligraphy","In the Islamic world, calligraphy became the most honored art, used to write the Qur’an and decorate mosques, palaces, tiles and pottery. Styles include the angular Kufic and the flowing Naskh and Thuluth. In the 900s the scholar Ibn Muqla created rules of proportion for letters. Arabic calligraphy was added to UNESCO’s list of intangible cultural heritage in 2021."],["European calligraphy","In medieval Europe, monks and scribes copied books by hand with quill pens, creating beautiful manuscripts such as the Book of Kells, made around 800 in Ireland or Scotland. Under Charlemagne, a clear script called Carolingian minuscule was developed, which later became the basis for our lowercase letters. The printing press reduced the need for scribes, but calligraphy remained an art."]],
k:[["Meaning","Beautiful writing"],["Chinese tools","Brush, ink, paper, inkstone"],["Revered Chinese master","Wang Xizhi (4th century)"],["Arabic styles","Kufic, Naskh, Thuluth"],["Philippine script","Baybayin"]],
d:[["353 CE","Wang Xizhi writes the Preface to the Orchid Pavilion poems"],["c. 800","The Book of Kells is created"],["900s","Ibn Muqla sets rules for Arabic letter proportions"],["1593","The Doctrina Christiana is printed in Manila with baybayin"],["2009","Chinese calligraphy is recognized by UNESCO"],["2021","Arabic calligraphy is recognized by UNESCO"]],
s2:[["The Orchid Pavilion","In 353 CE, Wang Xizhi and friends gathered at the Orchid Pavilion to drink wine and write poems. Wang wrote a preface for the collection, Lantingji Xu, which became the most celebrated work in Chinese calligraphy. The original was reportedly buried with the Tang emperor Taizong, who loved it, but copies have been studied for centuries."],["Baybayin","Before Spanish colonization, many peoples in the Philippines wrote with baybayin, a script in which each character stands for a consonant with a vowel. The Doctrina Christiana, printed in Manila in 1593, included Tagalog in baybayin. Today artists and young Filipinos are reviving baybayin in calligraphy, tattoos, logos and on banknotes and government seals. Related scripts are still used by the Hanunuo Mangyan and Tagbanwa peoples."],["Indian and Tibetan calligraphy","India has many scripts, including Devanagari, Bengali, Tamil and Gurmukhi, each with calligraphic traditions in religious texts and art. Persian calligraphy flourished in Mughal India. Tibetan monks copy sacred texts in elegant scripts, and calligraphy decorates prayer flags and temple walls."],["Calligraphy and the computer","In 2005 Steve Jobs, co-founder of Apple, said that a calligraphy class he took at Reed College inspired the attractive range of typefaces in early Macintosh computers. Digital fonts today owe much to centuries of calligraphy, and many are designed by type designers trained in hand lettering."],["Learning calligraphy","Calligraphy takes patience and practice. Beginners learn to hold the brush or pen correctly, control pressure and make basic strokes before forming letters. Many people find it calming, like meditation. Popular forms today include brush lettering and modern calligraphy used in invitations and art."],["Calligraphy as art","Calligraphy can be read, but it is also enjoyed as pure art. Some Chinese and Japanese calligraphers write in a wild cursive style, almost abstract. Contemporary artists from the Middle East, such as practitioners of calligraffiti, combine Arabic letters with street art and modern painting."]],
sp:["The Four Treasures","Chinese calligraphers rely on the Four Treasures of the Study. The brush, made of animal hair, can produce strokes from hair-thin to bold. Ink comes as a solid stick, ground with water on the inkstone, a smooth stone dish. Paper, invented in China around 2,000 years ago, absorbs the ink. Fine sets of these tools have been treasured as works of art in themselves."],
g:[["Calligraphy","The art of beautiful handwriting"],["Script","A system or style of writing"],["Baybayin","A pre-colonial Philippine writing system"],["Manuscript","A book or document written by hand"]],
f:["Calligraphy means beautiful writing.","Our lowercase letters come from a script developed under Charlemagne.","Baybayin is being revived in the Philippines."],
fun:"A calligraphy class helped inspire the fonts on early Apple computers."});

ADDART({id:"drums",t:"Drums and Percussion",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"🥁",w:"Percussion instrument",r:["orchestra","phdance","jazz","musicw"],
p:[["The heartbeat of music","Percussion instruments make sound when struck, shaken or scraped."],["Among the oldest instruments","Drums have been played for thousands of years in nearly every culture."],["Rhythm everywhere","From West African talking drums to Japanese taiko and Philippine kulintang, percussion drives music."]],
l:"Percussion instruments produce sound when they are hit, shaken or scraped. They include drums, which have a skin or membrane stretched over a frame, and many other instruments such as gongs, bells, xylophones, rattles and cymbals. Percussion provides the rhythm at the heart of most music and is used in ceremonies, celebrations, communication and dance around the world. Some percussion instruments, such as the xylophone, gongs and steelpan, can also play melodies.",
s:[["Kinds of percussion","Instruments with a stretched skin, such as drums, are called membranophones. Instruments that vibrate as a whole, such as gongs, bells, cymbals and xylophones, are called idiophones. Some percussion instruments have a definite pitch, like the timpani, and others, like the snare drum, do not."],["Ancient drums","Drums are among the oldest instruments. Archaeologists in China have found drums made with alligator skin dating back several thousand years. Ancient Egypt, Mesopotamia and Mesoamerica all used drums in religious ceremonies and festivals. Bronze drums made by the Dong Son culture of Vietnam more than 2,000 years ago spread across Southeast Asia."],["Drums of Africa","Drums are central to many African musical traditions. The djembe, a goblet-shaped drum from West Africa, produces bass, tone and slap sounds. Talking drums, such as the Yoruba dùndún of Nigeria, can change pitch by squeezing cords, imitating the tones of speech so drummers can send messages and praise poetry."],["The drum kit","The drum kit, or drum set, developed in the United States in the early 1900s so that one player could play several drums and cymbals using hands and feet. It includes a bass drum played with a pedal, a snare drum, tom-toms, a hi-hat and cymbals. It became the rhythmic engine of jazz, rock, pop and many other styles."]],
k:[["Drums (membranophones)","Skin stretched over a frame"],["Idiophones","Gongs, bells, cymbals, xylophones"],["Philippine gong ensemble","Kulintang"],["Trinidad’s national instrument","Steelpan"],["Orchestral drums","Timpani"]],
d:[["c. 5500 BCE","Early drums are used in Neolithic China"],["c. 500 BCE","Dong Son bronze drums are made in Vietnam"],["Early 1900s","The drum kit develops in the United States"],["1930s–1940s","The steelpan is developed in Trinidad and Tobago"],["1981","The taiko group Kodo is founded on Sado Island, Japan"],["2005","UNESCO recognizes the gong culture of Vietnam’s Central Highlands"]],
s2:[["Kulintang","In the southern Philippines, especially among the Maguindanao, Maranao and Tausug peoples, the kulintang ensemble features a row of small bronze gongs played with sticks to make melodies, accompanied by larger gongs and a drum called the dabakan. Similar gong-chime traditions are found in Indonesia, Malaysia and Brunei. In the Cordillera, the Ifugao, Kalinga and other peoples play flat gongs called gangsa."],["Taiko","Taiko are Japanese drums of many sizes, from small hand drums to huge drums over a meter wide. Once used in temples, festivals and battles, taiko became a powerful performing art in the 20th century. Ensembles such as Kodo, founded in 1981, perform energetic, athletic shows, and taiko groups now exist around the world."],["The steelpan","The steelpan, or steel drum, was developed in Trinidad and Tobago in the 1930s and 1940s, when musicians began hammering discarded oil drums into tuned instruments that can play melodies and chords. Steel bands play at Trinidad’s Carnival, and the steelpan is the country’s national instrument."],["Tabla","The tabla is a pair of hand drums from the Indian subcontinent, central to Hindustani classical music. Players use their fingers and palms to produce an astonishing range of sounds, and they learn rhythms by reciting syllables called bols. The great tabla player Zakir Hussain helped bring the tabla to audiences worldwide."],["Drums in festivals","Drums bring energy to festivals everywhere. In the Philippines, drums and percussion drive the dancing at the Ati-Atihan in Kalibo, the Sinulog in Cebu and the Dinagyang in Iloilo. In Brazil, samba schools parade with huge drum sections called baterias during Carnival. In Korea, farmers’ music, nongak, features drums and gongs."],["Percussion in the orchestra","The orchestra’s percussion section includes timpani, tuned kettledrums, as well as the bass drum, snare drum, cymbals, triangle, xylophone, glockenspiel and many others. A percussionist may play several instruments in one piece. Some composers have written entire works for percussion ensembles."]],
sp:["Drums that talk","In West Africa, the hourglass-shaped talking drum is tied with cords from top to bottom. When the drummer squeezes the drum under the arm, the skins tighten and the pitch rises. Because languages such as Yoruba use rising and falling tones to change meaning, skilled drummers can imitate speech, sending messages, announcing events and reciting proverbs and praise poems that listeners can understand."],
g:[["Percussion","Instruments played by striking, shaking or scraping"],["Membranophone","An instrument with a vibrating stretched skin"],["Idiophone","An instrument whose whole body vibrates"],["Kulintang","A Philippine ensemble of small tuned gongs"]],
f:["Drums are among the oldest instruments.","Talking drums can imitate speech.","Kulintang is a row of small tuned gongs."],
fun:"The steelpan of Trinidad and Tobago was first made from discarded oil drums."});

