// Planet Earth articles, part 4: Mount Fuji, the Rocky Mountains, waterfalls, monsoons and the deep ocean.
ADDART({id:"fuji",t:"Mount Fuji",c:"Geography",i:"🗻",w:"Mount Fuji",r:["japancountry","volc","ukiyoe","kilimanjaro","taal"],
p:[["Japan’s highest peak","Mount Fuji, at 3,776 meters, is the highest mountain in Japan."],["A sleeping volcano","It is an active volcano that last erupted in 1707."],["Sacred and beautiful","Fuji is a sacred symbol of Japan and has inspired artists for centuries."]],
l:"Mount Fuji, called Fujisan in Japanese, is the highest mountain in Japan, rising 3,776 meters on the island of Honshu about 100 kilometers southwest of Tokyo. Its almost perfectly symmetrical, snow-capped cone has made it a national symbol, celebrated in poetry, paintings and photographs. Fuji is an active stratovolcano that last erupted in 1707. Considered sacred in Shinto and Buddhist traditions, it was listed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2013 as a sacred place and source of artistic inspiration.",
s:[["A young volcano","Mount Fuji sits where three tectonic plates meet. It is made of layers of lava and ash built up over several older volcanoes during the past 100,000 years or so; the present cone is relatively young. Its last eruption, the Hōei eruption of 1707, came weeks after a huge earthquake and covered Edo, now Tokyo, in ash. Scientists monitor Fuji closely."],["A sacred mountain","Fuji has been worshipped for centuries. Shinto shrines called Sengen shrines honor the goddess Konohanasakuya-hime, associated with the mountain. Pilgrims climbed Fuji as a religious practice, and in the Edo period, groups called Fuji-kō organized pilgrimages and built small model Fujis for those who could not make the journey."],["Climbing Fuji","Fuji can be climbed in the summer season, usually July to early September, when the trails are free of snow. Hundreds of thousands of people climb each year, often overnight to watch the sunrise, called goraikō, from the summit. Because of crowding and safety concerns, the authorities introduced climbing fees and limits on daily climbers in 2024."],["The Fuji Five Lakes","At the northern foot of Fuji lie five lakes formed when lava flows dammed rivers: Kawaguchi, Yamanaka, Sai, Shōji and Motosu. They offer famous reflections of the mountain. The surrounding area, including forests that grew on old lava flows, is part of Fuji-Hakone-Izu National Park."]],
k:[["Height","3,776 m"],["Location","Honshu, between Shizuoka and Yamanashi prefectures"],["Type","Stratovolcano"],["Last eruption","1707"],["World Heritage Site","2013"]],
d:[["c. 100,000 years ago","Older volcanoes begin building the Fuji area"],["864–866","The Jōgan eruption creates large lava flows"],["1707","The Hōei eruption covers Edo in ash"],["c. 1831","Hokusai’s Thirty-six Views of Mount Fuji are published"],["1872","Women are officially allowed to climb Fuji"],["2013","Fuji becomes a UNESCO World Heritage Site"]],
s2:[["Fuji in art","Mount Fuji has appeared in Japanese art for more than a thousand years. Katsushika Hokusai’s print series Thirty-six Views of Mount Fuji, including The Great Wave, and Utagawa Hiroshige’s views made the mountain famous around the world. The ancient poetry collection Man’yōshū, from the 700s, already praised Fuji’s beauty."],["Women on the mountain","For centuries, women were forbidden to climb to the summit of Fuji for religious reasons. The ban was officially lifted in 1872. According to accounts, an Englishwoman, Fanny Parkes, climbed it in 1867, and a Japanese woman, Tatsu Takayama, had reached the summit disguised as a man in 1832."],["Weather on Fuji","The summit is cold and windy, with temperatures often below freezing even in summer nights. A weather station operated on the summit for decades, and in 1964 a weather radar was built there to track typhoons approaching Japan. Lenticular clouds, shaped like flying saucers, often form over the peak."],["Fuji and the bullet train","On clear days, passengers on the Tōkaidō Shinkansen, the high-speed train between Tokyo and Osaka, get spectacular views of Fuji. Fuji can be seen from Tokyo on clear winter days, about 100 kilometers away."],["If Fuji erupts","An eruption of Fuji could spread ash over Tokyo, disrupting trains, flights, power and water for millions of people. The Japanese government has prepared hazard maps and evacuation plans and advises residents to keep emergency supplies, even though no one knows when Fuji will erupt again."],["Overcrowding and protection","Fuji’s popularity has brought crowds, litter and traffic. Some climbers attempt the steep ascent without rest, risking altitude sickness, or in unsuitable clothing. Recent rules on fees, numbers and timing aim to protect both climbers and the mountain’s environment."]],
sp:["A perfect cone","Mount Fuji’s graceful shape comes from repeated eruptions of fluid lava and ash, piling up evenly around the central vent. From many directions, its slopes appear almost perfectly symmetrical, rising gently at the base and steeply near the top. Snow covers the peak for much of the year, usually from autumn to late spring. This classic shape has made Fuji one of the most recognizable mountains on Earth."],
g:[["Stratovolcano","A tall, cone-shaped volcano of layered lava and ash"],["Goraikō","Watching the sunrise from Fuji’s summit"],["Pilgrimage","A journey to a sacred place"],["Lenticular cloud","A lens-shaped cloud that forms over mountains"]],
f:["Mount Fuji is 3,776 meters high.","Fuji last erupted in 1707.","Women were banned from climbing Fuji until 1872."],
fun:"On clear winter days, Mount Fuji can be seen from Tokyo, about 100 kilometers away."});

ADDART({id:"rockies",t:"The Rocky Mountains",c:"Geography",i:"🏔️",w:"Rocky Mountains",r:["usacountry","andes","geysers","alps","canyon"],
p:[["Backbone of a continent","The Rocky Mountains stretch more than 4,800 kilometers through western Canada and the United States."],["The Continental Divide","Rivers on one side of the Rockies flow toward the Pacific; on the other, toward the Atlantic and Arctic."],["Wild places","The Rockies are home to grizzly bears, elk, wolves and famous national parks."]],
l:"The Rocky Mountains, or Rockies, are a major mountain range in western North America, running more than 4,800 kilometers from northern British Columbia in Canada to New Mexico in the United States. Their highest peak is Mount Elbert in Colorado, at 4,401 meters. The Rockies form much of the Continental Divide, which separates rivers flowing to the Pacific from those flowing to the Atlantic and Arctic oceans. They are home to Indigenous nations, rich wildlife and celebrated national parks such as Banff, Jasper, Yellowstone and Glacier.",
s:[["How the Rockies formed","The Rockies were mostly raised between about 80 and 55 million years ago, during a mountain-building event called the Laramide orogeny, when the Pacific plates pushed under North America far inland. Later, erosion by rivers and Ice Age glaciers carved valleys, lakes and sharp peaks."],["Rivers of the West","Many great rivers begin in the Rockies, including the Colorado, the Missouri, the Columbia, the Arkansas and the Rio Grande. Snow that melts each spring feeds these rivers, which provide water for farms and cities across a huge area of dry land."],["Indigenous peoples","The Rockies have been home to many Indigenous nations for thousands of years, including the Shoshone, Ute, Blackfoot, Crow, Arapaho, Ktunaxa and Stoney Nakoda. They hunted bison and elk, gathered plants and traveled mountain passes for trade. Many places in the mountains remain sacred to them."],["Wildlife","The Rockies support grizzly and black bears, elk, moose, bighorn sheep, mountain goats, cougars, lynx and wolves. Wolves were wiped out from Yellowstone by the 1920s and reintroduced in 1995, changing the behavior of elk and helping trees and streams recover, an example of how one species can affect a whole ecosystem."]],
k:[["Length","More than 4,800 km"],["Highest peak","Mount Elbert, 4,401 m"],["Countries","Canada and the United States"],["Formed","About 80–55 million years ago"],["Famous parks","Banff, Jasper, Yellowstone, Glacier, Rocky Mountain"]],
d:[["c. 80 million years ago","The Laramide orogeny begins raising the Rockies"],["1805","The Lewis and Clark expedition crosses the Rockies"],["1858–1859","The Pikes Peak gold rush draws settlers to Colorado"],["1885","Banff becomes Canada’s first national park; the Canadian Pacific Railway is completed"],["1909","The Burgess Shale fossils are discovered"],["1995","Gray wolves are reintroduced to Yellowstone"]],
s2:[["National parks","The Rockies contain some of North America’s most famous parks. Yellowstone, partly in the Rockies, was the world’s first national park in 1872. Banff, created in 1885, was Canada’s first, with turquoise lakes such as Lake Louise and Moraine Lake. Rocky Mountain National Park in Colorado and Glacier National Park in Montana protect high peaks, forests and alpine meadows."],["The Burgess Shale","In 1909 the American scientist Charles Walcott discovered the Burgess Shale in the Canadian Rockies, in what is now Yoho National Park. Its rocks preserve the soft bodies of strange sea creatures from about 508 million years ago, giving a rare view of early animal life during the Cambrian explosion."],["Crossing the mountains","The Rockies were a great barrier to travel. In 1805 the American Lewis and Clark expedition, guided in part by the Shoshone woman Sacagawea, crossed them on the way to the Pacific. Railways such as the Canadian Pacific, completed in 1885, used tunnels and passes, and many Chinese laborers worked on building them in dangerous conditions."],["Gold and silver","Gold and silver rushes in the 1800s brought miners and settlers to the Rockies, founding towns such as Denver and Leadville in Colorado. Many towns boomed and then were abandoned when the mines ran out, leaving ghost towns that tourists visit today."],["Changing mountains","Glaciers in the Rockies are melting as the climate warms. Glacier National Park had about 150 glaciers in 1850 and far fewer today. Warmer winters also help bark beetles spread, killing large areas of pine forest, and drier conditions increase the risk of wildfires."],["Recreation","Millions of visitors come to the Rockies each year to hike, climb, ski, fish, camp and watch wildlife. Ski resorts such as Aspen, Vail, Whistler and Banff’s Lake Louise attract skiers from around the world, and trails such as the Continental Divide Trail run thousands of kilometers along the mountains."]],
sp:["The Continental Divide","Along the crest of the Rockies runs the Continental Divide, an imaginary line separating watersheds. A raindrop falling on one side may flow to the Pacific Ocean, while one falling a few meters away on the other side may flow to the Gulf of Mexico and the Atlantic. At a place called Triple Divide Peak in Montana, water can flow to three oceans: the Pacific, the Atlantic and, through Hudson Bay, the Arctic."],
g:[["Orogeny","A period of mountain building"],["Continental Divide","A line separating rivers flowing to different oceans"],["Watershed","An area of land that drains into one river or sea"],["Alpine","Relating to high mountains above the tree line"]],
f:["The Rockies stretch more than 4,800 km.","Mount Elbert is the highest peak of the Rockies.","Wolves were reintroduced to Yellowstone in 1995."],
fun:"From Triple Divide Peak in Montana, water can flow to three different oceans."});

ADDART({id:"waterfalls",t:"Waterfalls",c:"Geography",i:"💦",w:"Waterfall",r:["hydropower","wcycle","canyon","amazon","nile"],
p:[["Water in free fall","A waterfall is a place where a river or stream drops over a steep edge."],["Shaped by erosion","Waterfalls form where water wears away soft rock faster than hard rock."],["Natural wonders","Angel Falls, Victoria Falls, Iguazu and Niagara are among the most famous."]],
l:"A waterfall is a point in a river or stream where water flows over a vertical drop or a series of steep drops. Waterfalls form where rivers cross bands of hard and soft rock, at the edges of plateaus, in valleys carved by glaciers or along fault lines. They range from small cascades to mighty falls like Victoria Falls in Africa, which sends up spray visible from many kilometers away. The tallest waterfall, Angel Falls in Venezuela, plunges 979 meters. Waterfalls attract millions of visitors and can provide hydroelectric power.",
s:[["How waterfalls form","Many waterfalls form where a river flows over a layer of hard rock lying on top of softer rock. The water wears away the soft rock underneath faster, undercutting the hard layer until pieces break off. Over time, the waterfall slowly moves upstream, leaving a gorge. At its base, falling water digs a deep plunge pool."],["Kinds of waterfalls","Plunge waterfalls drop straight down without touching the rock. Horsetail falls stay in contact with the rock. Cascades tumble down a series of rock steps. Tiered falls drop in several distinct stages. Some waterfalls are tall and narrow, while others, like Victoria Falls, are wide curtains of water."],["The tallest","Angel Falls in Venezuela is the world’s tallest uninterrupted waterfall, with a total height of 979 meters and a main drop of about 807 meters, falling from the flat-topped mountain Auyán-tepui. Its Pemon Indigenous name is Kerepakupai Merú. It is named after the American pilot Jimmie Angel, who flew over it in 1933."],["The widest and biggest","Victoria Falls, on the border of Zambia and Zimbabwe, is about 1.7 kilometers wide and 108 meters high, forming one of the largest curtains of falling water on Earth. Iguazu Falls, on the border of Argentina and Brazil, is made of about 275 separate falls. By volume of water, falls on the Congo River in Africa are among the largest."]],
k:[["Tallest","Angel Falls, Venezuela, 979 m"],["Largest sheet of water","Victoria Falls, about 1.7 km wide"],["Most separate drops","Iguazu Falls, about 275"],["Famous in North America","Niagara Falls"],["Philippine example","Maria Cristina Falls, Iligan"]],
d:[["1855","David Livingstone names Victoria Falls after Queen Victoria"],["1895","Niagara Falls begins producing hydroelectric power"],["1901","Annie Edson Taylor goes over Niagara Falls in a barrel"],["1933","Jimmie Angel flies over Angel Falls"],["1984","Iguazu National Park in Argentina becomes a World Heritage Site"],["1989","Victoria Falls becomes a World Heritage Site"]],
s2:[["Mosi-oa-Tunya","Victoria Falls is known locally as Mosi-oa-Tunya, the smoke that thunders, because its spray rises hundreds of meters and its roar can be heard from far away. The Scottish missionary David Livingstone, the first European to describe it, in 1855, named it after Queen Victoria. In the wet season, more than 500 million liters of water can pour over it every minute."],["Niagara Falls","Niagara Falls, on the border of Canada and the United States, is made of three falls: Horseshoe Falls, American Falls and Bridal Veil Falls. Much of its water is diverted to power plants. Erosion once moved the falls upstream by about a meter a year, but engineering works have slowed it greatly. In 1901 a 63-year-old teacher, Annie Edson Taylor, survived going over the falls in a barrel."],["Waterfalls of the Philippines","The Philippines has many waterfalls. Maria Cristina Falls in Iligan powers hydroelectric plants that supply much of Mindanao. Pagsanjan Falls in Laguna is famous for boat rides through a gorge. Kawasan Falls in Cebu, Tinago Falls in Iligan and Asik-Asik Falls in Cotabato, which seems to pour out of a cliff of plants, attract many visitors."],["Waterfalls and power","The energy of falling water can turn turbines to make electricity. Niagara Falls was one of the first places to generate hydroelectric power on a large scale in the 1890s. Many waterfalls around the world power plants, though this can reduce the flow that visitors see."],["Life around waterfalls","The constant spray of large waterfalls creates rain forests around them, as at Victoria Falls and Iguazu, where rare plants and animals thrive. Some fish and birds, such as the great dusky swift at Iguazu, nest behind the falling water. Gobies in Hawaii climb waterfalls using suction cups on their bellies."],["Frozen and hidden falls","In very cold winters, some waterfalls freeze into towers of ice, which climbers ascend with ice axes. The biggest waterfall on Earth is actually underwater: the Denmark Strait cataract, between Greenland and Iceland, where cold, dense water sinks about 3,500 meters beneath warmer water."]],
sp:["The world’s biggest waterfall is underwater","Beneath the Denmark Strait, between Greenland and Iceland, very cold, heavy water from the Nordic seas flows over a ridge and plunges down into the deep Atlantic. It falls about 3,500 meters, more than three times the height of Angel Falls, carrying millions of cubic meters of water each second. No one can see it, since it happens entirely beneath the sea surface, but it is part of the great ocean currents that move heat around the planet."],
g:[["Plunge pool","A deep pool at the base of a waterfall"],["Gorge","A narrow valley with steep rock walls"],["Cascade","A waterfall that tumbles down rock steps"],["Erosion","The wearing away of rock and soil"]],
f:["Angel Falls is the tallest waterfall.","Victoria Falls is called the smoke that thunders.","The biggest waterfall on Earth is under the sea."],
fun:"The largest waterfall on Earth is hidden under the sea between Greenland and Iceland."});

ADDART({id:"monsoon",t:"Monsoons",c:"Geography",i:"🌧️",w:"Monsoon",r:["climate","phtyph","storms","rice","indocean"],
p:[["Seasonal winds","A monsoon is a seasonal change in wind direction that brings wet and dry seasons."],["Rain for billions","Monsoon rains water the crops that feed billions of people in Asia and Africa."],["Habagat and amihan","In the Philippines, the southwest monsoon is habagat and the northeast monsoon is amihan."]],
l:"A monsoon is a large-scale seasonal reversal of winds, bringing a rainy season and a dry season to much of the tropics. The word comes from the Arabic mawsim, meaning season. The most powerful monsoons occur in South and Southeast Asia, where moist ocean winds bring heavy rain in summer. Monsoons are vital for farming and water supplies for billions of people, but too much rain causes floods and landslides, while a weak monsoon can bring drought and hunger.",
s:[["How monsoons work","In summer, land heats up faster than the ocean. Warm air rises over the land, creating low pressure, and moist air from the ocean flows in to replace it. As the moist air rises over land and mountains, it cools and drops heavy rain. In winter, the pattern reverses: land cools faster, and dry winds blow from land toward the sea."],["The South Asian monsoon","India receives most of its yearly rain during the southwest monsoon, from about June to September. The monsoon usually reaches the southern state of Kerala around the start of June and spreads north. Its arrival is eagerly awaited, celebrated in songs and festivals, because farmers depend on it to plant rice, cotton and other crops."],["Monsoons in the Philippines","The Philippines has two main monsoons. The southwest monsoon, habagat, usually from about June to September, brings warm, moist air and heavy rains, especially to the western side of the country. The northeast monsoon, amihan, from about November to February, brings cooler, drier air. Typhoons can strengthen the habagat, causing severe flooding."],["Other monsoons","Monsoons also affect West Africa, where the rains are crucial for farmers in the Sahel; northern Australia; East Asia, where the rainy season is called meiyu in China and tsuyu in Japan; and parts of the southwestern United States and Mexico, which have a summer monsoon of thunderstorms."]],
k:[["Word origin","Arabic mawsim, season"],["South Asian rainy season","About June–September"],["Philippine southwest monsoon","Habagat"],["Philippine northeast monsoon","Amihan"],["Wettest places","Mawsynram and Cherrapunji, India"]],
d:[["1st century BCE","Greek sailors learn to use monsoon winds to sail to India"],["1686","Edmond Halley explains monsoons by the heating of land and sea"],["1875","The India Meteorological Department is founded"],["1972","PAGASA, the Philippine weather agency, is established"],["2005","Record monsoon rains flood Mumbai"],["2012","Habagat rains flood much of Metro Manila"]],
s2:[["Sailing with the monsoon","For thousands of years, sailors in the Indian Ocean used the monsoon winds to travel. They sailed from Arabia and East Africa to India with the southwest monsoon and returned with the northeast monsoon. This seasonal rhythm shaped trade in spices, textiles and other goods linking Asia, Africa and the Middle East."],["The wettest places on Earth","Mawsynram and Cherrapunji in the hills of Meghalaya, India, are among the wettest places on Earth, receiving more than 11 meters of rain a year on average, mostly during the monsoon, as moist winds from the Bay of Bengal rise over the hills. People there use umbrellas called knups, woven from bamboo and leaves."],["Floods and landslides","Monsoon rains can cause disastrous floods. In 2010 monsoon floods covered about a fifth of Pakistan, affecting some 20 million people. In the Philippines, habagat rains in 2012, enhanced by a typhoon, submerged large parts of Metro Manila. Deforestation, poor drainage and building on floodplains make floods worse."],["When the monsoon fails","If the monsoon is late or weak, crops fail and water runs short. In the past, failed monsoons led to terrible famines in India and elsewhere. Today irrigation, reservoirs, weather forecasting and food storage reduce the risk, but farmers still depend greatly on the rains."],["El Niño and the monsoon","El Niño, a warming of the eastern Pacific Ocean that occurs every few years, often weakens the South Asian monsoon and brings drier conditions to the Philippines, Indonesia and Australia. La Niña, its opposite, often brings more rain. Forecasters watch the Pacific to predict monsoon strength."],["Monsoons and climate change","Scientists expect a warmer climate to make monsoon rainfall more intense and variable, with heavier downpours and longer dry spells in some regions. This could increase the risk of both floods and droughts for the billions of people who depend on monsoon rains."]],
sp:["Amihan and Habagat in Filipino life","The words amihan and habagat are part of everyday Filipino life. Weather forecasts on television announce when the habagat season begins, signaling the rainy season and school class suspensions during heavy rains. The amihan brings cool, breezy months around Christmas, when many festivals take place. Fishermen and farmers have long planned their work around these seasonal winds."],
g:[["Monsoon","A seasonal reversal of winds bringing wet and dry seasons"],["Habagat","The southwest monsoon in the Philippines"],["Amihan","The northeast monsoon in the Philippines"],["El Niño","A periodic warming of the eastern Pacific Ocean"]],
f:["Monsoon comes from the Arabic word for season.","India gets most of its rain during the summer monsoon.","Amihan brings cooler air to the Philippines."],
fun:"People in Meghalaya, India, one of the wettest places on Earth, use woven umbrellas called knups."});

ADDART({id:"trench",t:"The Deep Ocean and Ocean Trenches",c:"Geography",i:"🌊",w:"Deep sea",r:["pacific","submarine","tect","geysers"],
p:[["The largest habitat","The deep ocean is the largest living space on Earth, dark, cold and under huge pressure."],["Ocean trenches","Trenches are the deepest places on Earth, formed where tectonic plates dive beneath each other."],["Mostly unexplored","Much of the deep seafloor has never been mapped in detail or seen by people."]],
l:"The deep ocean, below about 200 meters, is where sunlight fades and the waters become cold, dark and crushingly heavy. It makes up most of the ocean and is the largest habitat on Earth, home to strange creatures adapted to darkness and pressure. The deepest parts are ocean trenches, long, narrow valleys formed where one tectonic plate sinks beneath another. The Mariana Trench in the Pacific reaches nearly 11 kilometers deep at the Challenger Deep, and the Philippine Trench is among the deepest in the world.",
s:[["Zones of the ocean","Scientists divide the ocean into zones by depth. The sunlit zone, down to about 200 meters, has enough light for plants to grow. The twilight zone, to about 1,000 meters, is dim. Below lie the midnight zone, the abyssal zone, from about 4,000 meters, and the hadal zone, below about 6,000 meters, found only in trenches."],["How trenches form","Trenches form at subduction zones, where a heavy ocean plate slides beneath another plate and sinks into the mantle. The bending plate forms a deep, curved valley. Subduction zones also produce powerful earthquakes, tsunamis and chains of volcanoes, such as those of Japan, Indonesia and the Philippines."],["The Mariana Trench","The Mariana Trench in the western Pacific, east of the Mariana Islands, is the deepest known place on Earth. Its deepest point, the Challenger Deep, is about 10,900 meters below sea level. If Mount Everest were placed there, its peak would still be more than 2 kilometers underwater. The pressure is over 1,000 times that at the surface."],["Life in the dark","Deep-sea animals have remarkable adaptations. Many make their own light, called bioluminescence, to find food, attract mates or confuse predators. Anglerfish dangle glowing lures. Gulper eels have huge mouths. Many creatures feed on marine snow, flakes of dead material drifting down from above."]],
k:[["Average ocean depth","About 3,700 m"],["Deepest point","Challenger Deep, about 10,900 m"],["Deepest zone","Hadal zone, below 6,000 m"],["Philippine Trench","More than 10,000 m deep"],["Pressure at the deepest point","Over 1,000 times surface pressure"]],
d:[["1872–1876","HMS Challenger’s expedition explores the deep sea"],["1875","The Challenger expedition records the depth of the Mariana Trench"],["1960","Trieste reaches the Challenger Deep"],["1977","Hydrothermal vents and their life are discovered"],["2012","James Cameron dives alone to the Challenger Deep"],["2023","A snailfish is filmed at over 8,300 m, the deepest fish yet seen"]],
s2:[["The Philippine Trench","The Philippine Trench runs about 1,300 kilometers along the eastern side of the Philippines, where the Philippine Sea Plate sinks beneath the islands. It is one of the deepest trenches in the world, reaching more than 10,000 meters at a point called the Emden Deep. In 2021 Filipino scientist Deo Florence Onda and explorer Victor Vescovo dived to its deepest point, the first Filipino to do so."],["The Challenger expedition","From 1872 to 1876, the British ship HMS Challenger sailed around the world studying the oceans, measuring depths by lowering weighted ropes and collecting thousands of new species. It recorded a depth of about 8,200 meters near the Mariana Islands, and the Challenger Deep is named after it. The expedition is considered the birth of modern oceanography."],["Hydrothermal vents","In 1977 scientists in the submersible Alvin discovered hot springs on the seafloor near the Galápagos Islands, surrounded by giant tube worms, clams and crabs. These creatures rely on bacteria that make food from chemicals in the vent water, not from sunlight. Some scientists think life on Earth may have begun at such vents."],["The deepest fish","Snailfish are small, pale fish with soft bodies that live in trenches. In 2023 scientists filmed a snailfish at a depth of over 8,300 meters in a trench off Japan, the deepest fish ever recorded. Scientists think fish cannot survive much deeper because of the effects of pressure on their bodies."],["Mapping the seafloor","Most of the seafloor has never been mapped in high detail; parts of the Moon and Mars are mapped better. Ships use sonar to measure depth by timing echoes. The international Seabed 2030 project aims to map the whole ocean floor in detail, and by the mid-2020s about a quarter had been mapped."],["Threats to the deep","Even the deep sea is affected by people. Plastic has been found in the Mariana Trench, and deep-sea animals contain pollutants from the surface. Some companies want to mine metal-rich nodules from the seafloor for batteries, but scientists warn this could destroy fragile habitats that take thousands of years to recover. Countries are debating rules at the International Seabed Authority."]],
sp:["Giants of the deep","The giant squid, which can grow longer than a school bus, lived for centuries in sailors’ legends of sea monsters. Scientists had only dead or dying specimens until 2004, when Japanese researchers photographed a live giant squid in its natural habitat, and in 2012 it was filmed in the deep sea for the first time. Its eyes, as big as dinner plates, are among the largest of any animal, helping it see in the dim depths."],
g:[["Trench","A long, deep valley in the ocean floor"],["Subduction","When one tectonic plate sinks beneath another"],["Bioluminescence","Light produced by living things"],["Hadal zone","The deepest ocean zone, found in trenches"]],
f:["The Challenger Deep is nearly 11 km deep.","Many deep-sea animals make their own light.","The Philippine Trench is one of the deepest on Earth."],
fun:"If Mount Everest were placed in the Challenger Deep, its peak would still be underwater."});

