// Space articles, part 4: Mars rovers, the asteroid belt, spacesuits and how the Solar System formed.
ADDART({id:"marsrovers",t:"Mars Rovers",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🤖",w:"Mars rover",r:["mars","astrobio","robot","spaceagencies","rocket"],
p:[["Robots on Mars","Mars rovers are robotic vehicles that drive across the surface of Mars to study it."],["Searching for water and life","Rovers have found evidence that Mars once had lakes, rivers and conditions suitable for life."],["Controlled from Earth","Scientists on Earth send commands, but signals take several minutes each way."]],
l:"Mars rovers are wheeled robots sent to explore the surface of Mars. Equipped with cameras, drills, lasers and laboratories, they study rocks, soil and air to understand the planet’s history and whether it ever supported life. Since 1997, NASA has landed five successful rovers, and China landed its Zhurong rover in 2021. Together they have shown that ancient Mars had liquid water, lakes and the chemical ingredients for life, transforming our picture of the Red Planet.",
s:[["Sojourner","The first rover to work on Mars was Sojourner, a six-wheeled robot the size of a microwave oven, carried by NASA’s Mars Pathfinder lander in July 1997. It explored rocks near the landing site for almost three months, proving that a rover could operate on another planet."],["Spirit and Opportunity","In January 2004, the twin rovers Spirit and Opportunity landed on opposite sides of Mars. Planned to last 90 Martian days, Spirit worked for over six years, and Opportunity for over fourteen, driving more than 45 kilometers, farther than a marathon. They found minerals and rocks that form in water, showing Mars was once wet."],["Curiosity","Curiosity, about the size of a small car, landed in Gale Crater in August 2012, lowered by a rocket-powered sky crane. Powered by a nuclear battery, it has been climbing Mount Sharp, a mountain of layered rock. It found that Gale Crater once held a long-lasting lake with the chemistry needed for microbial life, and detected organic molecules."],["Perseverance","Perseverance landed in Jezero Crater, an ancient lake with a river delta, in February 2021. It searches for signs of past life and collects rock samples in sealed tubes for possible return to Earth. In 2024 it found a rock with unusual spots that scientists consider a possible sign of ancient life, though other explanations remain possible."]],
k:[["First rover","Sojourner, 1997"],["Longest-working","Opportunity, 2004–2018"],["Largest","Perseverance and Curiosity"],["Chinese rover","Zhurong, 2021"],["Signal delay","About 3 to 22 minutes each way"]],
d:[["1971","The Soviet Mars 3 lander carries a small rover but fails soon after landing"],["1997","Sojourner explores Mars"],["2004","Spirit and Opportunity land"],["2012","Curiosity lands in Gale Crater"],["2021","Perseverance and China’s Zhurong land; Ingenuity makes the first powered flight on another planet"],["2024","Perseverance finds a rock with possible signs of ancient life"]],
s2:[["Ingenuity","Perseverance carried Ingenuity, a small helicopter weighing about 1.8 kilograms. On 19 April 2021, it made the first powered, controlled flight on another planet, rising about 3 meters in the thin Martian air. Designed for just five test flights, it flew 72 times before a damaged rotor ended its mission in January 2024."],["Zhurong","China’s Tianwen-1 mission orbited Mars and, in May 2021, landed the Zhurong rover in a plain called Utopia Planitia, making China the second country to operate a rover on Mars. Named after a god of fire in Chinese mythology, Zhurong studied the soil and underground layers with radar, finding signs of past water."],["Driving on Mars","Because radio signals take minutes to travel between Earth and Mars, rovers cannot be driven with a joystick. Engineers plan each day’s moves, send commands and wait for results. Modern rovers can also navigate on their own, using cameras to spot hazards. Rovers usually move only tens or hundreds of meters a day."],["Seven minutes of terror","Landing on Mars is extremely difficult. A spacecraft enters the atmosphere at about 20,000 km/h and must slow to a gentle touchdown in about seven minutes, without help from Earth, using a heat shield, parachute and rockets. Engineers call this the seven minutes of terror. Many earlier Mars landers crashed."],["Dust storms and endings","Mars has huge dust storms that can cover the whole planet. In 2018 a global storm blocked sunlight from Opportunity’s solar panels, and it fell silent; its last message was interpreted by many as saying its battery was low and it was getting dark. Spirit got stuck in soft sand in 2009 and stopped communicating in 2010."],["Future rovers","The European Space Agency is preparing the Rosalind Franklin rover, which will drill up to 2 meters below the surface, where signs of life might be protected from radiation. Space agencies are also studying ways to bring Perseverance’s samples back to Earth and, in the longer term, to send human explorers to Mars."]],
sp:["The sky crane","Curiosity and Perseverance were too heavy for airbags, which had cushioned earlier rovers bouncing onto the surface. Engineers invented the sky crane: a rocket-powered platform hovered about 20 meters above the ground and lowered the rover on cables. When the wheels touched down, the cables were cut and the platform flew away to crash at a safe distance. The bold idea worked perfectly both times."],
g:[["Rover","A vehicle that drives across the surface of a planet or moon"],["Sky crane","A rocket platform that lowers a rover on cables"],["Organic molecule","A carbon-based molecule, a building block of life"],["Delta","Sediment deposited where a river enters a lake or sea"]],
f:["Opportunity worked on Mars for over 14 years.","Ingenuity was the first helicopter to fly on another planet.","China landed its Zhurong rover on Mars in 2021."],
fun:"The Opportunity rover was planned to last 90 days but worked for more than 14 years."});

ADDART({id:"asteroidbelt",t:"The Asteroid Belt",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🪨",w:"Asteroid belt",r:["comet","solar","jupiter","pluto","meteors"],
p:[["Rocks between the planets","The asteroid belt is a ring-shaped region between Mars and Jupiter filled with rocky and metallic bodies."],["Leftovers","Asteroids are leftovers from the formation of the Solar System about 4.6 billion years ago."],["Mostly empty space","Despite the millions of asteroids, they are spread so far apart that spacecraft pass through safely."]],
l:"The asteroid belt is a broad ring of space between the orbits of Mars and Jupiter where most of the Solar System’s asteroids orbit the Sun. It contains the dwarf planet Ceres, large asteroids such as Vesta and Pallas, and millions of smaller rocks. Asteroids are remnants of the early Solar System, material that never formed a planet, probably because Jupiter’s powerful gravity stirred it up. Despite its crowded image in films, the belt is mostly empty space, and the total mass of all its asteroids is only a few percent of the Moon’s.",
s:[["Discovery","On 1 January 1801, the Italian astronomer Giuseppe Piazzi discovered Ceres, which was at first thought to be a missing planet between Mars and Jupiter. Soon Pallas, Juno and Vesta were found. As more and more were discovered, astronomers realized they were a new class of small bodies, which William Herschel called asteroids, meaning star-like."],["Why no planet formed","As the planets formed from a disk of dust and gas, the material in this region should have clumped together into a planet. But the gravity of nearby Jupiter stirred up the small bodies, making them collide too fast to stick together. Many were thrown out of the region, leaving only a small fraction of the original material."],["Kinds of asteroids","Most asteroids fall into three main types. Dark, carbon-rich C-type asteroids are the most common, especially in the outer belt. Stony S-type asteroids are found more in the inner belt. Metallic M-type asteroids may be the iron cores of shattered early worlds."],["Ceres","Ceres, about 940 kilometers across, is the largest object in the belt and contains roughly a third of its total mass. In 2006 it was classified as a dwarf planet. It has a rocky core, an icy mantle and possibly a layer of salty water deep inside. NASA’s Dawn spacecraft found bright patches of salt in Occator Crater."]],
k:[["Location","Between Mars and Jupiter"],["Largest object","Ceres, about 940 km wide"],["First discovered","Ceres, 1 January 1801"],["Total mass","A few percent of the Moon’s"],["Main types","C-type, S-type, M-type"]],
d:[["1801","Giuseppe Piazzi discovers Ceres"],["1807","Vesta is discovered"],["1866","Daniel Kirkwood discovers gaps in the belt caused by Jupiter"],["2011","Dawn begins orbiting Vesta"],["2015","Dawn begins orbiting Ceres"],["2023","NASA’s Psyche spacecraft launches toward a metal-rich asteroid"]],
s2:[["The Dawn mission","NASA’s Dawn spacecraft, launched in 2007 and powered by ion engines, orbited Vesta from 2011 to 2012 and then Ceres from 2015 until 2018. It was the first spacecraft to orbit two different bodies beyond the Earth–Moon system. It revealed Vesta’s giant impact basin and Ceres’s bright salt deposits and icy interior."],["Kirkwood gaps","In 1866 the American astronomer Daniel Kirkwood noticed gaps in the asteroid belt where few asteroids orbit. Asteroids at these distances would circle the Sun in a simple ratio to Jupiter’s orbit, for example three times for every one of Jupiter’s, so Jupiter’s regular tugs push them out of those orbits."],["Psyche","Psyche is an asteroid about 220 kilometers wide that appears to be rich in metal, perhaps the exposed core of an early planet. NASA’s Psyche spacecraft, launched in October 2023, is due to arrive in 2029. Studying it could reveal what lies deep inside rocky planets like Earth, where we cannot go."],["Not like the movies","In films, spaceships dodge through crowded fields of tumbling rocks. In reality, asteroids in the belt are usually hundreds of thousands of kilometers apart. Spacecraft such as Pioneer 10, Voyager and New Horizons have flown through the belt without any danger of collision."],["Trojans and near-Earth asteroids","Not all asteroids are in the belt. Trojan asteroids share Jupiter’s orbit, gathered in two swarms ahead of and behind the planet; NASA’s Lucy mission, launched in 2021, is visiting several of them. Near-Earth asteroids have orbits that bring them close to Earth; Japan’s Hayabusa2 and NASA’s OSIRIS-REx brought back samples from two such asteroids, Ryugu and Bennu."],["Mining asteroids","Some asteroids contain valuable metals such as iron, nickel and platinum, and others contain water ice that could be turned into rocket fuel. Companies and space agencies have studied asteroid mining, but it remains far in the future because of the great cost and difficulty."]],
sp:["Bright spots on Ceres","As Dawn approached Ceres in 2015, it spotted mysterious bright spots shining from inside Occator Crater. Up close, they turned out to be deposits of sodium carbonate, a kind of salt, left behind when salty water rose from below and evaporated. The discovery suggests that Ceres may still have pockets of liquid brine deep beneath its surface, making this small world more active than once thought."],
g:[["Asteroid","A small rocky or metallic body orbiting the Sun"],["Dwarf planet","A round body orbiting the Sun that has not cleared its orbit"],["Kirkwood gap","A gap in the belt caused by Jupiter’s gravity"],["Ion engine","A spacecraft engine that uses electrically charged particles"]],
f:["Ceres was the first asteroid discovered.","The asteroid belt is mostly empty space.","Dawn orbited both Vesta and Ceres."],
fun:"All the asteroids in the belt together weigh only a few percent as much as our Moon."});

ADDART({id:"spacesuits",t:"Spacesuits",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🧑‍🚀",w:"Space suit",r:["astronauts","iss","apollo","space"],
p:[["A personal spacecraft","A spacesuit protects an astronaut from the dangers of space, like a small spacecraft shaped for a person."],["Air, pressure and temperature","Suits supply oxygen, keep the body under pressure and protect against extreme heat and cold."],["Walking in space","Astronauts wear special suits for spacewalks outside spacecraft and on the Moon."]],
l:"A spacesuit is a garment that keeps a person alive in the vacuum of space or in the low pressure of high altitudes. Space has no air to breathe, no pressure to keep body fluids from boiling and temperatures that swing from scorching in sunlight to freezing in shadow. Suits worn for spacewalks are complex machines, with layers of protective fabric, a pressurized inner bladder, a helmet, gloves and a backpack that supplies oxygen, removes carbon dioxide, controls temperature and provides communications.",
s:[["Why suits are needed","Without a suit, a person exposed to space would lose consciousness within about 15 seconds as oxygen left the blood. The lack of pressure would make body fluids begin to vaporize. Unfiltered sunlight would burn, and tiny meteoroids could strike. Temperatures outside can range from above 120°C in sunlight to below minus 150°C in shadow."],["Layers of protection","A spacewalk suit has many layers. Next to the skin, a garment with tubes of cool water removes body heat. A pressure bladder holds air in, and a restraint layer keeps it from ballooning. Outer layers of strong, insulating and reflective fabrics protect against heat, cold, radiation and micrometeoroids."],["Two kinds of suits","Launch and entry suits, such as the Russian Sokol and SpaceX’s suits, protect astronauts inside spacecraft if the cabin loses pressure. Spacewalk suits, such as NASA’s Extravehicular Mobility Unit and Russia’s Orlan, let astronauts work outside for many hours. A spacewalk suit with its backpack weighs well over 100 kilograms on Earth but feels weightless in orbit."],["Working in a suit","Moving in a pressurized suit is tiring, like squeezing a balloon with every motion. Gloves are especially hard to bend, and astronauts’ hands can become sore and fingernails damaged after long spacewalks. Astronauts train underwater in huge pools wearing suits to practice tasks in conditions similar to weightlessness."]],
k:[["First spacewalk","Alexei Leonov, 18 March 1965"],["First moonwalk suits","Apollo A7L, 1969"],["NASA spacewalk suit","Extravehicular Mobility Unit (EMU)"],["Russian spacewalk suit","Orlan"],["First commercial spacewalk","Polaris Dawn, 2024"]],
d:[["1934","Pilot Wiley Post flies high using an early pressure suit"],["1965","Alexei Leonov makes the first spacewalk"],["1969","Apollo 11 astronauts walk on the Moon"],["1984","Bruce McCandless makes the first untethered spacewalk"],["2008","Zhai Zhigang makes China’s first spacewalk"],["2019","Christina Koch and Jessica Meir make the first all-female spacewalk"]],
s2:[["Leonov’s dangerous spacewalk","On 18 March 1965, the Soviet cosmonaut Alexei Leonov floated outside Voskhod 2 for about 12 minutes, the first spacewalk. But his suit ballooned in the vacuum, and he could not fit back through the airlock. He secretly let air out of the suit, risking decompression sickness, to squeeze back inside. He later became a celebrated space artist."],["Suits for the Moon","The Apollo A7L suits, worn by the 12 astronauts who walked on the Moon between 1969 and 1972, had about 21 layers and a backpack life-support system. Lunar dust was a big problem: it was sharp and clingy, wearing away fabrics and seals. New suits being developed for future Moon missions must be more flexible and resistant to dust."],["Flying free","In February 1984, the American astronaut Bruce McCandless flew about 100 meters away from the Space Shuttle Challenger using a jet-powered backpack called the Manned Maneuvering Unit, with no safety line. The photo of him floating alone above Earth became one of the most famous images of spaceflight."],["Suits around the world","Different countries have developed their own suits. Russia’s Orlan suits are entered through a door in the back. China’s Feitian suit was first used in 2008, when Zhai Zhigang made China’s first spacewalk. Astronauts from many countries have worn American and Russian suits on the International Space Station."],["Fitting every astronaut","Spacesuits must fit well to work safely. In 2019 a planned all-female spacewalk was postponed because only one medium-size suit torso was ready. In October 2019 Christina Koch and Jessica Meir completed the first all-female spacewalk. Future suits are being designed to fit a wider range of body sizes."],["Close calls","Spacewalks are risky. In 2013 the Italian astronaut Luca Parmitano’s helmet began filling with water from a leak in the cooling system during a spacewalk, covering his eyes, nose and ears. He made his way back to the airlock by memory and touch. Engineers fixed the problem and added absorbent pads and snorkels to helmets."]],
sp:["Wiley Post’s pressure suit","In 1934 the American aviator Wiley Post wanted to fly high into the jet stream, where the air is too thin to breathe. With the B. F. Goodrich company, he developed one of the first practical pressure suits, made of rubber and fabric with a metal helmet that looked like a diving helmet. He flew to heights of over 12,000 meters. His suit helped lay the foundation for the spacesuits of later decades."],
g:[["Spacewalk","Activity outside a spacecraft in space"],["Pressure suit","A suit that keeps the body at safe air pressure"],["Airlock","A chamber that lets astronauts move between spacecraft and space"],["Life support","Systems that provide air, temperature control and other needs"]],
f:["Alexei Leonov made the first spacewalk in 1965.","Spacesuits contain water tubes to cool astronauts.","The first all-female spacewalk was in 2019."],
fun:"The first spacewalker had to let air out of his ballooning suit to get back inside."});

ADDART({id:"solarform",t:"How the Solar System Formed",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🌌",w:"Formation and evolution of the Solar System",r:["solar","sun","nebula","moon","kuiper","hotjupiters"],
p:[["Born from a cloud","The Solar System formed about 4.6 billion years ago from a collapsing cloud of gas and dust."],["A spinning disk","The Sun formed at the center, and the planets grew from a flat disk of material around it."],["Rocky and giant worlds","Rocky planets formed near the Sun, and gas and ice giants formed farther out."]],
l:"The Solar System formed about 4.6 billion years ago from a giant cloud of gas and dust that collapsed under its own gravity. Most of the material fell to the center to form the Sun, while the rest flattened into a spinning disk. Within the disk, dust grains stuck together into pebbles, boulders and eventually planets. This idea, called the nebular hypothesis, was first proposed in the 1700s and is now supported by meteorites, computer models and telescope images of young stars surrounded by planet-forming disks.",
s:[["The solar nebula","The Sun and planets began in a cold cloud of gas, mostly hydrogen and helium, with dust containing heavier elements made by earlier generations of stars. Something, perhaps the shock wave from a nearby exploding star, caused part of the cloud to collapse. As it shrank, it spun faster and flattened into a disk, like pizza dough spun in the air."],["The Sun ignites","Most of the mass gathered at the center, becoming hotter and denser until nuclear fusion began, turning hydrogen into helium. The Sun was born. It contains about 99.8 percent of all the mass in the Solar System; everything else, including all the planets, makes up the remaining 0.2 percent."],["Building planets","In the disk, dust grains collided and stuck together, growing into larger bodies called planetesimals, a few kilometers across. Their gravity pulled in more material, and they collided and merged to form protoplanets and eventually planets. This process took from a few million to tens of millions of years."],["Rocky and giant planets","Close to the hot young Sun, only rock and metal could stay solid, so small rocky planets formed: Mercury, Venus, Earth and Mars. Beyond a boundary called the frost line, water and other substances froze into ice, providing more building material. There, large cores formed quickly and pulled in vast amounts of gas, becoming Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus and Neptune."]],
k:[["Age","About 4.6 billion years"],["Theory","Nebular hypothesis"],["Sun’s share of mass","About 99.8 percent"],["Oldest solids","Mineral grains in meteorites, about 4.57 billion years old"],["Key boundary","The frost line"]],
d:[["1755","Immanuel Kant suggests the Solar System formed from a rotating cloud"],["1796","Pierre-Simon Laplace develops the nebular hypothesis"],["1950s","Meteorite dating fixes the Solar System’s age at about 4.5 billion years"],["2005","The Nice model proposes that the giant planets moved after forming"],["2014","ALMA images a planet-forming disk around the young star HL Tauri"],["2019","New Horizons flies past Arrokoth, a pristine building block"]],
s2:[["Clues from meteorites","Some meteorites contain tiny white grains called calcium-aluminum-rich inclusions, the oldest known solids in the Solar System. By measuring radioactive elements in them, scientists have dated them to about 4.567 billion years ago, marking the birth of the Solar System. Meteorites are like time capsules from that era."],["Disks around other stars","Telescopes now show planet-forming disks around young stars. In 2014 the ALMA radio telescope in Chile captured an image of the disk around HL Tauri, with dark rings that may be gaps cleared by forming planets. Such images confirm that our Solar System’s birth was not unusual."],["The birth of the Moon","Not long after Earth formed, about 4.5 billion years ago, a body about the size of Mars, named Theia, is thought to have collided with it. The impact threw debris into orbit, which gathered to form the Moon. This giant-impact hypothesis explains why the Moon has a small iron core and rocks similar to Earth’s."],["Wandering giant planets","Computer models suggest that the giant planets did not stay where they formed. Their gravity interacted with leftover planetesimals, causing Jupiter to move slightly inward and Saturn, Uranus and Neptune outward. Such movements may have scattered comets and asteroids, sending some crashing into the inner planets and helping shape the Kuiper Belt."],["Leftover building blocks","Asteroids, comets and Kuiper Belt objects are leftovers from planet formation. In 2019 NASA’s New Horizons spacecraft flew past Arrokoth, a small Kuiper Belt object made of two lobes gently stuck together. Its shape supports the idea that planetesimals formed by the gentle collapse of clouds of pebbles."],["The Sun’s future","The Sun is about halfway through its life. In about 5 billion years, it will run low on hydrogen in its core and swell into a red giant, probably swallowing Mercury and Venus and perhaps Earth. It will then shed its outer layers, leaving behind a small, hot white dwarf that will slowly cool over billions of years."]],
sp:["Made of stardust","The hydrogen in the Solar System dates back to the Big Bang, but almost all the heavier elements, the carbon in our bodies, the oxygen we breathe, the iron in our blood and the silicon in rocks, were made inside earlier stars that lived and died before the Sun was born. Those stars scattered their elements into space, enriching the cloud from which the Solar System formed. In a real sense, we are made of stardust."],
g:[["Nebula","A cloud of gas and dust in space"],["Planetesimal","A small body that combines with others to form planets"],["Frost line","The distance from a star beyond which ice can form"],["Protoplanetary disk","A disk of gas and dust around a young star"]],
f:["The Solar System is about 4.6 billion years old.","The Sun holds about 99.8 percent of the Solar System’s mass.","The Moon probably formed from a giant collision."],
fun:"The atoms in your body were made inside stars that died before the Sun was born."});

