// Science and Numbers articles, part 4: molecules, measurement, simple machines, percentages and friction.
ADDART({id:"molecules",t:"Molecules and Chemical Bonds",c:"Science",i:"⚛️",w:"Molecule",r:["atom","reactions","carbonchem","crystals","mixtures"],
p:[["Atoms joined together","A molecule is a group of two or more atoms held together by chemical bonds."],["Sharing and swapping electrons","Atoms bond by sharing electrons or by giving and taking them."],["Shapes matter","The shape of a molecule decides how it behaves, from how water flows to how medicines work."]],
l:"A molecule is the smallest unit of a substance that keeps its chemical properties, made of atoms joined by chemical bonds. A water molecule, H₂O, has two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom; oxygen gas is made of molecules of two oxygen atoms. Molecules range from tiny pairs of atoms to giant chains like DNA containing billions of atoms. Chemical bonds form because atoms become more stable by sharing or transferring their outer electrons. Understanding bonds and molecular shapes explains much of chemistry and biology.",
s:[["Covalent bonds","In a covalent bond, two atoms share a pair of electrons, which holds them together. Atoms can share one, two or three pairs, forming single, double or triple bonds. Water, carbon dioxide, sugar and the proteins in our bodies are held together by covalent bonds. The American chemist Gilbert Lewis described the shared-electron-pair bond in 1916."],["Ionic bonds","In an ionic bond, one atom gives one or more electrons to another. The atom that loses electrons becomes a positive ion, and the one that gains them becomes a negative ion, and opposite charges attract. Table salt, sodium chloride, forms this way: sodium gives an electron to chlorine. Ionic compounds usually form crystals and dissolve in water."],["Forces between molecules","Weaker attractions also act between molecules. Hydrogen bonds, attractions between slightly positive hydrogen atoms and slightly negative atoms such as oxygen, hold water molecules close together, giving water its high boiling point and surface tension. Hydrogen bonds also hold the two strands of DNA together."],["Molecular shapes","Molecules have definite 3D shapes because electron pairs push each other apart. Water is bent, with an angle of about 104.5 degrees; carbon dioxide is straight; and methane is shaped like a pyramid with a triangular base, called a tetrahedron. A molecule’s shape affects its smell, taste and how it fits into other molecules, such as enzymes and receptors."]],
k:[["Example","Water, H₂O"],["Main bond types","Covalent, ionic, metallic"],["Avogadro constant","About 6.022 × 10²³ per mole"],["Water bond angle","About 104.5°"],["Key book","The Nature of the Chemical Bond (Pauling, 1939)"]],
d:[["1808","John Dalton publishes his atomic theory"],["1811","Amedeo Avogadro proposes that gases contain molecules"],["1916","Gilbert Lewis describes the shared electron-pair bond"],["1939","Linus Pauling publishes The Nature of the Chemical Bond"],["1953","The double-helix structure of DNA is described"],["2009","Scientists at IBM image the bonds of a single molecule"]],
s2:[["Avogadro’s idea","In 1811 the Italian scientist Amedeo Avogadro proposed that equal volumes of gases at the same temperature and pressure contain the same number of particles. He suggested that gases such as oxygen are made of molecules of two atoms. His idea was largely ignored for about 50 years. The number of particles in one mole of a substance, about 602 sextillion, is called the Avogadro constant in his honor."],["Linus Pauling","The American chemist Linus Pauling used quantum mechanics to explain how atoms bond and why molecules have particular shapes. His 1939 book The Nature of the Chemical Bond became one of the most influential science books ever. He won the Nobel Prize in Chemistry in 1954 and the Nobel Peace Prize in 1962 for campaigning against nuclear weapons testing."],["Seeing a molecule","Molecules are far too small to see with ordinary microscopes. In 2009 scientists at IBM in Zurich used an atomic force microscope, with a tip ending in a single molecule, to image a molecule called pentacene, showing its five rings of atoms and the bonds between them. Such images confirm the structures chemists had worked out for decades."],["Mirror-image molecules","Some molecules exist in two forms that are mirror images, like left and right hands, and cannot be placed exactly on top of each other. This property is called chirality. The two forms can behave differently: one form of the molecule carvone smells like spearmint, while its mirror image smells like caraway. In medicines, one form may heal while the other is useless or harmful."],["Giant molecules","Some molecules are enormous. Polymers such as plastics and proteins are long chains of repeating units. A single DNA molecule in one human chromosome can contain hundreds of millions of base pairs and would stretch several centimeters if unwound. Diamond can be thought of as a single giant network of bonded carbon atoms."],["Same atoms, different molecules","The same atoms can form different molecules with very different properties. Oxygen gas, O₂, is essential for breathing, while ozone, O₃, is a pollutant near the ground but protects life high in the atmosphere by absorbing ultraviolet light. Ethanol and dimethyl ether have the same atoms but different arrangements, one a liquid alcohol, the other a gas."]],
sp:["Why water is special","Water molecules are small but remarkable. Because oxygen pulls electrons more strongly than hydrogen, a water molecule is slightly negative at the oxygen end and slightly positive at the hydrogen ends. These charges let water molecules stick together through hydrogen bonds, so water is liquid at room temperature, dissolves many substances, and expands when it freezes, making ice float. Without these properties, life as we know it would not exist."],
g:[["Molecule","A group of atoms joined by chemical bonds"],["Covalent bond","A bond in which atoms share electrons"],["Ion","An atom or group of atoms with an electric charge"],["Hydrogen bond","A weak attraction involving hydrogen atoms"]],
f:["A water molecule has two hydrogen atoms and one oxygen atom.","Table salt is held together by ionic bonds.","Mirror-image molecules can smell different."],
fun:"One form of the molecule carvone smells like spearmint, and its mirror image smells like caraway."});

ADDART({id:"measure",t:"Measurement and Units",c:"Science",i:"📐",w:"International System of Units",r:["timephys","scimeth","euclid","goldage"],
p:[["How much, how long, how heavy","Measurement means finding the size, amount or degree of something using standard units."],["Metric for the world","Most of the world uses the metric system, built on units such as the meter and kilogram."],["Defined by nature","Today the basic units are defined by unchanging constants of nature."]],
l:"Measurement is the process of comparing a quantity, such as length, mass, time or temperature, with a standard unit. Early units were based on body parts and everyday objects, and they varied from place to place. In the 1790s France created the metric system, based on the meter and kilogram, which grew into the International System of Units, SI, used by nearly every country and by scientists everywhere. Since 2019, all seven SI base units have been defined by fundamental constants of nature, making them the same anywhere in the universe.",
s:[["Ancient units","Early people measured with their bodies. The cubit, used in ancient Egypt and Mesopotamia, was the length from elbow to fingertip. The foot, the hand and the pace were also common. In the Philippines, traditional measures included the dangkal, a handspan, and the dipa, the length of outstretched arms, while rice was measured by the ganta and the salop."],["The metric system","During the French Revolution, scientists created a new system meant to be for all people, for all time. The meter was defined as one ten-millionth of the distance from the North Pole to the equator through Paris. The gram was based on the mass of water in a small cube. Units were related by multiples of ten, using prefixes such as kilo- for a thousand and milli- for a thousandth."],["The SI base units","The International System of Units, adopted in 1960, has seven base units: the meter for length, the kilogram for mass, the second for time, the ampere for electric current, the kelvin for temperature, the mole for amount of substance and the candela for brightness. All other units, such as the newton for force and the watt for power, are built from these."],["Accuracy and precision","Accuracy means how close a measurement is to the true value; precision means how consistent repeated measurements are. Scientists always state the uncertainty of their measurements. Better instruments, such as atomic clocks and laser rulers, allow measurements of astonishing precision."]],
k:[["International system","SI, adopted 1960"],["Base units","Seven"],["Metric system created","France, 1790s"],["Meter defined by","The speed of light (since 1983)"],["World Metrology Day","20 May"]],
d:[["c. 3000 BCE","Egyptians use the royal cubit"],["1799","Platinum standards for the meter and kilogram are made in France"],["1875","The Metre Convention is signed by 17 nations"],["1960","The International System of Units (SI) is adopted"],["1967","The second is defined using caesium atoms"],["2019","All SI base units are redefined using constants of nature"]],
s2:[["Goodbye, Le Grand K","From 1889 the kilogram was defined by a single platinum-iridium cylinder, known as Le Grand K, kept in a vault near Paris. Comparisons showed that copies were changing very slightly relative to it. On 20 May 2019, the kilogram was redefined using the Planck constant, a fundamental number of physics, so it no longer depends on any object."],["Measuring time","The second was once defined as a fraction of a day, but Earth’s rotation is slightly irregular. Since 1967, the second has been defined as the time it takes for a certain kind of radiation from caesium atoms to oscillate 9,192,631,770 times. Atomic clocks based on this definition keep GPS, phones and the internet in step."],["The cost of mixed-up units","In 1999 NASA lost the Mars Climate Orbiter because one team used imperial units, pound-force, while another used metric newtons. The spacecraft approached Mars too low and was destroyed. The mistake showed why scientists and engineers insist on using the same units."],["Metric around the world","Almost every country officially uses the metric system, including the Philippines. The United States still uses customary units such as inches, pounds and Fahrenheit in daily life, though science and medicine there use metric. Many countries mix systems informally, measuring height in feet or televisions in inches."],["Temperature scales","Temperature is measured in degrees Celsius in most of the world, where water freezes at 0 and boils at 100 at sea level. The Fahrenheit scale is used in the United States. Scientists use the kelvin scale, which starts at absolute zero, the lowest possible temperature, equal to minus 273.15°C."],["Measuring the very big and very small","Scientists use prefixes to describe huge and tiny quantities, from nanometers, billionths of a meter, used for computer chip features, to gigawatts for power stations. In 2022 new prefixes were added, ronna- and quetta- for enormous numbers and ronto- and quecto- for tiny ones, to keep up with growing data and science."]],
sp:["Measuring the Earth to make the meter","In 1792 two French astronomers, Jean-Baptiste Delambre and Pierre Méchain, set out to measure the distance between Dunkirk and Barcelona, which lie on the same line of longitude, to calculate the length of the meter. Working through revolution and war, they climbed church towers and hills to measure angles. The survey took about seven years. Méchain worried for the rest of his life about a small error he had found in his measurements."],
g:[["Unit","A standard quantity used in measurement"],["SI","The International System of Units"],["Precision","How consistent repeated measurements are"],["Absolute zero","The lowest possible temperature"]],
f:["The SI has seven base units.","The kilogram was redefined in 2019.","A unit mix-up caused the loss of a Mars spacecraft."],
fun:"The meter was originally based on the size of the Earth."});

ADDART({id:"machines",t:"Simple Machines",c:"Science",i:"⚙️",w:"Simple machine",r:["newton","energyphys","wheel","bicycle"],
p:[["Making work easier","Simple machines are basic devices that make it easier to lift, move or split things."],["Six classic machines","They are the lever, wheel and axle, pulley, inclined plane, wedge and screw."],["Inside every machine","Complex machines, from bicycles to cranes, are built from simple machines."]],
l:"A simple machine is a basic mechanical device that changes the size or direction of a force, making work easier. The six classical simple machines are the lever, the wheel and axle, the pulley, the inclined plane, the wedge and the screw. People have used them since prehistoric times to build monuments, lift water and make tools. Simple machines do not create energy; they trade force for distance, letting a small force move a large load over a longer distance. Almost every complex machine combines several of them.",
s:[["The lever","A lever is a rigid bar that turns around a fixed point called a fulcrum. Pushing down on the long end of a lever can lift a heavy load near the fulcrum. Seesaws, crowbars, scissors, bottle openers and even your arm work as levers. The Greek scientist Archimedes explained the law of the lever in the 200s BCE."],["Wheel and axle and pulley","A wheel and axle is a large wheel attached to a smaller rod, so turning the wheel turns the axle with more force, as in a doorknob, steering wheel or screwdriver. A pulley is a wheel with a groove for a rope; using several pulleys together lets a person lift a heavy weight with less force, as in cranes and flagpoles."],["Inclined plane, wedge and screw","An inclined plane, or ramp, lets you raise a heavy object by pushing it up a gentle slope rather than lifting it straight up. A wedge is two inclined planes back to back, used to split or cut, like an axe or knife. A screw is an inclined plane wrapped around a rod, turning rotation into strong forward motion."],["Mechanical advantage","The mechanical advantage of a machine tells how much it multiplies force. A lever with the effort three times farther from the fulcrum than the load has a mechanical advantage of three. But there is a trade-off: to lift the load a certain height, you must push three times as far. The total work, force times distance, stays the same, apart from losses to friction."]],
k:[["Number of classic machines","Six"],["Lever pivot","Fulcrum"],["Law of the lever","Archimedes, 3rd century BCE"],["Key idea","Trade force for distance"],["Measure of effect","Mechanical advantage"]],
d:[["c. 2.6 million years ago","Early humans make stone tools that work as wedges"],["c. 3500 BCE","The wheel and axle appear in Mesopotamia"],["c. 2500 BCE","Ramps help build the pyramids of Egypt"],["3rd century BCE","Archimedes explains levers and pulleys"],["1st century CE","Heron of Alexandria describes five simple machines"],["1600","Galileo explains machines in terms of work"]],
s2:[["Archimedes and the lever","Archimedes of Syracuse understood levers so well that he reportedly said, give me a place to stand and I will move the Earth. According to ancient writers, he used a system of pulleys to let a single person pull a fully loaded ship onto land. His work laid the foundation for the science of mechanics."],["The Archimedes screw","The Archimedes screw is a large screw inside a tube. When its lower end is placed in water and turned, water is carried upward along the thread. Used for more than 2,000 years to irrigate fields and drain land, it is still used today in water treatment plants and even to generate electricity from flowing water."],["Building the pyramids","No one knows exactly how the pyramids of Egypt were built, but ramps, levers and sledges were almost certainly used. A wall painting in the tomb of Djehutihotep shows workers pouring water in front of a sledge carrying a giant statue; experiments in 2014 showed that wet sand can greatly reduce the force needed to pull a sledge."],["Compound machines","Most machines combine several simple machines. A bicycle uses wheels and axles, levers in the brakes and pedals, and pulleys in the chain and gears. Scissors are two levers with wedge-shaped blades. A crane combines pulleys, levers and a wheel and axle to lift enormous loads."],["Simple machines in the body","The human body uses simple machines. Bones act as levers, joints act as fulcrums and muscles provide the force. When you raise your forearm, your elbow is the fulcrum. Your front teeth work like wedges to bite into food."],["Rube Goldberg machines","The American cartoonist Rube Goldberg drew absurdly complicated machines that performed simple tasks through long chains of levers, pulleys, ramps and falling objects. Today students build real Rube Goldberg machines in competitions, learning about simple machines, energy and engineering while having fun."]],
sp:["The screw that lifts water","Tradition credits Archimedes with inventing the water screw in the 200s BCE, though similar devices may have been used earlier in Egypt and Mesopotamia; some historians think they watered the Hanging Gardens of Babylon. Turning a hand crank, a single worker could lift a steady stream of water from a river or canal into a field. The same simple principle now moves grain, plastic pellets and even fish in modern factories and farms."],
g:[["Lever","A bar that turns on a fixed point"],["Fulcrum","The pivot point of a lever"],["Mechanical advantage","How much a machine multiplies force"],["Compound machine","A machine made of two or more simple machines"]],
f:["There are six classic simple machines.","Scissors are made of two levers.","A screw is an inclined plane wrapped around a rod."],
fun:"Archimedes said that with a long enough lever and a place to stand, he could move the Earth."});

ADDART({id:"percent",t:"Percentages and Ratios",c:"Mathematics",i:"💯",w:"Percentage",r:["stats","prob","alg","inflation","fib"],
p:[["Parts of a hundred","A percentage is a number expressed as a part of 100, written with the sign %."],["Comparing amounts","Ratios compare two quantities, such as 3 cups of flour to 1 cup of sugar."],["Everywhere in life","Percentages and ratios are used in shopping, cooking, sports, maps, science and money."]],
l:"A percentage is a way of expressing a number as a fraction of 100; the word comes from the Latin per centum, meaning by the hundred. Fifty percent, written 50%, means 50 out of every 100, or one half. A ratio compares two or more quantities, such as the number of boys to girls in a class. Percentages and ratios are among the most useful ideas in everyday mathematics, used to compare prices, calculate discounts and interest, mix ingredients, read maps and understand news and statistics.",
s:[["Calculating percentages","To find a percentage of a number, multiply the number by the percentage and divide by 100. For example, 20% of 50 is 50 × 20 ÷ 100 = 10. To turn a fraction into a percentage, divide and multiply by 100: 3 out of 4 is 0.75, or 75%. A percentage can also be written as a decimal: 12% is 0.12."],["Ratios and proportions","A ratio such as 2:3 means that for every 2 of one thing there are 3 of another. Recipes, paint mixes and medicines rely on ratios. When two ratios are equal, they form a proportion. If 2 kilograms of rice cost 100 pesos, a proportion shows that 5 kilograms cost 250 pesos."],["Percent change","Percent change shows how much something has grown or shrunk compared with its original size. If a price rises from 80 to 100, the increase is 20, which is 25% of 80. Beware: if a price rises by 50% and then falls by 50%, it ends lower than it began, because the second change is calculated from a bigger number."],["Scales and maps","Maps and models use ratios called scales. A map scale of 1:50,000 means 1 centimeter on the map equals 50,000 centimeters, or 500 meters, on the ground. Model cars, building plans and globes are all made to scale."]],
k:[["Meaning","Out of one hundred"],["Symbol","%"],["Word origin","Latin per centum"],["Ratio example","2:3"],["Map scale example","1:50,000"]],
d:[["c. 6 CE","Emperor Augustus taxes goods sold at one hundredth of their value"],["1400s","Italian merchants abbreviate per cento, leading to the % sign"],["1494","Luca Pacioli describes the Rule of 72 for doubling money"],["1683","Jacob Bernoulli studies compound interest and discovers the number e"],["1800s","Percentages become common in business and statistics"],["1900s","Percentages appear in weather forecasts and opinion polls"]],
s2:[["The percent sign","The % sign developed gradually from abbreviations of the Italian per cento used by merchants in the 1400s. Over time, the abbreviation changed into a symbol that looks like a small fraction with zeros, reflecting its meaning of parts per hundred. Related symbols include ‰, per mille, meaning parts per thousand."],["Discounts and taxes","Shops advertise discounts as percentages, such as 30% off. If a shirt costs 500 pesos, 30% off saves 150 pesos, so it costs 350. Governments collect taxes as percentages; the Philippines’ value-added tax, for example, is 12% on many goods and services. Understanding percentages helps people make smart choices."],["Interest and the Rule of 72","Banks pay interest on savings and charge interest on loans as a percentage of the money. With compound interest, interest is earned on earlier interest too, so money grows faster over time. A handy shortcut, the Rule of 72, says that dividing 72 by the yearly interest rate gives roughly the number of years for money to double: at 6%, about 12 years."],["Percentage points","A percentage point is the simple difference between two percentages. If unemployment rises from 4% to 5%, it has risen by 1 percentage point, but by 25% relative to the starting value. News reports sometimes confuse the two, so careful readers check which is meant."],["Ratios in art and nature","Artists and architects have long used ratios to create pleasing proportions. Leonardo da Vinci’s drawing Vitruvian Man shows ideal human proportions. Musical harmony depends on ratios of frequencies: two notes an octave apart have a frequency ratio of 2:1. The golden ratio, about 1.618, appears in some art and natural patterns."],["Percentages in science and sport","Scientists use percentages to describe concentrations, such as 21% oxygen in the air, and probabilities, such as a 70% chance of rain. Sports statistics use percentages for shooting accuracy in basketball and win rates. Percentages make it easy to compare groups of different sizes."]],
sp:["Up 50%, down 50%","Imagine a toy costs 100 pesos. Its price rises by 50%, to 150 pesos. Then it falls by 50%. Is it back to 100? No: 50% of 150 is 75, so the new price is 75 pesos. Because each percentage is calculated from a different starting amount, equal percentage rises and falls do not cancel out. This surprising fact matters for prices, investments and populations."],
g:[["Percentage","A number expressed as parts of 100"],["Ratio","A comparison of two quantities"],["Proportion","An equation stating that two ratios are equal"],["Percentage point","The difference between two percentages"]],
f:["Percent means out of one hundred.","A 1:50,000 map scale means 1 cm stands for 500 m.","A 50% rise followed by a 50% fall leaves you lower than you started."],
fun:"If a price rises 50% and then falls 50%, it ends up lower than it started."});

ADDART({id:"friction",t:"Friction",c:"Science",i:"🛷",w:"Friction",r:["newton","energyphys","machines","thermo","cars"],
p:[["A force that resists sliding","Friction is the force that resists the movement of one surface sliding across another."],["Useful and wasteful","Friction lets us walk, hold objects and stop cars, but it also wastes energy and wears things out."],["Heat from rubbing","Rubbing surfaces together turns motion into heat, which is why rubbing your hands warms them."]],
l:"Friction is a force that acts between surfaces in contact, resisting their motion relative to each other. Without friction, we could not walk, write, tie knots or drive cars; everything would slide like on extremely slippery ice. But friction also turns useful energy into heat, wears down machine parts and makes engines less efficient. Engineers reduce unwanted friction with smooth surfaces, lubricants and bearings, and increase helpful friction with tire treads, brake pads and rough soles.",
s:[["What causes friction","Even surfaces that look smooth are rough at a microscopic scale, covered with tiny bumps. When two surfaces press together, these bumps catch and stick, and atoms on the surfaces attract each other. Pushing one surface across the other requires force to break these contacts, producing friction."],["Static and kinetic friction","Static friction holds still objects in place; it is why a heavy box does not start moving until you push hard enough. Once the box slides, kinetic friction resists its motion and is usually smaller, which is why it is easier to keep something moving than to start it. Rolling friction, for wheels, is much smaller still."],["The laws of friction","Leonardo da Vinci studied friction around 1500 and noticed that it depends on how hard surfaces are pressed together, not on how large the touching area is. In 1699 the French scientist Guillaume Amontons rediscovered these rules, and Charles-Augustin de Coulomb developed them further in 1785. They are still used today as good approximations."],["Fluid friction","Objects moving through air or water also experience friction, called drag or air resistance. Drag increases with speed and with the size of the surface facing the flow. Streamlined shapes, like those of fish, birds, airplanes and racing cars, reduce drag. Parachutes use large surfaces to increase drag and slow a fall."]],
k:[["Kinds","Static, kinetic, rolling, fluid"],["Depends on","Surfaces and how hard they press together"],["Early study","Leonardo da Vinci, c. 1500"],["Laws published","Amontons (1699), Coulomb (1785)"],["Science of friction","Tribology"]],
d:[["Prehistory","People make fire by rubbing sticks together"],["c. 1500","Leonardo da Vinci studies friction"],["1699","Guillaume Amontons publishes the laws of friction"],["1785","Charles-Augustin de Coulomb extends the laws"],["1826","John Walker invents the friction match"],["1966","The word tribology is introduced for the science of friction"]],
s2:[["Making fire","For tens of thousands of years, people have made fire using friction, spinning a wooden stick against another piece of wood until the heat lights tinder. In 1826 the English chemist John Walker invented the friction match, which bursts into flame when struck. Safety matches, which light only on a special strip, followed in the 1840s and 1850s."],["Friction on the road","Car tires depend on friction to grip the road for accelerating, turning and stopping. Treads channel water away so tires keep contact with wet roads; if water builds up under a tire, it can skid, called aquaplaning. Brakes work by pressing pads against discs or drums, turning the car’s motion into heat."],["Reducing friction","Engineers reduce friction with lubricants such as oil and grease, which form a slippery layer between moving parts. Ball bearings replace sliding with rolling. Air hockey tables float pucks on a cushion of air, and magnetic levitation trains float above their tracks, eliminating wheel friction entirely."],["The cost of friction","Friction and wear cost the world enormous amounts of energy and money. A 2017 study estimated that about a fifth of the world’s energy use goes to overcoming friction in vehicles, machines and industry. Better lubricants, coatings and designs could save energy and cut emissions."],["Why ice is slippery","Ice is slippery because a thin, watery layer forms on its surface, letting skates and shoes slide easily. Scientists still debate exactly why this layer forms; pressure, heat from friction and the special nature of water molecules at the surface all seem to play roles. Skaters glide because friction on ice is so low."],["Friction in space","Spacecraft returning to Earth hit the atmosphere at tremendous speed, and the compressed air in front of them becomes extremely hot. Heat shields protect them. Meteors glow for the same reason. In the vacuum of space itself, there is almost no air resistance, so objects keep moving once pushed."]],
sp:["Water on the sand","A painting in the ancient Egyptian tomb of Djehutihotep, from about 1900 BCE, shows a huge statue being dragged on a sledge while a man pours water onto the sand in front of it. For a long time, people thought this was a ritual. In 2014 physicists at the University of Amsterdam showed that the right amount of water stiffens sand so that the sledge does not dig in, roughly halving the force needed to pull it. The Egyptians had discovered a clever way to reduce friction."],
g:[["Friction","A force that resists sliding between surfaces"],["Lubricant","A substance that reduces friction"],["Drag","Friction from air or water on a moving object"],["Tribology","The science of friction, wear and lubrication"]],
f:["Friction turns motion into heat.","It is easier to keep something moving than to start it.","Ancient Egyptians wet sand to reduce friction."],
fun:"Ancient Egyptians poured water on sand to make dragging giant statues easier."});

