ADDART({id:"chilecountry",t:"Chile",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🌶️",w:"Chile",r:["andes","desert","quake","scope","metals","sculpture"],
p:[["A ribbon of land","Chile stretches about 4,300 kilometers along the west coast of South America but is on average only about 180 kilometers wide."],["Desert to glaciers","It runs from the Atacama, one of the driest deserts on Earth, to glaciers and fjords near Antarctica."],["Eyes on the sky","Its clear, dry skies make northern Chile home to some of the world’s greatest observatories."]],
l:"Chile is a long, narrow country in South America, squeezed between the Andes Mountains and the Pacific Ocean. About 18.5 million people live there, most of them in the central valley around the capital, Santiago. Chile includes Easter Island, or Rapa Nui, far out in the Pacific. It is the world’s largest producer of copper and a major source of lithium, fruit, wine and salmon. After a military dictatorship from 1973 to 1990, Chile returned to democracy and became one of Latin America’s most prosperous countries.",
s:[["Land","Chile is about 4,300 kilometers long. In the north lies the Atacama Desert, where some weather stations have recorded almost no rain for years. The fertile central valley has a Mediterranean climate, ideal for vineyards and orchards. The south has rainforests, lakes, volcanoes, fjords and glaciers, ending at Cape Horn. The Andes form the eastern border, including Ojos del Salado, at 6,893 meters the highest volcano on Earth."],["Earthquakes and volcanoes","Chile lies where the Nazca Plate dives beneath South America, making it one of the most earthquake-prone countries. On 22 May 1960, the Valdivia earthquake, magnitude 9.5, was the strongest ever recorded, causing a tsunami that crossed the Pacific to Hawaii, Japan and the Philippines. Chile has about 90 active volcanoes and strict building codes."],["People","Most Chileans are of mixed Indigenous and European descent. The Mapuche, the largest Indigenous people, resisted Inca and Spanish conquest for centuries in the south. Spanish is the main language. Santiago, founded by Pedro de Valdivia in 1541, is home to about 40 percent of the population."],["Economy","Chile is the world’s top copper producer, with giant mines such as Escondida in the Atacama, and one of the largest producers of lithium, used in batteries. It exports grapes, cherries, wine, salmon and wood around the world. Trade agreements with many countries, including in Asia, have made it one of the most open economies in the world."]],
k:[["Capital","Santiago"],["Population","About 18.5 million (2024 census)"],["Area","About 756,000 km²"],["Highest point","Ojos del Salado, 6,893 m"],["Currency","Chilean peso"]],
d:[["c. 1450s","The Inca Empire expands into northern and central Chile"],["1520","Ferdinand Magellan sails through the strait now named after him"],["1541","Pedro de Valdivia founds Santiago"],["1818","Chile declares independence from Spain"],["1960","The Valdivia earthquake, the strongest ever recorded, strikes"],["1973","A military coup brings Augusto Pinochet to power"],["1990","Democracy is restored after a 1988 referendum"]],
s2:[["The Atacama Desert","The Atacama, in northern Chile, is the driest non-polar desert on Earth; some places receive only a few millimeters of rain a year. Its soil is so dry that NASA has tested Mars rovers there. In rare wet years, the desert bursts into bloom with flowers, an event called the desierto florido."],["Observatories","The high, dry Atacama has some of the clearest skies on Earth, so many of the world’s great telescopes are built there, including the Very Large Telescope, the ALMA radio telescope array and the Vera C. Rubin Observatory. The Extremely Large Telescope, with a mirror 39 meters wide, is being built on Cerro Armazones and is planned to begin observing around 2030."],["Rapa Nui","Easter Island, or Rapa Nui, lies about 3,700 kilometers off Chile’s coast in the Pacific. Settled by Polynesians perhaps around 1200, it is famous for its giant stone statues, the moai. Chile annexed the island in 1888. Its Rapa Nui people keep their own language and culture."],["Dictatorship and democracy","On 11 September 1973, the military overthrew the elected socialist president Salvador Allende, and General Augusto Pinochet ruled until 1990. Thousands of people were killed or disappeared and tens of thousands were imprisoned or tortured. In a 1988 referendum, Chileans voted against Pinochet staying in power, and democracy was restored. Chileans continue to debate this history."],["Poets of Chile","Chile is famous for its poets. Gabriela Mistral became the first Latin American writer to win the Nobel Prize in Literature, in 1945. Pablo Neruda, known for his love poems and his Canto General, won it in 1971. Poetry is taught and loved throughout the country."],["The rescue of the 33","On 5 August 2010, a collapse trapped 33 miners about 700 meters underground at the San José copper and gold mine near Copiapó. After 17 days, a drill reached them and found a note saying they were all alive. On 13 October, after 69 days underground, all 33 were lifted to the surface one by one in a narrow capsule, watched by hundreds of millions of people worldwide."]],
sp:["The strongest earthquake","The earthquake that struck near Valdivia in southern Chile on 22 May 1960 had a magnitude of 9.5, the strongest ever measured by instruments. It lasted about ten minutes, changed the shape of the coastline and triggered landslides and a volcanic eruption. Its tsunami crossed the Pacific Ocean, killing people in Hawaii, Japan and the Philippines many hours later. The disaster led to the creation of better Pacific tsunami warning systems."],
g:[["Atacama","A very dry desert in northern Chile"],["Mapuche","The largest Indigenous people of Chile"],["Fjord","A long, narrow sea inlet between steep cliffs"],["Referendum","A vote by all citizens on a single question"],["Copper","A reddish metal used in electric wires"]],
f:["Chile is about 4,300 km long.","The strongest earthquake ever recorded struck Chile in 1960.","Easter Island belongs to Chile."],
fun:"NASA has tested Mars rovers in Chile’s Atacama Desert because it is so dry."});

ADDART({id:"colombiacountry",t:"Colombia",c:"Countries and Places",i:"☕",w:"Colombia",r:["coffee","andes","amazon","latinind","birds","novel"],
p:[["Two oceans","Colombia is the only country in South America with coasts on both the Caribbean Sea and the Pacific Ocean."],["Wonderfully diverse","It has more kinds of birds than any other country and amazing variety in plants and animals."],["Coffee and culture","Colombia is famous for coffee, emeralds, music, dance and the novels of Gabriel García Márquez."]],
l:"Colombia is a country in northwestern South America, with about 53 million people. Its capital, Bogotá, sits high in the Andes at about 2,600 meters. Colombia’s landscapes include Caribbean beaches, snowy peaks, coffee-growing hills, Pacific rainforests and the Amazon. It is one of the world’s most biologically diverse countries. After decades of armed conflict, the government signed a peace agreement with the FARC rebel group in 2016, and Colombia is now known for its growing cities, tourism and lively culture.",
s:[["Land","The Andes split into three ranges in Colombia, with the Magdalena and Cauca river valleys between them. The Caribbean coast in the north is warm and includes the Sierra Nevada de Santa Marta, the world’s highest coastal mountain range, with peaks of about 5,700 meters. The east is covered by vast grasslands, the llanos, and the Amazon rainforest."],["Biodiversity","Colombia has more bird species than any other country, around 1,900, and more kinds of orchids, as well as thousands of butterflies, frogs and fish. Its national parks protect cloud forests, mangroves and coral reefs. The Caño Cristales river turns red, yellow, green and blue in some seasons because of colorful plants growing on its bed."],["People and history","Before the Spanish arrived, peoples such as the Muisca and the Tayrona built societies with skilled goldwork. The Spanish founded Santa Marta in 1525 and Bogotá in 1538. Colombia declared independence in 1810, and Simón Bolívar’s victory at Boyacá in 1819 secured it. Panama was part of Colombia until 1903."],["Economy","Colombia exports oil, coal, coffee, flowers, bananas and emeralds; it produces most of the world’s finest emeralds. Its high-quality arabica coffee, grown on mountain slopes, is famous worldwide. Medellín, Bogotá and Cali have growing technology, fashion and tourism industries."]],
k:[["Capital","Bogotá"],["Population","About 53 million (2024)"],["Area","About 1.14 million km²"],["Highest peaks","About 5,700 m, Sierra Nevada de Santa Marta"],["Currency","Colombian peso"]],
d:[["c. 800 CE","The Tayrona build Ciudad Perdida, the Lost City"],["1538","The Spanish found Bogotá"],["1810","Colombia declares independence"],["1819","Bolívar wins the Battle of Boyacá"],["1903","Panama separates from Colombia"],["1982","Gabriel García Márquez wins the Nobel Prize in Literature"],["2016","A peace agreement is signed with the FARC"]],
s2:[["The legend of El Dorado","The Muisca people of the Andes held a ceremony in which a new ruler, covered in gold dust, sailed onto Lake Guatavita and offered gold and emeralds to the gods. Spanish conquerors heard of this and imagined a city of gold, El Dorado, which they searched for in vain. Beautiful Muisca gold objects can be seen in Bogotá’s Gold Museum."],["García Márquez","The writer Gabriel García Márquez, born in Aracataca in 1927, wrote One Hundred Years of Solitude, published in 1967, about generations of a family in the imaginary town of Macondo. His style, mixing everyday life with the magical, became known as magical realism. He won the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1982."],["Conflict and peace","From the 1960s, Colombia suffered a long armed conflict between the government, left-wing guerrilla groups such as the FARC, right-wing paramilitaries and drug traffickers. Hundreds of thousands of people died and millions were displaced. In 2016 the government and the FARC signed a peace agreement, and President Juan Manuel Santos won the Nobel Peace Prize. Some violence by other armed groups continues."],["Medellín’s transformation","In the 1980s and 1990s, Medellín was one of the most violent cities in the world. It then invested in public transport, schools, libraries and parks, including cable cars, opened in 2004, linking hillside neighborhoods to the city center. Its turnaround has been studied by city planners around the world."],["Music and dance","Colombia is famous for cumbia, which blends Indigenous, African and Spanish music, and vallenato, played with accordion, drum and scraper, recognized by UNESCO. Salsa thrives in Cali. Colombian singers such as Shakira, Juanes, Carlos Vives and Karol G are known worldwide."],["Coffee country","The Coffee Cultural Landscape of Colombia, in the hills of the central Andes, is a UNESCO World Heritage Site. Coffee is grown on small family farms on steep slopes and picked by hand. The fictional coffee farmer Juan Valdez, with his mule, became a well-known symbol of Colombian coffee from 1958."]],
sp:["The Lost City","High in the Sierra Nevada de Santa Marta lies Ciudad Perdida, the Lost City, built by the Tayrona people around 800 CE, centuries before Machu Picchu. Its stone terraces, stairways and plazas were abandoned after the Spanish conquest and hidden by forest until 1972. Visitors reach it by a multi-day hike through the jungle. The Indigenous peoples of the Sierra, including the Kogi and Arhuaco, still consider it sacred."],
g:[["Llanos","Tropical grasslands of Colombia and Venezuela"],["Magical realism","A style of fiction mixing reality with magical events"],["Emerald","A green gemstone"],["Muisca","An Indigenous people of the Colombian Andes"],["Cumbia","A Colombian music and dance style"]],
f:["Colombia has more bird species than any other country.","Colombia has coasts on two oceans.","Most of the world’s finest emeralds come from Colombia."],
fun:"The Caño Cristales river in Colombia is called the river of five colors."});

ADDART({id:"netherlandscountry",t:"The Netherlands",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🌷",w:"Netherlands",r:["vangogh","bicycle","canals","eu","trade","indonesiacountry"],
p:[["Low land","The Netherlands means low lands; about a quarter of the country lies below sea level."],["Holding back the sea","For centuries the Dutch have built dikes, dams and pumps to protect their land from water."],["Bikes and tulips","It is famous for bicycles, windmills, tulips, canals and great painters."]],
l:"The Netherlands is a small, densely populated country in northwestern Europe, with about 18 million people. Amsterdam is its capital, while the government sits in The Hague. Much of the country is flat and low, and about a quarter lies below sea level, protected by dikes and dunes. In the 1600s, the Dutch Golden Age made the Netherlands a world power in trade, science and art. Today it is a wealthy trading nation, home to Europe’s largest port, Rotterdam, and a founding member of the European Union.",
s:[["Land and water","The Netherlands sits on the deltas of the Rhine, Meuse and Scheldt rivers. Over centuries, the Dutch drained lakes and marshes to create new land called polders, using windmills and later electric pumps. The highest point in the European part of the country, the Vaalserberg, is only 322 meters high."],["The Dutch Golden Age","In the 1600s, after winning independence from Spain in the Eighty Years’ War, the Dutch Republic became Europe’s leading trading power. The Dutch East India Company, founded in 1602, traded across Asia. Painters such as Rembrandt and Johannes Vermeer, and scientists such as Christiaan Huygens and Antonie van Leeuwenhoek, worked there."],["Colonial empire","The Dutch built a colonial empire, including the Dutch East Indies, now Indonesia, Suriname and Caribbean islands. Colonial rule brought great wealth to the Netherlands but also forced labor, wars and suffering for colonized peoples, a history the Netherlands has increasingly acknowledged. Aruba, Curaçao and Sint Maarten remain countries within the Kingdom of the Netherlands."],["Modern Netherlands","The Netherlands is a constitutional monarchy and parliamentary democracy. It is known for international institutions in The Hague, including the International Court of Justice. Its farmers are among the world’s most productive, and it is the world’s second-largest exporter of agricultural products by value, including flowers, vegetables and dairy."]],
k:[["Capital","Amsterdam (government in The Hague)"],["Population","About 18 million (2024)"],["Area","About 41,500 km²"],["Below sea level","About a quarter of the land"],["Currency","Euro"]],
d:[["1568","The Eighty Years’ War against Spain begins"],["1602","The Dutch East India Company is founded"],["1637","Tulip mania collapses"],["1648","Spain recognizes Dutch independence"],["1940–1945","Germany occupies the Netherlands in World War II"],["1953","The North Sea flood kills more than 1,800 people"],["1997","The Delta Works are completed"]],
s2:[["The Delta Works","On the night of 31 January 1953, a storm surge broke through dikes in the southwest, flooding large areas and killing more than 1,800 people. The government responded with the Delta Works, a huge system of dams, sluices and storm barriers completed in 1997. The Maeslantkering, a barrier with two giant swinging arms, protects Rotterdam."],["Tulip mania","Tulips came to the Netherlands from the Ottoman Empire in the late 1500s. In the 1630s, prices for rare tulip bulbs rose to extraordinary levels before suddenly collapsing in 1637, an episode often called the first speculative bubble. Today the Netherlands is the world’s biggest producer of tulip bulbs, and the Keukenhof gardens attract visitors each spring."],["A nation of cyclists","The Netherlands has more bicycles than people, and cycling is part of daily life for children and adults alike. Separate bike paths, traffic lights for cyclists and huge bicycle parking garages, such as the one at Utrecht station with space for over 12,000 bikes, make cycling safe and easy."],["The Dutch in Asia","Dutch traders reached Asia in the 1590s and built a trading empire centered on Batavia, now Jakarta. They were the only Europeans allowed to trade with Japan for over two centuries, from the island of Dejima in Nagasaki. In the Philippines, the Dutch fought Spain in a series of naval battles in 1646, known as the Battles of La Naval de Manila."],["Anne Frank","During World War II, Germany occupied the Netherlands, and most of the country’s Jews were murdered in the Holocaust. Anne Frank, a Jewish girl, hid with her family in a secret annex in Amsterdam from 1942 to 1944, where she wrote her famous diary. She died in a concentration camp in 1945. Her hiding place is now a museum."],["Windmills","Windmills were once essential in the Netherlands for pumping water, grinding grain and sawing wood. About a thousand historic windmills survive. The 19 windmills at Kinderdijk, built in the 1700s to drain the polders, are a UNESCO World Heritage Site."]],
sp:["Making new land","The Dutch have created large areas of land from the sea. After a great dam, the Afsluitdijk, closed off an inlet of the sea in 1932, creating a freshwater lake, engineers pumped dry huge sections to make new polders. Flevoland, the newest province, created in 1986, was almost entirely reclaimed from the water. A saying goes that God created the world, but the Dutch created the Netherlands."],
g:[["Dike","A wall or bank built to hold back water"],["Polder","Low land reclaimed from water and protected by dikes"],["Delta","Land formed where a river splits and meets the sea"],["Golden Age","A period of great wealth and achievement"],["Sluice","A gate that controls the flow of water"]],
f:["About a quarter of the Netherlands is below sea level.","The Netherlands has more bicycles than people.","Flevoland was reclaimed from the sea."],
fun:"There are more bicycles than people in the Netherlands."});

ADDART({id:"tanzaniacountry",t:"Tanzania",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🦒",w:"Tanzania",r:["kilimanjaro","grasslands","lakes","kenyacountry","decol","evo"],
p:[["Roof of Africa","Tanzania is home to Mount Kilimanjaro, Africa’s highest mountain."],["Great wildlife","The Serengeti and Ngorongoro Crater are among the greatest wildlife areas on Earth."],["Swahili nation","Kiswahili, spoken across East Africa, unites Tanzania’s more than 120 ethnic groups."]],
l:"Tanzania is a country in East Africa on the Indian Ocean, formed in 1964 by the union of Tanganyika and the islands of Zanzibar. About 68 million people live there. Its official capital is Dodoma, while Dar es Salaam is the largest city and main port. Tanzania is famous for Mount Kilimanjaro, the Serengeti, the spice islands of Zanzibar and fossil sites that tell the story of human origins. Kiswahili is the national language, and Tanzania has been one of Africa’s most stable and peaceful countries.",
s:[["Land","Tanzania has a long tropical coast, a high central plateau and mountains. Kilimanjaro, at 5,895 meters, rises in the northeast. Tanzania borders three great lakes: Victoria, Africa’s largest; Tanganyika, the world’s second-deepest; and Malawi, also called Nyasa. The Great Rift Valley crosses the country."],["Wildlife","About a third of Tanzania is protected in national parks and reserves. The Serengeti hosts the great migration of wildebeest and zebras. The Ngorongoro Crater, a vast collapsed volcano, is filled with lions, elephants, rhinos and flamingos. Selous, now called Nyerere National Park, is one of Africa’s largest protected areas."],["History","Swahili trading towns such as Kilwa Kisiwani flourished on the coast from about the 900s. Germany colonized the mainland in the 1880s as part of German East Africa; the Maji Maji rebellion of 1905 to 1907 against German rule was brutally crushed. After World War I, Britain ruled Tanganyika. It became independent in 1961, and Zanzibar in 1963."],["People and language","Tanzania has more than 120 ethnic groups, including the Sukuma, Chaga, Haya and Maasai. Its first president, Julius Nyerere, promoted Kiswahili as a national language, helping unite the country. Most people are Christian or Muslim, and Zanzibar is mainly Muslim. In 2021 Samia Suluhu Hassan became Tanzania’s first woman president."]],
k:[["Capital","Dodoma (largest city: Dar es Salaam)"],["Population","About 68 million (2024)"],["Area","About 945,000 km²"],["Highest point","Kilimanjaro, 5,895 m"],["Currency","Tanzanian shilling"]],
d:[["c. 3.6 million years ago","Early human ancestors leave footprints at Laetoli"],["c. 900s","Kilwa and other Swahili trading towns grow"],["1885","Germany begins colonizing the mainland"],["1905–1907","The Maji Maji rebellion against German rule"],["1961","Tanganyika becomes independent"],["1964","Tanganyika and Zanzibar unite as Tanzania"],["2021","Samia Suluhu Hassan becomes the first woman president"]],
s2:[["Olduvai and Laetoli","Olduvai Gorge, in northern Tanzania, is one of the world’s most important places for understanding human origins. In 1959 and 1960, Mary and Louis Leakey and their team found fossils of early human relatives there, including Homo habilis. At nearby Laetoli in 1978, Mary Leakey’s team discovered footprints of upright-walking ancestors preserved in volcanic ash about 3.6 million years ago."],["Zanzibar","Zanzibar is an archipelago off the coast, famous as the spice islands for its cloves, nutmeg and cinnamon. Its Stone Town, a UNESCO World Heritage Site, mixes African, Arab, Indian and European architecture, with carved wooden doors. In the 1800s Zanzibar was a center of the East African slave trade, which ended in 1873. The singer Freddie Mercury was born there in 1946."],["Julius Nyerere","Julius Nyerere, a teacher called Mwalimu, led Tanganyika to independence in 1961 and was president of Tanzania until 1985. He promoted Ujamaa, a form of African socialism based on village communities, which improved literacy and health but struggled economically. He also supported independence movements across southern Africa. He stepped down voluntarily, which was rare in Africa at the time."],["Kiswahili","Kiswahili, or Swahili, is a Bantu language with many words borrowed from Arabic and other languages. It is spoken by tens of millions of people across East and Central Africa. Words such as safari, meaning journey, and hakuna matata, no worries, are known worldwide. UNESCO named 7 July World Kiswahili Language Day."],["Tanzanite","Tanzanite is a striking blue-violet gemstone found in only one place on Earth: a small area near Mount Kilimanjaro, where it was discovered in 1967. It became popular as a gem in the 1970s. Because there is only one source, it is considered rarer than diamonds."],["Kilwa Kisiwani","On a small island off the southern coast, the ruins of Kilwa Kisiwani tell of a rich medieval trading city. From the 1200s to the 1400s, it controlled the gold trade from the interior, minted its own coins and built the Great Mosque and the palace of Husuni Kubwa. The traveler Ibn Battuta visited in 1331 and called it one of the most beautiful cities in the world."]],
sp:["Ngorongoro Crater","About 2 to 3 million years ago, a giant volcano in northern Tanzania collapsed, leaving the Ngorongoro Crater, the world’s largest unbroken, unflooded volcanic caldera, about 20 kilometers across and 600 meters deep. Its floor of grassland, swamp and forest supports about 25,000 large animals, including lions, elephants, buffaloes and some of the last black rhinos in East Africa. Maasai herders also live in the wider Ngorongoro Conservation Area."],
g:[["Kiswahili","A Bantu language spoken across East Africa"],["Ujamaa","Nyerere’s policy of village-based African socialism"],["Caldera","A large crater formed when a volcano collapses"],["Archipelago","A group of islands"],["Union","The joining of two states into one"]],
f:["Tanzania was formed by uniting Tanganyika and Zanzibar.","Tanzanite is found only in Tanzania.","Lake Tanganyika is the second-deepest lake in the world."],
fun:"Freddie Mercury, singer of the band Queen, was born in Zanzibar."});

ADDART({id:"samoacountry",t:"Samoa",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🌺",w:"Samoa",r:["fijicountry","nzcountry","pacific","australiacountry","decol","hawaii"],
p:[["Heart of Polynesia","Samoa is an island nation in the South Pacific, with two main islands, Upolu and Savai’i."],["The Samoan way","Samoans value fa’a Samoa, the Samoan way of life built on family, chiefs, church and respect."],["First to independence","In 1962 Samoa became the first Pacific island country to gain independence in the 20th century."]],
l:"Samoa is a Polynesian island country in the South Pacific, about halfway between Hawaii and New Zealand. About 220,000 people live there, mostly on the two large volcanic islands of Upolu, home to the capital, Apia, and Savai’i. Samoans have lived on the islands for about 3,000 years and keep a strong traditional culture centered on extended families and chiefs called matai. Samoa was ruled by Germany and then New Zealand before becoming independent in 1962. It is known for its beaches, rugby and the art of tatau, tattooing.",
s:[["Land","Samoa’s islands are volcanic, with rugged mountains, rainforests, waterfalls and coral reefs. Savai’i is one of the largest islands in Polynesia outside New Zealand and Hawaii, with lava fields from eruptions in the early 1900s. Mount Silisili, at 1,858 meters, is the highest point. The climate is tropical, with a rainy season and occasional cyclones."],["Fa’a Samoa","Fa’a Samoa, the Samoan way, shapes daily life. Each extended family, or aiga, is led by a matai, a chief chosen by the family, who represents it in the village council. Respect for elders, sharing and church are central. Many Samoans still live in open-sided houses called fale and gather for Sunday meals cooked in an earth oven, the umu."],["History","Polynesian voyagers settled Samoa around 3,000 years ago; pottery of the Lapita culture has been found there. In the 1800s, Germany, Britain and the United States competed for influence. In 1900 the islands were divided: the western islands became German Samoa, and the eastern ones became American Samoa, still a US territory. New Zealand took over Western Samoa in 1914."],["Independence","Many Samoans opposed New Zealand rule through a peaceful movement called the Mau. On 28 December 1929, known as Black Saturday, New Zealand police fired on a Mau procession in Apia, killing the high chief Tupua Tamasese Lealofi III and others. Western Samoa became independent on 1 January 1962 and changed its name to Samoa in 1997. New Zealand formally apologized in 2002 for past wrongs."]],
k:[["Capital","Apia"],["Population","About 220,000 (2024)"],["Area","About 2,840 km²"],["Highest point","Mount Silisili, 1,858 m"],["Currency","Tālā"]],
d:[["c. 800 BCE","Lapita voyagers settle Samoa"],["1889","A cyclone wrecks warships at Apia"],["1900","The islands are divided between Germany and the United States"],["1914","New Zealand takes over Western Samoa"],["1929","Black Saturday: police fire on the Mau movement"],["1962","Samoa becomes independent"],["2011","Samoa moves west of the International Date Line, skipping 30 December"]],
s2:[["The Apia cyclone","In March 1889, warships of the United States, Germany and Britain faced off in Apia harbor amid rivalry over Samoa. A powerful cyclone struck, wrecking six of the warships and killing about 200 sailors; only the British ship escaped to open sea. The disaster eased tensions and led to talks over the islands’ future."],["Tatau","The English word tattoo comes from Polynesian words such as the Samoan tatau. The traditional male tattoo, the pe’a, covers the body from waist to knees and is applied with tools made of bone and turtle shell in a painful process lasting days or weeks. Women may receive the malu on their thighs. These tattoos are signs of identity, courage and service."],["Skipping a day","Samoa once lay just east of the International Date Line, sharing a calendar day with the United States. But its trade grew closer to Australia and New Zealand, which were nearly a day ahead. So at midnight on 29 December 2011, Samoa jumped straight to 31 December, skipping 30 December entirely. Its time is now close to that of New Zealand."],["Rugby","Rugby union is the most popular sport in Samoa. The national team, Manu Samoa, is famous for its powerful tackling and its war dance, the siva tau, performed before matches. Many players of Samoan heritage play for teams in New Zealand, Australia and Europe."],["Robert Louis Stevenson","The Scottish author of Treasure Island, Robert Louis Stevenson, moved to Samoa for his health and lived there from 1890 until his death in 1894. Samoans called him Tusitala, teller of tales. He supported Samoan rights against foreign powers. He is buried on Mount Vaea above Apia, and his house is a museum."],["Samoans abroad","More Samoans live overseas than in Samoa itself, mainly in New Zealand, Australia and the United States. Money sent home by family members abroad is an important part of the economy. Samoan communities have made strong contributions to sport, music and politics in their new countries."]],
sp:["First woman prime minister","In 2021 Fiamē Naomi Mata’afa became Samoa’s first woman prime minister after a close election and a constitutional crisis, during which the outgoing government briefly locked Parliament’s doors. Her party’s members were sworn in at a ceremony in a tent outside Parliament, and the courts confirmed her government. The episode was seen as a test of the rule of law in the Pacific."],
g:[["Matai","A Samoan chief who leads an extended family"],["Fale","A traditional open-sided Samoan house"],["Aiga","A Samoan extended family"],["Tatau","Traditional Samoan tattooing"],["Mau","A Samoan movement for independence"]],
f:["The word tattoo comes from Polynesian words like tatau.","Samoa skipped 30 December 2011.","Robert Louis Stevenson is buried in Samoa."],
fun:"Samoa skipped a whole day, 30 December 2011, when it moved across the International Date Line."});

ADDART({id:"tongacountry",t:"Tonga",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🌋",w:"Tonga",r:["samoacountry","fijicountry","volc","tsunami","pacific","olymp"],
p:[["A Pacific kingdom","Tonga is the only kingdom in the Pacific, ruled by a royal family for over a thousand years."],["Never fully colonized","It was never fully colonized, though it was a British protected state from 1900 to 1970."],["Friendly Islands","Captain James Cook called Tonga the Friendly Islands because of the welcome he received."]],
l:"Tonga is a Polynesian kingdom in the South Pacific, made up of about 170 islands, of which only about 36 are inhabited. About 100,000 people live there, most on the main island of Tongatapu, home to the capital, Nuku’alofa. Tonga has a long royal history: its ancient Tu’i Tonga dynasty once held influence across much of the western Pacific. Tonga was never formally colonized and became fully sovereign in 1970. In 2022 the eruption of the undersea Hunga Tonga–Hunga Ha’apai volcano shook the world.",
s:[["Land","Tonga’s islands form a chain about 800 kilometers long, divided into groups such as Tongatapu, Ha’apai and Vava’u. Some are flat coral islands, while others are volcanic. The islands lie beside the Tonga Trench, one of the deepest trenches on Earth, where the Pacific Plate sinks beneath the Australian Plate, causing earthquakes and volcanoes."],["The Tu’i Tonga","From about the 900s, the sacred kings called Tu’i Tonga built a maritime chiefdom whose influence reached Samoa, Fiji, Wallis and Futuna and other islands. Large stone tombs called langi and the trilithon Ha’amonga ’a Maui, a gateway of three huge coral limestone blocks built around 1200, show their power."],["A modern kingdom","In 1875 King George Tupou I gave Tonga a written constitution, freed commoners from forced labor and gave them rights to land. Tonga became a British protected state in 1900 but kept its monarchy and internal government, becoming fully independent in 1970. Reforms in 2010 gave Tongans a more democratic parliament, with most members elected."],["Life and culture","Tongan life centers on family, church and respect for nobles and the royal family. People wear a ta’ovala, a woven mat wrapped around the waist, on formal occasions. Feasts with roast pig, root crops and seafood mark special events. Tongan bark cloth, ngatu, is decorated with bold patterns and given at weddings and funerals."]],
k:[["Capital","Nuku’alofa"],["Population","About 100,000 (2021 census)"],["Area","About 750 km²"],["Islands","About 170, about 36 inhabited"],["Currency","Pa’anga"]],
d:[["c. 800 BCE","Lapita voyagers settle Tonga"],["c. 950","The Tu’i Tonga dynasty begins"],["c. 1200","The trilithon Ha’amonga ’a Maui is built"],["1773","Captain James Cook visits Tonga"],["1875","King George Tupou I proclaims a constitution"],["1970","Tonga becomes fully independent"],["2022","The Hunga Tonga–Hunga Ha’apai volcano erupts"]],
s2:[["The 2022 eruption","On 15 January 2022, the undersea volcano Hunga Tonga–Hunga Ha’apai exploded in one of the largest volcanic eruptions in over a century. Its plume rose about 57 kilometers, higher than any eruption plume ever measured, and a pressure wave circled the globe several times. Tsunamis struck Tonga and reached coasts across the Pacific, and ash covered the islands. The blast cut Tonga’s undersea internet cable, isolating it for weeks."],["Whales of Vava’u","Every year from about July to October, humpback whales travel from Antarctica to the warm waters of Tonga to give birth and nurse their calves. Vava’u is one of the few places in the world where visitors can swim with humpback whales, under strict rules, making whale watching important for the economy."],["The Friendly Islands","Captain James Cook visited Tonga in 1773 and 1777 and named the islands the Friendly Islands. Later accounts suggest that some chiefs had planned to attack his crew but disagreed about it, so the friendliness may have been more complicated than Cook knew."],["Tongan sport","Rugby is hugely popular, and Tonga’s national team, the ’Ikale Tahi, performs the war dance Sipi Tau before matches. In 2016 the athlete Pita Taufatofua became world-famous when he carried Tonga’s flag at the Olympic opening ceremony in Rio de Janeiro, shirtless and gleaming with oil, and he later competed in both summer and winter Games."],["Tongans abroad","Many Tongans live in New Zealand, Australia and the United States, and money sent home by them is a major part of Tonga’s economy. Tongan churches, choirs and festivals are part of life in cities such as Auckland and Sydney."],["Climate threats","As a low-lying island nation in a cyclone zone, Tonga faces serious risks from rising seas, stronger storms and coastal erosion. Severe Cyclone Gita struck in 2018, damaging homes and the old parliament building. Tonga, like other Pacific nations, calls for strong global action on climate change."]],
sp:["Ha’amonga ’a Maui","On the northeastern tip of Tongatapu stands Ha’amonga ’a Maui, the Burden of Maui, built around 1200 by the eleventh Tu’i Tonga, Tu’itātui. Two upright stones, each about 5 meters high, support a third laid across the top, each weighing many metric tons. Its purpose is debated: it may have been a royal gateway or a monument. In 1967 King Tāufa’āhau Tupou IV suggested that notches on its top might mark the sunrise on the longest and shortest days of the year."],
g:[["Kingdom","A country ruled by a king or queen"],["Ngatu","Tongan decorated bark cloth"],["Ta’ovala","A woven mat worn around the waist"],["Trilithon","A structure of two upright stones and one across the top"],["Protected state","A country that keeps its own government but is protected by a stronger power"]],
f:["Tonga is the only kingdom in the Pacific.","The 2022 Tongan eruption sent a plume about 57 km high.","Humpback whales visit Tonga each year."],
fun:"Captain Cook called Tonga the Friendly Islands."});

;
